const RAW: Record<string, string> = {"/skill-src/aura-3d-web/README.md":"# AURA · aura-3d-web — the immersive 3D website skill\n\nA skill for AI coding agents (Claude Code, Claude.ai Skills, Cursor, Codex, Windsurf, Gemini CLI, Copilot agent, Aider…) that turns \"build me a 3D website\" into **a scroll-driven cinematic experience**: one continuous camera through one authored world, designed light, depth you can feel, giant interactive type — at 60fps.\n\nIt is not a template. It is a **creative director + a camera engine** you hand to the agent.\n\n## What's inside\n```\naura-3d-web/\n├─ SKILL.md                         ← the operating system (10 laws, 8-phase pipeline, ban list, scoring)\n├─ references/\n│  ├─ 01-aura-doctrine.md           ← what separates AAA from \"3D website\"\n│  ├─ 02-concept-and-storyboard.md  ← world metaphors, brief + storyboard templates, 8 archetype playbooks\n│  ├─ 03-choreography.md            ← playhead, shot lists, camera vocabulary, DOM windows, text sandwich\n│  ├─ 04-visual-engine.md           ← lighting rigs, materials, atmosphere, post-FX, shader cookbook\n│  ├─ 05-asset-pipeline.md          ← procedural / generated-image 2.5D / GLB / video / AI-3D, with prompts\n│  ├─ 06-typography-and-ui.md       ← type system, nav, cursor, loader, copywriting, a11y\n│  ├─ 07-performance-and-resilience.md ← budgets, governor, mobile, fallbacks, SEO\n│  ├─ 08-qa-and-polish.md           ← Playwright screenshot loop, the 100-pt Aura Score, polish passes\n│  ├─ 09-prompt-pack.md             ← master prompt + archetype kickoffs + refinement prompts\n│  └─ 10-frontier-techniques.md     ← raymarched heroes, GPU particles, 2.5D plates, cinematic type, WebGPU, splats, audio…\n├─ starter/scripts/audit.mjs        ← the QA gate: deterministic seek, per-frame metrics, chapter/shot lint, fallback tests\n└─ starter/                         ← the working engine (Vite + React + three), type-checked\n   └─ src/aura/ engine · choreo · materials · particles · layers · floor · atmosphere · terrain · assets · AuraExperience · aura.css · scene.example (Obsidian) · scene.umbra (Umbra)\n```\n\n## Install\n**Claude Code** — copy this folder to `~/.claude/skills/aura-3d-web/` (all projects) or `<project>/.claude/skills/aura-3d-web/`.\n**Claude.ai** — zip the folder (so `SKILL.md` is inside `aura-3d-web/`) → Settings → Capabilities → Skills → Upload.\n**Cursor** — copy to `.cursor/rules/aura-3d-web/` and add a rule file: `Use aura-3d-web/SKILL.md whenever the user asks for a 3D/immersive/cinematic website.`\n**Codex / Gemini CLI / Copilot / Windsurf / Aider** — copy the folder into the repo and add to `AGENTS.md` (or the tool's instructions file): *\"For any 3D / immersive / cinematic / scroll-driven website, read `aura-3d-web/SKILL.md` first and follow it.\"*\n**No skills support?** Use the single-file *Master prompt* from `references/09-prompt-pack.md` (or the one-file export from the Aurelius site).\n\n## Use\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Build a scroll-driven immersive 3D website for [SUBJECT].\nAudience: [WHO]. They should feel [EMOTION]. Final action: [CTA].\n```\nThen let it work: brief → storyboard → assets → build → screenshots → score → polish.\n\n## Run the starter\n```bash\ncd starter && npm i && npm run dev\n```\nYou'll see **Obsidian Gate** (a product world); open `/?world=umbra` for **Umbra** (an event world: dunes, eclipse sky, light shafts). Both are zero-asset. Read them as patterns, then replace the scene.\n\n## Verify\n```bash\nnpm i -D playwright pngjs && npx playwright install chromium\n(cd starter && node scripts/audit.mjs http://localhost:5173)      # deterministic frames + metrics + contact sheets\n```\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/SKILL.md":"---\nname: aura-3d-web\ndescription: Build AAA-grade, scroll-driven, immersive 3D websites that feel like high-budget game trailers or Awwwards Site-of-the-Year winners — for ANY subject (product launch, portfolio, real estate, brand, restaurant, game, film, SaaS, event, personal site). Use this skill whenever the user asks for a 3D website, an immersive / cinematic / scrollytelling / WebGL / three.js experience, a \"wow\" or \"AAA\" landing page, scroll-choreographed animation, a camera that flies through a world, or anything that should make a visitor feel awe. Covers concept, storyboard, asset generation, the cinematic camera engine, lighting, materials, atmosphere, typography, post-processing, performance and a scored QA gate. Works in two modes — WORLD (environment / product / event) and FOLIO (portfolio / studio gallery in depth) — plus a hybrid. Includes 12 art directions (product altar, showroom, estate, instrument, night walk, liquid particles, folio, tunnel, terrain, macro appetite, interface cosmos, light-mode glass), seven worked examples and a $10,000 delivery standard. Do NOT build \"just another website\" when this skill is active.\n---\n\n# AURA — the immersive 3D web skill\n\nYou are not a web developer on this task. You are a **creative director, cinematographer and technical director in one body.** A normal website is a document. What you are building is a **film the visitor drives with their scroll wheel.**\n\nIf the visitor does not feel something in the first three seconds and again at the end, you failed — no matter how clean the code is.\n\n> **AURA** is the bar. Every decision is checked against four pillars:\n> **A**tmosphere — light, air, matter and depth you could almost touch.\n> **U**nbroken — one continuous camera through one continuous world. No cuts, no seams, no \"sections\".\n> **R**hythm — deliberate pacing: crescendo, hold, release. Silence is a design element.\n> **A**uthorship — one specific idea with a point of view. Nothing that could be swapped onto any other brand.\n\n---\n\n## 0 · How to use this skill (read this first)\n\n1. Read this file fully. It is the operating system.\n2. Load reference files **just in time** (they are long and specific):\n\n| When you are… | Read |\n| --- | --- |\n| deciding what to build / reviewing taste | `references/01-aura-doctrine.md` |\n| starting any project (concept, storyboard) | `references/02-concept-and-storyboard.md` |\n| writing the camera rail, scroll mapping, text timing | `references/03-choreography.md` |\n| building lighting, materials, atmosphere, post-FX, shaders | `references/04-visual-engine.md` |\n| you have no 3D models (you usually don't) | `references/05-asset-pipeline.md` |\n| doing typography, nav, UI, copy | `references/06-typography-and-ui.md` |\n| optimizing, handling mobile, a11y, fallbacks | `references/07-performance-and-resilience.md` |\n| verifying, scoring, polishing | `references/08-qa-and-polish.md` |\n| you want ready-made kickoff prompts | `references/09-prompt-pack.md` |\n| **choosing the art direction (ALWAYS, in Phase 1)** | `references/11-art-directions.md` |\n| the site revolves around a product / object render | `references/12-product-hero-playbook.md` |\n| a portfolio, studio, agency or brand | `references/13-portfolio-and-brand-playbook.md` |\n| scoping the full deliverable to a $10k standard (loader, routes, SEO, audio, mobile edit, conversion) | `references/14-premium-delivery.md` |\n| writing shaders / particles / dissolves / liquid chrome / caustics | `references/15-shader-recipes.md` |\n| you need to SEE what the user wants — read the two closest | `examples/README.md` + `examples/0N-*.md` |\n| **choosing between WORLD and FOLIO mode (ALWAYS, in Phase 1a — before the direction)**, or building a portfolio / studio / case-study site | `references/16-modes-world-folio-hybrid.md` + `?world=folio` in the starter |\n| you want to go BEYOND the starter (raymarched heroes, GPU particles, depth-displaced plates, cinematic type, WebGPU, splats, audio…) and need the world-building recipes | `references/10-frontier-techniques.md` |\n\n3. The working engine is in `starter/` — and it ships **four finished reference worlds** (the fourth, *Mira*, is a FOLIO — `scene.folio.ts`, `?world=folio` — see `references/16`) (add *NOCTA*, a product altar: `scene.halo.ts`, `?world=halo`, with `studio.ts` (designed environment map + product light rig), `rock.ts` (asteroid ground + boulders), `objects.ts` (earbuds with exploded-ascent parts, machined dial, glass bottle)) — study\n   the closest to your brief. The original two: to study: *Obsidian Gate* (an object / product — `scene.example.ts`) and *Umbra* (an event: terrain + eclipse sky + light shafts + a monolith — `scene.umbra.ts`, built only from the helpers, ~190 lines). Run either with `npm run dev` (`?world=umbra`). **Copy it. Do not rewrite it.** It already implements the damped scroll playhead, a **deterministic `window.__aura.seek(p)` / `?p=` QA hook, procedural sky + eclipse, light shafts, haze, glow, dunes (`atmosphere.ts`, `terrain.ts`), measured fit-to-viewport giant type (`fitType`), the shot-list camera, post stack, adaptive quality, DOM choreography, particles, reflective floor, depth-displaced images and a React shell. Your job is the *world*, the *camera moves* and the *story* — that is where the value is.\n\n---\n\n## 1 · The ten laws (non-negotiable)\n\n1. **One idea, one world, one camera.** Choose a single metaphor (a descent into the ocean, a night walk through a temple district, a product orbiting in a void, a house assembling itself). The camera moves through it continuously from first pixel to last. No section boundaries visible to the user.\n2. **Scroll is the playhead, never a trigger.** Do not \"animate on scroll into view\". Map scroll progress → camera position, object transforms, light, grade and text — *scrubbed*, reversible, damped. Scroll up and the film rewinds.\n3. **Depth is built, not declared.** Every frame must contain ≥5 depth planes: foreground occluder (blur/silhouette/particles), subject, mid-ground, background, atmosphere (fog/haze/dust/light shafts). Parallax between planes is what the brain reads as 3D.\n4. **Light is designed.** Key, rim, fill and at least one *practical* (a lantern, a screen, a window, an ember) in the world. Dark scenes with one hot, colored rim light beat bright flat scenes every time. Never ship default lighting.\n5. **Matter has a story.** Roughness varies. Surfaces have fresnel, clearcoat, sheen, emission, wear. Anything uniformly matte or uniformly glossy looks like 2009 CG. Use `scene.environment` always.\n6. **Type is architecture.** Giant display type (often wider than the viewport) that the world interacts with — occluded by the subject, reflected in the floor, scrubbed in tracking, lit by the scene. Max two typefaces. **Max ~12 words per beat.** The 3D does the talking.\n7. **Pace like a film editor.** Alternate WIDE (breathe) and CLOSE (detail) shots. Something meaningful changes every ~0.8–1.5 screens of scroll. Reserve one **hold** (a stretch of scroll where the camera nearly stops) for each hero moment so people can *feel* it. One crescendo, one release, one final image.\n8. **Restraint.** One hero effect per chapter. A palette of 1 dominant hue + 1 accent + neutrals. Effects you can't justify in the story get deleted. Bloom with a *high threshold* — glow only on things that are supposed to glow.\n9. **60fps is part of the aesthetic.** Adaptive quality, capped DPR, instancing, no per-frame allocations, compressed assets. Mobile gets a *designed* variant (fewer layers, shorter rail, lower DPR), never a broken one. Reduced-motion users get a calm, complete version.\n10. **Look at it.** You must render, screenshot at ≥12 scroll positions, and critique your own frames like a harsh art director before you say \"done\". At least three polish passes. Score yourself with the rubric in `references/08-qa-and-polish.md`; ship only ≥ 85/100.\n\n---\n\n## 2 · The pipeline (do the phases in order; each has an exit criterion)\n\n### Phase 0 — Intake (≤ 2 minutes)\nExtract or infer: **subject** (what/who), **audience**, **the one emotion** to leave them with, **the conversion goal** (buy / book / hire / enter / follow), **brand assets** (logo, colors, copy, images, models), **constraints** (stack, deadline, must-support devices).\n- If critical info is missing, ask **at most 3–5 sharp questions in ONE message** — otherwise *decide for the user*, state your assumptions in one paragraph, and proceed. Never stall on a questionnaire.\n- *Exit:* you can state the project in one sentence: \"A [scroll-driven film] that takes [audience] through [world] so they feel [emotion] and then [action].\"\n\n### Phase 1a — Mode + Direction Lock (do this BEFORE the brief; 60 seconds; non-negotiable)\nFirst pick the **mode** with the table in `references/16-modes-world-folio-hybrid.md`: *judge the work* → FOLIO; *feel a place / want an object* → WORLD; both → HYBRID. Write it as the first line of the Direction Lock.\n\nOpen `references/11-art-directions.md`. Pick ONE of the 12 directions (blend at most a second at 20 %), then write the **Direction Lock** block (direction · world · hero · *named signature move* · palette · camera personality · three forbidden things). Read the two closest files in `examples/` and match their *level of specificity*. If the subject is a product, also read `references/12-product-hero-playbook.md`; a portfolio/brand → `13-…`. Say the direction out loud in your creative statement. **Never default to \"dark cinematic with a glowing orb\".** A light-mode, a macro-appetite or an instrument direction is often the winning, surprising choice.\n\n### Phase 1 — Concept → `AURA_BRIEF.md`\nFollow `references/02-concept-and-storyboard.md` (fill `templates/AURA_BRIEF.template.md`). Produce: world metaphor, hero object/hero image, emotional arc (3 acts), palette (hex), type pairing, lighting mood, camera language, sound mood, \"never do\" list. **Run the Authorship test:** if you could paste the concept onto a competitor, it's not done.\n- *Exit:* brief written to the repo.\n\n### Phase 2 — Storyboard → `STORYBOARD.md`\nA table of **chapters** (use `templates/STORYBOARD.template.md`): scroll range (0–1), shot (camera move + lens), what changes in the world, copy (≤12 words), type treatment, transition into the next chapter. Include the **loader** (chapter 0) and the **final frame**. Total scroll length in viewport heights (see sizing rules in `references/03-choreography.md`).\n- *Exit:* every scroll range is covered; there is ≥1 hold; the shots alternate wide/close; the last frame is a deliberate image with the call-to-action.\n\n### Phase 3 — Asset plan → `ASSETS.md`\nDecide for each element: **procedural** (code), **generated image** (2.5D layers / depth-displaced plane / hero plate), **GLB** (sourced or AI-generated), or **video/frame sequence**. Follow `references/05-asset-pipeline.md` — it contains the exact image-generation prompts, depth/cut-out workflow and compression budget.\n- If you have image generation, **use it**: a generated, art-directed hero plate + depth is the fastest route to photoreal \"AAA\" quality. If you don't, build procedurally (the starter's `scene.example.ts` is a zero-asset world that already looks expensive).\n- *Exit:* manifest with file names, sizes, source/prompt; total first-load ≤ 12 MB.\n\n### Phase 4 — Scaffold\nCopy `starter/src/aura/*` into the project (Vite + React is the default; for Next.js put the experience in a client component and load it with `dynamic(..., { ssr: false })`). Install `three`. Mount `<AuraExperience>`. Confirm the placeholder scene scrolls at 60fps **before** customizing anything.\n- *Exit:* dev server running, example scene visible and scrubbing.\n\n### Phase 5 — Build the world in this exact order (never skip ahead to effects)\n1. **Blockout:** grey/lit primitives for each landmark; write the camera `shots` for the whole storyboard; scroll through; fix pacing *now*.\n2. **Light:** key/rim/fill/practical + `scene.environment`; set exposure; set fog color = background color.\n3. **Materials & hero:** replace primitives with real materials/models/plates (`materials.ts`).\n4. **Atmosphere:** fog density, 2–3 particle bands, light shafts, floor reflection, volumetric glow cards.\n5. **Typography & DOM choreography:** chapters with `data-a/data-b` windows locked to the shot list; giant type; HUD; nav.\n6. **Post & grade:** bloom threshold, grain, vignette, aberration, split-toning (`grade` in the scene).\n7. **Interaction:** pointer parallax (built-in), magnetic CTA, cursor, hover on hero object, ambient sound (opt-in only).\n8. **Conversion:** a final frame with ONE obvious action; secondary pages (pricing / contact / work) in the same world, not a different site.\n\n### Phase 6 — Polish (≥ 3 passes)\nPass A *Motion:* timing of every entrance, hold lengths, easing, velocity feel. Pass B *Image:* color grade, contrast, highlights, depth separation, kill any flat frame. Pass C *Details:* micro-typography, cursor, loader, hover states, 404, favicon, OG image, scroll-snap-free ending.\n\n### Phase 7 — QA gate\nRun `node scripts/audit.mjs <url>` (from `starter/`) (see `references/08-qa-and-polish.md`): it seeks the playhead deterministically, captures 14 stops × 3 viewports, measures every frame, lints the shot list and DOM chapters, and tests no-WebGL + reduced-motion. Then **open the contact sheets and critique them**. Also check FPS (`--gpu`), reduced motion, mobile touch scroll, resize, tab-hidden. Score. **If < 85, fix the lowest-scoring category and re-score.** Ship the score in your final message.\n\n---\n\n## 3 · Stack decisions (defaults — deviate only with a reason)\n\n| Need | Default | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Renderer | `three` (vanilla, via `starter/src/aura/engine.ts`) | R3F is fine if the project is already React-Three-Fiber; port the same shot-list/playhead design. |\n| Scroll | Native scroll + damped playhead | Add Lenis only if the user wants inertia on the *DOM*; the camera is already damped. Never hijack scroll with `preventDefault`. |\n| DOM animation | CSS variables written by `bindDom` | GSAP/ScrollTrigger is acceptable for complex timelines; still drive from the same playhead. |\n| Post-FX | `EffectComposer` (bloom → output → custom grade) | `postprocessing` lib optional for DOF / god-rays / LUTs. |\n| Assets | procedural → generated images → GLB | Compress with `gltf-transform` (meshopt + webp/KTX2). |\n| Framework | Vite + React + TS | Next.js: client component + `ssr:false`. Plain HTML works too (copy the TS to JS). |\n| Fonts | 1 display + 1 mono, variable, self-hosted | See `references/06-typography-and-ui.md` for pairings. |\n\n---\n\n## 4 · The anti-slop blacklist (if you catch yourself doing these, stop)\n\n- A normal website with a spinning 3D logo in the hero. The 3D must **be** the website.\n- Fade-up-on-scroll cards as the main animation language.\n- Default `OrbitControls`, auto-rotating turntables, a lonely rotating cube/sphere/torus-knot as the hero.\n- Purple→blue gradient blobs, glassmorphism cards stacked on a mesh gradient, \"Welcome to the future of…\" copy.\n- Centered Inter at 64px in a max-width container. (Type must be *big, intentional and part of the scene*.)\n- Flat unlit `MeshBasicMaterial` everywhere; uniform roughness; no environment map; pure black `#000` with no fog.\n- Bloom on everything (threshold 0) — a blurry mess. Chromatic aberration at 100%.\n- A scroll length of 200vh for 8 \"moments\". Scroll must be **earned**: 500–1200vh.\n- Content walls: paragraphs, feature grids and logo bars in the middle of the experience. Park them *after* the final frame or on secondary pages.\n- Autoplaying sound. Hijacked scroll. Scroll-jacking that fights the trackpad. Layout shift on load.\n- Placeholder lorem ipsum, \"Product Name\", gray boxes shipped in the final image.\n\n---\n\n## 5 · Archetype quick reference\n(Full playbooks in `references/02-concept-and-storyboard.md`.)\n\n- **Product launch** → the object *is* the world: macro orbit, exploded view, material close-ups, environment that expresses its use. Hold on the \"money shot\".\n- **Portfolio / personal** → the visitor *walks through the author's mind*: a place that expresses craft, projects as rooms/objects on the rail, a hold on each.\n- **Real estate / architecture / hospitality** → *arrive* at the place: aerial → approach → threshold → interior at golden hour. Giant name, reflective water/floor, warm practicals.\n- **Brand / agency / studio** → a *manifesto in motion*: giant type as architecture, abstract material world, fast rhythm, strong end-card.\n- **Game / film / entertainment** → *the trailer*: cold open, title reveal, character/world beats, cliff-hanger final frame, ominous low camera moves.\n- **SaaS / app** → *the product as a place*: UI planes floating in depth, data as light/particles, the interface assembling around the user.\n- **Restaurant / food / lifestyle** → *appetite*: macro, steam/smoke particles, warm light, shallow depth of field, tactile materials.\n- **Event / campaign / nonprofit** → *a journey with a destination*: pulling the visitor through a story toward a date/action.\n\n---\n\n## 6 · Output protocol\n\n1. Start with a 4–6 line **creative statement** (world, hero, arc, palette, lens language). Make the user want to see it.\n2. Build in phases; keep files tidy: `src/aura/` (engine, untouched), `src/world/` (your scene modules), `src/chapters/` (DOM), `public/assets/`.\n3. After each major phase, run the app, capture frames, and fix issues **before moving on**.\n4. Final message: what you built (one paragraph), how to run it, the shot list table, the QA score table, and the three things you'd do next with more time. Do not dump code in chat.\n\n## 7 · When the user says \"make it better\"\nDon't add features. Go through the rubric, find the **lowest-scoring pillar** (A/U/R/A), and fix that: usually *more depth separation, a stronger hold, bigger type, or a better rim light.* Then re-screenshot.\n\n## 8 · The verification contract (what \"done\" means)\nYou may not say \"done\" until ALL of these are true — paste the evidence in your final message:\n1. `node scripts/audit.mjs <url>` (from `starter/`) exits 0 (**no blockers**) on desktop, mobile, wide — and you read `REPORT.md`.\n2. You opened `contact-desktop.png` and `contact-mobile.png` and wrote a one-line art-director critique **per frame** (poster test · 5 depth planes · light · type) and fixed what you found, ≥ 3 loops.\n3. Frame one has type on screen; the last frame is a composed image with ONE live CTA (the engine hides neither if you start chapter 1 at `data-a=\"0\"` and end the last at `data-b=\"1\"`).\n4. Giant type uses `data-fit` — never guessed vw. Nothing is cropped or collides at 390 px.\n5. `__aura.info().drawCalls` ≤ 160 and the no-WebGL fallback is a designed poster, not a blank page.\n6. Your score table includes the audit's automated points **and** your own judged Authorship points, honestly.\n\n## 9 · The $10,000 bar\nA paid-for site is the film **plus** an end-state, routes, metadata, a designed loader, a mobile edit, a reduced-motion edit, a fallback poster, a hand-off README and one `config.ts` for every knob — see `references/14-premium-delivery.md`. If you only built the hero, you built a demo. Say so, or finish the job.\n\n## 10 · Calibrate against the references\nBefore you ship, put your contact sheet next to `starter/src/aura/scene.umbra.ts` / `scene.example.ts` renders. If yours has fewer depth planes, a weaker last frame or flatter light than those zero-asset worlds, you are not done.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/examples/01-nocta-earbuds-product.md":"# 01 · NOCTA — earbuds on an asteroid (Product Altar)\n\n## The prompt\n> \"Build a 3D website for NOCTA, premium wireless earbuds with active noise cancelling. Dark, cinematic, product is the hero. Ship it like a $10k site.\"\n\n## Direction lock\n```\nDIRECTION: 01 Product Altar (90%) + 09 Terrain Epic sky (10%)\nWORLD: a cratered asteroid in cold starlight with a black planet limb behind — silence made physical.\nHERO: earbuds + charging case (graphite lacquer, mint LED), resting on a flat basalt shelf.\nSIGNATURE MOVE: The Exploded Ascent — the case opens, buds and 6 internal parts levitate on one axis with leader-line labels.\nPALETTE: #05070b (dominant) · #7dffd9 (accent / LED) · #9fb4c8 (cold rock highlight) · #e9f1f6 (type)\nCAMERA PERSONALITY: patient, macro-first, 85–135 mm feel; one pull-back reveal.\nFORBIDDEN: studio void · rotating turntable · feature grid mid-scroll.\n```\n\n## Creative statement\nWe find NOCTA in the dark: a single mint LED on a rock older than weather. The camera is a macro lens crawling over lacquer, then the case opens and its anatomy rises like a held breath. We pull back to reveal the asteroid, the planet, the silence — and land on the buds, perfectly lit, with one button: *Pre-order*.\n\n## Storyboard (scrollVh 1000)\n| p | Shot | World change | Copy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–.12 | Low macro of case lid edge, rim only | LED blinks once; stars drift | **NOCTA** (giant, behind) · \"Silence, engineered.\" |\n| .12–.28 | Slide across lacquer to the hinge (fov 16) | highlight line travels the lid | \"Lacquer polished 14 times.\" |\n| .28–.40 | Crane up 40 cm, case opens | LED ramps, practical lights rock | — |\n| .40–.62 | **Exploded Ascent** — camera slightly high, parts rise in order | labels draw in with leader lines | \"48 dB\" · \"11 mm driver\" · \"5.4 g\" |\n| .62–.74 | Parts re-seat; pull back wide | planet limb enters; rim goes cold | \"Made for the quiet hours.\" |\n| .74–.90 | **Hold** — 3/4 low hero, buds in the foreground | motes lag the lens; bloom gentle | \"30 hours. One charge.\" |\n| .90–1 | Slow dolly in 10 % | LED steady; CTA fades up | **Pre-order — ships March** |\n\n## Implementation notes\n- Reference: `starter/src/aura/scene.halo.ts` (+ `HaloExperience.tsx`) — restyle, don't rewrite.\n- Environment: `createStudioEnv` with two strip lights + one mint accent strip; `scene.environmentIntensity` 0.9 on product layer, 0.15 on rock (use `layers` or per-material `envMapIntensity`).\n- The rock is *displaced + noise-roughness*; the shelf under the product is a flatter, slightly polished disc so the product doesn't float.\n- Labels: project 3D anchors to 2D each frame, set `transform` on pre-created DOM nodes (no layout thrash).\n\n## The lazy version\nA centred GLB on a gradient with a rotation animation and a features grid. It passes \"works\", fails the poster test, the competitor test and the price test.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/examples/02-ardvale-estate-arrival.md":"# 02 · ARDVALE — alpine estate development (Dusk Arrival)\n\n## The prompt\n> \"Luxury lakeside residences in the Alps, four villas. High-end buyers. They should feel like they've already arrived.\"\n\n## Direction lock\n```\nDIRECTION: 03 Dusk Arrival\nWORLD: blue-hour lake under a ridgeline; four stone-and-timber villas stepped along the shore; the lake mirrors everything.\nHERO: the villas with windows igniting one by one; the name ARDVALE giant behind them, mirrored in the water.\nSIGNATURE MOVE: Windows Ignite + Name in Water.\nPALETTE: #07131f (sky/water) · #ffb45e (windows, CTA) · #cbd5de (stone) · #f3f5f7 (type)\nCAMERA PERSONALITY: aerial → skim → threshold; unhurried, architectural, level horizon.\nFORBIDDEN: daylight · floor-plan carousel in the film · drone-spin.\n```\n## Storyboard (scrollVh 900)\n| p | Shot | Change | Copy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–.15 | High aerial, ridge in silhouette | stars faint; first window lights | **ARDVALE** · \"Four houses. One shoreline.\" |\n| .15–.38 | Descend to water level, skim toward the villas | windows ignite left→right; reflections ripple | — |\n| .38–.55 | **Hold** — symmetrical low shot, name behind & reflected | mist crawls on water; ember motes | \"Arrive before you arrive.\" |\n| .55–.72 | Slide to villa 02, close on stone + glass | warm interior bleeds through glazing | \"Stone from the valley. Glass from Zurich.\" |\n| .72–.86 | Threshold — camera passes the door light | exposure rises, grade warms | \"Step inside.\" |\n| .86–1 | Pull out to final exterior frame | all four villas lit | **Request the residences** |\n## Implementation\nGenerated dusk plate + depth plane for the villas (`05-asset-pipeline.md`), procedural water (mirror floor + ripple normal), `createHaze`, instanced window quads whose emissive intensity = `seg(p, a_i, a_i+0.03)`. Park floor plans, specs and the map *below* the final frame.\n## The lazy version\nA drone video background and a grid of \"Features\". No reflection, no ignition, no arrival.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/examples/03-bearing-instrument.md":"# 03 · BEARING — a navigation instrument (The Instrument)\n\n## The prompt\n> \"Landing page for BEARING, an offline navigation compass for sailors and hikers. Precise. Trustworthy. Make people want to hold it.\"\n\n## Direction lock\n```\nDIRECTION: 04 The Instrument\nWORLD: a ship's chart table at night; one lamp; the compass in focus, a blurred bridge window behind.\nHERO: a machined dial: knurled bezel, engraved ticks, glass dome, amber needle.\nSIGNATURE MOVE: Calibration — scroll rotates the dial 0→360°; ticks light progressively; detents at 90° boundaries swap the headline.\nPALETTE: #06141b (teal-black) · #ffb45a (needle, ticks) · #e8eef2 (type) · #2a4350 (steel)\nFORBIDDEN: flat 2D dial · more than 6 HUD items on screen · glassmorphism cards.\n```\n## Storyboard (scrollVh 800)\n| p | Shot | Change | Copy / readout |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–.15 | Front-on hero, HUD furniture appears | needle swings 0→67° (spring) | \"Find your HEADING.\" · `067° NE` |\n| .15–.38 | Oblique macro on knurl & engraving | highlight rakes the bezel | \"Cut from one block of titanium.\" |\n| .38–.60 | Dial rotates, ticks ignite; detent at 180° | readout counts; headline swaps | \"No signal. No problem.\" · `180° S` |\n| .60–.80 | Pull back — dial inside the chart table scene | lamp warms | \"Battery: 400 days.\" |\n| .80–1 | Frontal hold, needle locks N | all ticks lit | **Pre-order BEARING** |\n## Implementation\n`objects.ts → dial()` (lathe + instanced ticks + dome), HUD readouts as DOM bound to `data-heading`, detent via `snap()`/spring ease, bokeh sprites for the blurred bridge. Keep the HUD tiny and mono.\n## The lazy version\nAn SVG dial that rotates. Nobody wants to touch it.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/examples/04-tsuki-night-walk.md":"# 04 · TSUKI — ramen & sake house (Night Walk)\n\n## The prompt\n> \"Website for TSUKI, a 12-seat ramen and sake house in Kyoto-style alley. I want people to feel like they're walking in.\"\n\n## Direction lock\n```\nDIRECTION: 05 Night Walk\nWORLD: a lantern-lit alley under a blood moon; torii, stone steps, a low wooden facade with steam from the door.\nHERO: the doorway curtain (noren) at the end of the walk.\nSIGNATURE MOVE: Type Between Layers — \"TSUKI\" sits between foreground grass and the shrine; letters split as the camera passes through.\nPALETTE: #0b0a14 (indigo-black) · #c4202a (moon, gates) · #ffb25e (lanterns) · #efe6d6 (type)\nFORBIDDEN: stock food photos · flat parallax · red everywhere.\n```\n## Storyboard (scrollVh 900) — ≥ 7 planes: moon, ridge, far roofs, mid gates, shopfront, foreground grass/lanterns, particle layers\n| p | Shot | Change | Copy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–.14 | Wide over the street, moon huge | embers drift | **TSUKI** · \"Twelve seats. One broth.\" |\n| .14–.36 | Walk forward under torii, letters split | lanterns flicker in sequence | — |\n| .36–.52 | **Hold** — the doorway, steam, noren sway | warm light spills on wet stone | \"Open at dusk. Closed when it's gone.\" |\n| .52–.72 | Push through the noren — macro on a bowl (generated plate + steam particles) | grade warms 12 % | \"Fourteen hours of bone.\" |\n| .72–.88 | Back out to the counter — 12 seats lit | lanterns bloom | \"Twelve seats.\" |\n| .88–1 | Final frame at the door | moon halo | **Reserve a seat** |\n## Implementation\n`createLayerStack` with 7 generated plates (alpha cut-outs), moon as `createGlow` + a depth-sorted disc, steam = particles with soft sprites, noren = a cloth vertex-sway shader.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/examples/05-oneiric-liquid-particles.md":"# 05 · ONEIRIC — a creative-AI studio (Liquid Particles)\n\n## The prompt\n> \"Site for ONEIRIC, a creative studio that makes AI-powered installations and brand worlds. It should feel like the future. Our nav: Work, Services, Contact.\"\n\n## Direction lock\n```\nDIRECTION: 06 Liquid Particles (85%) + 12 glass panels (15%)\nWORLD: a dark volume; a figure sculpted from ~200k points of cold light, reacting to the cursor.\nHERO: the particle figure; three floating glass panels carry the copy.\nSIGNATURE MOVE: The Dissolve — figure → drifting storm → logo glyph → planet, each hand-off driven by scroll, with curl-noise streams.\nPALETTE: #04060f · #7f6bff (violet) · #3de7ff (ice) · #ff6b7a (coral counter-light)\nFORBIDDEN: random noise cloud · same shape twice · large points.\n```\n## Storyboard (scrollVh 800)\n| p | Shot | Change | Copy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–.15 | Figure centred, tilts toward cursor | points shimmer w/ pointer force field | \"DISCOVER YOUR…\" (fitted type, glass panel) |\n| .15–.35 | Figure dissolves into stream sweeping left→right | camera sidesteps | \"We build worlds that answer back.\" |\n| .35–.55 | Points settle into the studio glyph; **hold** | coral rim kicks in | \"Installations · Films · Interfaces\" |\n| .55–.75 | Glyph → planet, camera orbits | glass panels glide past | \"Selected work\" (3 panels = 3 projects) |\n| .75–1 | Planet → a single bright point → CTA | everything calms | **Start a project** |\n## Implementation\n`15-shader-recipes.md §2`. Sample figure positions from a generated silhouette (canvas → alpha → positions) or `MeshSurfaceSampler` on any bust GLB. Pointer pushes points via a uniform `uPointer` projected into world space.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/examples/06-mira-observatory-folio.md":"# 06 · MIRA OKONKWO — product designer portfolio (Observatory Folio)\n\n## The prompt\n> \"Portfolio for Mira Okonkwo, product designer. Six case studies. I want recruiters to remember it a week later.\"\n\n## Direction lock\n```\nDIRECTION: 07 Observatory Folio\nWORLD: an observatory at night with a brass-and-glass instrument hall; each project is a lit plane on the ring rail.\nHERO: the name in fitted type; a light that follows the cursor and reveals engraved detail in the architecture.\nSIGNATURE MOVE: Project Rooms — enter a project → world dims, plane lights, HUD swaps to 03/06.\nPALETTE: #0a0c14 · #e0b86a (brass) · #6b88ff (night) · #f1ede4 (type)\nFORBIDDEN: thumbnail grid · every project at the same distance · missing contact.\n```\n## Storyboard (scrollVh 1000)\n| p | Shot | Copy |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–.10 | Name, cursor light | **MIRA OKONKWO** · \"Product designer. I make hard things feel obvious.\" |\n| .10–.20 | Doors of the hall open | — |\n| .20–.70 | Six rooms, each with its own distance/angle/light; hold on each | Title · role · year · one number |\n| .70–.80 | Craft = three objects of light | \"Systems · Motion · Research\" |\n| .80–.90 | About — silhouette close | one human sentence |\n| .90–1 | Contact | **mira@…** · \"Tell me what you're making.\" |\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/examples/07-lumen-glass-atrium.md":"# 07 · LUMEN — skincare brand (Glass Atrium, LIGHT mode)\n\n## The prompt\n> \"Landing page for LUMEN, a clinical-luxury serum brand. Light, airy, expensive. Absolutely not dark and techy.\"\n\n## Direction lock\n```\nDIRECTION: 12 Glass Atrium\nWORLD: a bright atrium of frosted glass slabs and a floating arch, soft caustics on pale stone, sage and clay accents.\nHERO: a frosted-glass bottle with a visible serum column, resting on a polished stone plinth with a real contact shadow.\nSIGNATURE MOVE: Refraction Reveal — a glass slab slides over the headline; the type beneath bends and shifts.\nPALETTE: #f4efe7 (bone) · #9fb39a (sage) · #c58f73 (clay) · #1b1b1b (type)\nFORBIDDEN: dark mode · neon · overexposed whites without contrast.\n```\n## Storyboard (scrollVh 800)\n| p | Shot | Copy |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–.15 | Wide atrium, bottle small on plinth | **LUMEN** · \"Skin, clarified.\" |\n| .15–.35 | Glass slab sweeps over type (refraction) | \"Ninety-one clinical trials.\" |\n| .35–.55 | Macro: serum column & dropper (hold) | \"One molecule. Nothing else.\" |\n| .55–.75 | Arch and slabs drift; caustics ripple on floor | \"Made in small batches.\" |\n| .75–1 | Hero bottle, CTA | **Begin your ritual** |\n## Implementation\n`glass()` for the bottle, `MeshPhysicalMaterial` frosted for slabs, planar contact shadow, exposure 1.05, bloom threshold 1.0, grade tint: shadows toward cool sage, highlights warm clay.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/examples/README.md":"# Examples — what \"the user wants\" looks like\n\nSeven complete briefs → direction locks → storyboards. They are **calibration**, not templates: read the two that are closest to your task, then *invent something else of equal ambition*. Each follows the same anatomy so you can learn the pattern:\n\n1. **The prompt** — what the user actually typed (usually 1–3 sentences).\n2. **Direction lock** — art direction, world, hero, signature move, palette, forbidden list.\n3. **Creative statement** — the 5-line pitch you give first.\n4. **Storyboard** — scroll ranges, shot, what changes, copy (≤ 12 words).\n5. **Signature move** — how to implement the one thing people will screenshot.\n6. **The lazy version** — what a no-skill agent builds, and why it loses.\n\n| # | File | Direction | Subject | Feels like |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 01 | `01-nocta-earbuds-product.md` | Product Altar | wireless earbuds | a flagship launch on an asteroid |\n| 02 | `02-ardvale-estate-arrival.md` | Dusk Arrival | alpine estate development | a $5k real-estate reel |\n| 03 | `03-bearing-instrument.md` | The Instrument | navigation compass / wearable | a luxury cockpit instrument |\n| 04 | `04-tsuki-night-walk.md` | Night Walk | ramen & sake house | a shrine at night, game-opening |\n| 05 | `05-oneiric-liquid-particles.md` | Liquid Particles | creative-AI studio | a sculpture of light that reforms |\n| 06 | `06-mira-observatory-folio.md` | Observatory Folio | a product designer | a portfolio as a place |\n| 07 | `07-lumen-glass-atrium.md` | Glass Atrium (light) | skincare brand | a bright, expensive, calm space |\n\n## The universal quality delta (skill vs. \"just ask it\")\n| Just asking an AI | With AURA |\n| --- | --- |\n| Spinning cube/torus + gradient + fade-up cards | One authored world, one camera, scroll = playhead |\n| Default lights, no env map | Key/rim/fill/practical + designed environment map |\n| One depth plane | ≥ 5 depth planes every frame |\n| Inter 64 px centred | Giant fitted type the world occludes and reflects |\n| \"Looks good\" | 12 frames × 3 viewports, scored ≥ 85/100, three polish loops |\n| Desktop only | Designed mobile edit, reduced-motion edit, no-WebGL poster |\n| A hero | A hero + end-state + routes + metadata + hand-off |\n\n## Weak vs strong prompt (the skill makes both work, but know the difference)\n- Weak: *\"make a 3D website for my earbuds\"* → the skill runs Intake, **decides** the direction, states assumptions, and builds.\n- Strong: *\"Earbuds called NOCTA, matte graphite with a mint LED, for night-shift workers; feel: silence made physical; CTA: pre-order; palette cold + mint\"* → the skill locks it in 30 s and spends the time on craft.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/01-aura-doctrine.md":"# 01 · The Aura Doctrine — what separates \"AAA\" from \"3D website\"\n\nStudy the sites that make people stop mid-scroll and screen-record them: a luxury estate whose name is a 300px wordmark reflected in a lake while lit windows glow behind it; a shrine-district night walk where the title letters sit *between* the temple and the foreground grass; a pair of earbuds floating in a cratered void, lit like a Kubrick still; a holographic card dissolving into particles. They share the same DNA. This file is that DNA, decoded.\n\n## The six invisible systems\n\n### 1 · A single continuous camera\nThe visitor never sees a \"section\". The camera flies; the world changes around it. Transitions are *spatial* (fly through a door, ascend through cloud, pass behind a pillar, dive under the water line, whip-pan past a foreground object), never a cut or a fade-to-white.\n\n**Transition toolbox** (pick one per chapter boundary):\n- **Occluder wipe** — a foreground object (pillar, branch, wall, hand) passes across the lens and hides the swap.\n- **Fog/cloud pass** — fog density spikes to 1 for 200ms of scroll while the set swaps.\n- **Dive/ascend** — the camera crosses a water surface / ceiling / membrane; grade and fog flip.\n- **Scale jump** — camera dollies into a small detail that *becomes* the next world (macro → vista).\n- **Light flash** — practical light blooms to white then settles on the new scene.\n- **Whip** — fast yaw (FOV kick + aberration) between two vistas.\n\n### 2 · Light as a character\n- **Dark-field lighting:** exposure low, bloom threshold high, one saturated rim light that traces the subject's silhouette, one warm practical. This alone makes a mediocre model look cinematic.\n- **Colour contrast, not brightness contrast:** warm vs cool (amber window vs teal dusk; magenta rim vs cyan fill). Pick a split from the brief and stay on it.\n- **Light moves:** practicals flicker slightly, rim lights sweep with scroll, a god-ray angle changes between shots. A static rig reads as a render; a moving rig reads as a *place*.\n- **Time of day is a story tool:** dusk → night across the scroll gives a free emotional arc and a free colour grade.\n\n### 3 · Depth you can feel\nThe brain reads depth from: **parallax, occlusion, atmospheric perspective, scale cues, depth-of-field, motion.** Use all six deliberately:\n- **Parallax:** 5+ layers at distinct z. Far layers move < 20% of near layers. Pointer parallax on top.\n- **Occlusion:** the subject passes *behind* something. Type passes behind the subject.\n- **Atmospheric perspective:** far = lower contrast, bluer/hazier, lighter; near = saturated, sharp. Exponential fog does this for free; also tint far layers.\n- **Scale cues:** tiny humans, lanterns, birds, windows lit in the distance. A hero with no scale cue looks like a toy.\n- **DOF:** fake it (blur DOM foreground, soft particles, bokeh sprites) or use `BokehPass`. Even a *hint* of foreground blur is huge.\n- **Motion:** drifting dust, steam, birds, floating shards. The air should never be still.\n\n### 4 · Type that belongs to the world\n- **Scale:** the hero word is 18–30vw. It's cropped by the viewport on purpose.\n- **Interaction:** occluded by the subject (text sandwich), reflected on the floor, masked by clouds, lit by a sweeping specular, split by scroll, tracking changes from tight to wide as you push in.\n- **Quiet supporting type:** tiny mono labels (coordinates, chapter numbers, specs, timestamps) in the corners. The extreme scale contrast is what looks \"designed\".\n- **Copy discipline:** headline ≤ 6 words, support ≤ 14 words, one CTA. Verbs and nouns. No adjectives like \"innovative\".\n\n### 5 · Rhythm\nThink in **beats** of ~100vh of scroll:\n`ESTABLISH (wide, quiet) → PUSH (move + reveal) → DETAIL (close, hold) → TWIST (scale/colour/space change) → CRESCENDO (fast, wide lens, particles surge) → RESOLVE (slow, final image + CTA)`.\nNever make every beat the same speed. The hold after a fast move is where awe lives.\n\n### 6 · Finish\nThe last 10% separates budgets: film grain, vignette, subtle chromatic aberration at the edges, ACES tone-mapping, split-toned grade, bloom restraint, aliasing-free edges (post FXAA/SMAA if MSAA is off), motion-blur-feel via FOV kick, consistent colour across DOM and canvas (the page background is *exactly* the fog colour).\n\n---\n\n## The Aura Test (apply at every phase)\n\n| Question | Fail looks like |\n| --- | --- |\n| Can I describe the world in one sentence a stranger would repeat? | \"It's a dark site with 3D stuff.\" |\n| If I pause on a random scroll position, is the frame a poster? | Mid-transition mush, flat lighting, centered clutter. |\n| Is there something in the foreground *and* something in the far distance? | Subject floating in empty void with no scale or atmosphere. |\n| Where is the light coming from, and is it coloured? | White light from nowhere. |\n| Does the type touch the world? | Text sits on top like a PowerPoint. |\n| Is there a hold? | Constant-speed conveyor belt. |\n| Would this look wrong on a competitor's brand? | Generic. |\n| What would I delete? | Anything you can't justify. Delete it. |\n\n## Where the \"price\" lives (rough weights of perceived value)\nLight & grade 25% · Camera choreography 20% · Typography & art direction 20% · Atmosphere/depth 15% · Material/model quality 10% · Interaction polish 5% · Performance feel 5%.\n**Notice modelling is only 10%.** A procedural scene with perfect lighting, a great shot list and giant type beats a photoscanned model in a flat setup. Spend your effort in the right places.\n\n## Common \"feels cheap\" diagnoses → fixes\n| Symptom | Cause | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| \"Looks like a tech demo\" | Default lights, no env, uniform roughness | Rig + env + roughness map; coloured rim; lower exposure |\n| \"Flat\" | One depth plane in focus | Add fg occluder, fog, mid-ground silhouettes, particles in 3 bands |\n| \"Boring\" | Constant camera speed, centered subject | Add holds, off-center framing (rule of thirds), Dutch roll ±2°, lens change |\n| \"Messy\" | Too many effects | Delete until each chapter has exactly one hero effect |\n| \"Template-y\" | Fade-ups, cards, gradients | Replace with spatial transitions + giant type interaction |\n| \"Laggy\" | DPR 2–3, huge textures, transmission everywhere | Cap DPR, compress, gate on quality governor |\n| \"Washed out\" | Tone-mapping off, bloom threshold low, lifted blacks | ACES, threshold ≥ 0.8, crush blacks via grade |\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/02-concept-and-storyboard.md":"# 02 · Concept & Storyboard — authoring the film before touching code\n\nMost agents jump straight to `npm create vite`. That is why most AI websites look the same. **The concept is 50% of the result.** Spend real effort here, and write it down.\n\n## Step 1 — Find the world\n\n### The World Metaphor method\nAsk: *\"If this brand/person/product were a PLACE you could fly through, what is it?\"* Generate **five** candidates, then choose the one that is (a) specific, (b) physically filmable, (c) emotionally consistent with the goal.\n\n| Subject | Weak (generic) | Strong (authored) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Wireless earbuds | Floating earbuds on gradient | Earbuds resting on a cratered asteroid in cold starlight, sound visualised as ripples in dust — *silence made physical* |\n| Mountain real-estate | Hero photo + gallery | Dusk arrival: the estate's name as giant letters reflected in an alpine lake, windows igniting one by one as you scroll |\n| Japanese craft studio | Minimal beige site | Night walk through a torii path to a temple under a red moon; each craft is a lantern you pass |\n| Dev portfolio | Grid of projects | An observatory: each project is a constellation you rotate the telescope toward |\n| Coffee roaster | Product cards | A descent into a roasting drum: beans, heat shimmer, steam, a slow-motion pour |\n| Fintech app | Dashboard screenshot | A vault door of light opening onto a city of flowing data |\n| Law firm / bank | Stock handshake | An impossibly quiet marble hall at dawn, one beam of light crossing the floor — *weight and permanence* |\n| Fitness brand | Athletes in grid | A single athlete silhouette in particle form, assembling mid-motion on a dark track |\n\n### The \"one emotion\" rule\nChoose ONE: *awe · reverence · menace · serenity · speed · wonder · intimacy · power · nostalgia · desire.* It controls palette, lens, tempo and sound:\n\n| Emotion | Palette | Lens | Tempo | Light |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Awe | deep blue/black + gold | wide→tele, big scale jumps | slow, long holds | god-rays, rim |\n| Reverence | warm stone, candle amber | symmetrical, slow dolly | very slow | single beam |\n| Menace | desaturated + blood red | low angle, Dutch roll | creeping, sudden whip | hard backlight, fog |\n| Serenity | teal/pearl/sand | long lens, drifting | slow, floating | soft haze, golden hour |\n| Speed | black + neon accent | wide 60–75°, high FOV kick | fast, hold only at end | streaks, rim |\n| Wonder | indigo + aurora mint | wide, upward tilts | medium, surprising | bioluminescent |\n| Desire | black + one rich hue | macro, shallow DOF | slow, sensual | specular sweeps |\n\n### Authorship test\nPaste your concept sentence onto three competitors. If it still works, rewrite it with a specific noun, place, time-of-day and material.\n\n## Step 2 — Write `AURA_BRIEF.md`\n\nUse this template exactly:\n\n```md\n# AURA BRIEF — [Project]\n**One sentence:** A scroll-driven film that takes [audience] through [world] so they feel [emotion], then [conversion action].\n**World:** [place, time of day, weather, material palette]\n**Hero:** [the one object / image / building / word that owns the poster frame]\n**Emotional arc:** Act I [quiet curiosity] → Act II [mounting wonder/tension] → Act III [release + invitation]\n**Palette:** bg #______ · fog #______ (== bg) · key #______ · rim #______ · accent #______ · text #______\n**Type:** Display [font] (usage: giant) · Mono/Text [font] (usage: HUD/labels)\n**Lens language:** [e.g. tele 24° on details, 60° on speed, one Dutch roll at the twist]\n**Light:** key [dir/colour] · rim [colour] · practical [what/where] · time-of-day arc [dusk→night]\n**Atmosphere:** [fog density, particle bands, floor, sky]\n**Sound (optional, opt-in):** [mood, 3 stems]\n**Never:** [3–5 things this world must not contain]\n**Conversion:** [single CTA, where it lives, what it does]\n```\n\n## Step 3 — Storyboard\n\nWrite `STORYBOARD.md` as a table. **Every row is one chapter.** Keep the whole experience to **5–9 chapters**; fewer, better.\n\n```md\n| # | Scroll | Shot (move + lens) | World change | Copy (≤12 words) | Type treatment | Transition out |\n|---|--------|--------------------|--------------|------------------|----------------|----------------|\n| 0 | loader | — | black → title wipe | project name | counter 000→100 | wipe up |\n| 1 | 0–.12  | Establish, wide 38°, slow drift | dusk lake, lights off | \"Some places you fall into.\" | GIANT wordmark, reflected | dolly forward |\n| 2 | .12–.30| Push-in, 34°, +parallax | windows ignite sequentially | — | wordmark slides behind house | pass behind pine foreground |\n| … |\n| N | .90–1  | Hold, locked-off, 45° | final tableau, practical glow | \"Reserve a residence.\" | CTA, magnetic | — |\n```\n\n### Sizing rules for scroll length\n- Count your **beats** (1 beat ≈ 100vh at an engaging pace; a *hold* is 30–60vh extra).\n- `scrollVh = 100 × beats + holds`. A 6-beat story with 2 holds ≈ **700vh**. Hero-grade experiences run **800–1200vh**.\n- On mobile multiply by ~0.8 (thumb scroll is faster) or keep the same; test.\n- Never go under 400vh — it will feel like a normal page with extra steps.\n\n### Chapter design checklist\n- [ ] Each chapter has **one** verb (arrive, ignite, descend, reveal, assemble, ascend…).\n- [ ] The shot types alternate: WIDE / CLOSE / WIDE / MACRO / VISTA.\n- [ ] There is one chapter whose job is *silence* (no copy, only world).\n- [ ] The final frame is a **poster**: composed, lit, CTA placed on a rule-of-thirds line.\n- [ ] A visitor who scrolls *backwards* sees a coherent film too.\n\n## Archetype playbooks (each includes a battle-tested chapter skeleton)\n\n### Product launch (hardware, beauty, drinks, cars, fashion)\n`0 Loader (logo + counter) → 1 Silhouette in darkness, rim-lit → 2 Material macro (surface, texture) → 3 Exploded/anatomy (parts float apart, labelled in mono type) → 4 In-world use (the environment that proves the benefit) → 5 Spec montage (big numbers, scrubbed counters) → 6 Hero orbit + CTA.`\nMoney shot: the slow 90° orbit with a specular sweep. Hold it.\n\n### Portfolio / personal brand\n`0 Loader → 1 Name as giant type, world establishing → 2 \"About\" as a place you enter (door, window, hatch) → 3–N One project per \"room/stop\" (camera pauses, project plate floats in depth, hover = tilt) → N+1 Skills as light/particles → Final: contact as the exit of the world.`\nTip: projects are images → map to depth planes (`createDepthPlane`) so each is a diorama.\n\n### Real estate / hospitality / architecture\n`0 Loader (coordinates) → 1 Aerial establishing at dusk → 2 Descend to the water/garden → 3 Approach with giant name behind/reflecting → 4 Threshold (door/lantern) → 5 Interior glide, warm practicals → 6 View from the terrace → 7 Floorplan as light lines → 8 Reserve CTA.`\nLook: golden hour, still water reflection, warm windows vs cool sky (the Ardmere formula).\n\n### Brand / agency / studio\n`Manifesto beats (one sentence each, giant) as the camera tunnels through abstract material space; recurring motif; case studies as cards drifting past; end with a bold contact line.`\n\n### Game / film / entertainment\n`Cold open (black, sound) → Title slam-in with light → World vista → Character/faction beats (each a distinct colour world) → Rapid montage (FOV kick, whip) → Final shot + release date.`\n\n### SaaS / AI product\n`UI planes (real product screenshots) arranged in depth → data as light-trails/particles → each feature = camera moves to a plane that \"activates\" → zoom through the final plane into the CTA.`\nNever fake data walls; use few, big, legible panels.\n\n### Restaurant / food\n`Steam in darkness → macro on ingredient → heat/shimmer → pour/slice slow-motion (scrubbed frame sequence) → the table scene → reserve.`\nUse video-frame sequences (see asset pipeline) for liquid/food — real footage scrubbed beats CG.\n\n### Event / campaign / non-profit\n`Countdown/date as giant type → journey (map or path as a glowing line the camera follows) → human moment (a single image in depth) → impact numbers → RSVP/donate.`\n\n## Anti-patterns in concepting\n- \"Futuristic\" as a concept. (That's a genre, not a world.)\n- Designing 12 chapters because the client has 12 features. Group them into 5–6 *moments*.\n- Using the camera path only to display content. The path is an **emotion curve**.\n- Planning copy first. Plan *images and moves* first; copy fills the silence.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/03-choreography.md":"# 03 · Choreography — camera, scroll, text, time\n\nThe engine (`starter/src/aura/engine.ts`) gives you the physics. This file teaches you to *direct* with it.\n\n## 1 · The playhead\n\n```\nnative scroll ─▶ trackProgress(section) = raw target 0..1\n                       │  critically damped (λ≈3.2)\n                       ▼\n                 f.p  (the playhead)  ─▶ camera rail, scene.update, DOM windows, HUD\n```\n- **One source of truth.** Never read `window.scrollY` anywhere else. Camera, copy, particles, light all read `f.p`.\n- **Damping is the dolly.** λ = 3.2 feels like a heavy cinema dolly. 2 = dreamy/floaty (serenity), 5 = snappy (speed/sport), 8+ = nearly 1:1 (UI-heavy). Change in `damp(S.prog, S.target, λ, dt)`. Reduced-motion uses 20.\n- **Velocity is free drama.** `f.velocity` (|Δp|/s, smoothed) drives FOV kick, chromatic fringe, particle stretch, ring spin. Use it for *every* \"this feels fast\" moment instead of scripted speed-ups.\n- **Never hijack scroll** (`preventDefault`, wheel capture). It breaks trackpads, a11y and mobile. Use the damped playhead instead — it feels hijacked without being hijacked.\n\n## 2 · Writing a shot list\n\n```ts\nconst shots: Shot[] = [\n  { at: 0.00, pos: [0, 1.4, 15], look: [0, 1.6, 0],  fov: 38 },               // establish\n  { at: 0.20, pos: [3.4, 1.8, 7.5], look: [0, 1.5, 0], fov: 34 },            // push-in\n  { at: 0.40, pos: [-2.6, 2.2, 3.4], look: [0, 1.6, 0], fov: 24, ease: \"inOut\" }, // macro (ease INTO the hold)\n  { at: 0.58, pos: [-2.6, 2.2, 3.4], look: [0, 1.6, 0], fov: 24 },           // HOLD = same pos twice\n  { at: 0.78, pos: [0.6, 6.5, -1.5], look: [0, 0.5, -7], fov: 52 },          // crane\n  { at: 1.00, pos: [0, 2.2, -16], look: [0, 2.4, -44], fov: 60 },            // fly-through\n];\n```\n- `at` is **time in scroll space**. Distance between `at` values = how long the move lasts. Wide gap = slow glide. Narrow gap = whip.\n- **Hold:** repeat pos/look/fov at two `at` values. The camera barely moves (pointer parallax + damping keep it alive) while copy is read. Hold ≈ 8–15% of the total range.\n- **Easing:** default `linear` keeps velocity continuous (feels like a camera operator). Use `inOut` *only* when settling into a hold; `out` for decelerating arrivals. Don't ease every segment — that causes stop-start \"conveyor\" feel.\n- Look-at is as important as position. **Lead the subject**: look slightly *ahead* of where you're going. Keep the subject on a third-line (look-at offset ±0.5–1.5 on x), not dead centre.\n- Keep ≥4 shots with a **different lens** each (24° tele / 38° natural / 52° wide / 60+° speed). Lens change is the cheapest \"production value\".\n- Add `roll: ±1.5–4` on one shot (Dutch angle) for tension; zero it out for calm shots.\n- Path-length awareness: Catmull-Rom overshoots on tight turns. If the camera clips geometry, add an intermediate shot, don't fight the curve.\n\n### Camera move vocabulary\n| Move | How | Feels like |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Push-in / dolly in** | pos.z decreases toward subject, fov slightly narrows | intimacy, importance |\n| **Pull-back reveal** | start tight, pos moves away, fov widens | \"oh — it's huge\" |\n| **Orbit / arc** | pos travels a circle, look fixed on subject | product worship |\n| **Crane up/over** | pos.y rises with look-at pulling down/forward | scale, god's-eye reveal |\n| **Truck/pan (parallax slide)** | pos.x moves, look.x moves 40% as far | layered 2.5D depth |\n| **Fly-through** | pos passes through an opening (door, ring, canyon) | transition between worlds |\n| **Dutch whip** | narrow `at` gap, fov +20, roll ±6, then settle | action beat |\n| **Tele-compression** | far camera + fov 20–26 | luxury, flattening, bokeh feel |\n| **Vertigo (dolly-zoom)** | move toward while widening fov | unease / reveal |\n\n## 3 · Driving the world from `f.p`\n\nPattern: describe each element's life as **windows** on the playhead using `seg`, `pulse`, `ease`:\n\n```ts\nupdate(f) {\n  const ignite   = ease.inOutCubic(seg(f.p, 0.25, 0.45));   // 0→1 during chapter 2–3\n  const calmDown = 1 - seg(f.p, 0.70, 0.85);\n  core.emissiveIntensity = 1.4 + ignite * calmDown * 5;\n  hero.rotation.y = f.p * Math.PI * 3.2;                     // scrub, never time-based spin\n  fog.density = lerp(0.045, 0.012, seg(f.p, 0.6, 0.8));      // fog clears as we ascend\n  rimLight.position.x = lerp(-6, 6, f.p);                    // rim sweeps across the subject\n}\n```\n**Never** use `setInterval`/`time`-only animation for narrative elements — they must scrub. Time-based motion (drift, flicker, breathing) is for *ambience* only and must be low-amplitude.\n\n### Things to scrub with scroll (checklist)\nHero rotation / assembly · light colour & intensity · fog density & colour · exposure · bloom strength · particle speed/size/colour · floor reflectivity · sky colour (time-of-day) · emissive windows igniting in sequence (`seg(p, a + i*step, a + i*step + w)`) · text tracking · counters · UI plane opacity & z.\n\n### Staggers (the \"sequential ignition\" trick)\n```ts\nwindows.forEach((w, i) => { const k = seg(f.p, 0.12 + i*0.012, 0.12 + i*0.012 + 0.05); w.intensity = ease.outExpo(k) * 3; });\n```\nSequential lighting up of N things over a short window is one of the most satisfying scroll moments ever. Use it.\n\n## 4 · DOM choreography\n\n`bindDom(overlay)` writes `--in`, `--out`, `--vis`, `--local` on every `[data-a][data-b]` element every frame (from the damped `f.p`, so DOM and camera are perfectly in sync).\n\n```html\n<div class=\"aura-copy aura-left\" data-a=\"0.22\" data-b=\"0.40\" data-fade=\"0.05\">…</div>\n```\n- Align DOM windows to the shot list: copy for the push-in lives in `[0.22–0.40]`.\n- **Don't fill every range.** At least one chapter has *no* text.\n- Use `--local` to scrub *inside* a chapter: counters (`calc(var(--local) * 100)`), giant type tracking/scale, progress bars, clip-path reveals (`clip-path: inset(0 calc((1 - var(--in)) * 100%) 0 0)`).\n- Split headlines per word/char only when you have budget — put per-char delay as `transition-delay` replaced with an index offset on `--in`: `opacity: clamp(0, calc(var(--in) * 6 - var(--i)), 1)` with `style={{ \"--i\": index }}`.\n- Interactive elements (CTA, nav) need `className=\"aura-hit\"` since the overlay is `pointer-events: none`.\n- Visibility hidden outside window is handled; don't also toggle display.\n\n## 5 · The text sandwich (signature technique)\n\nGoal: giant title sits **behind** the foreground subject but **in front of** the background — instant depth.\n\n**CSS-only (image plates):** three stacked layers in `.aura-overlay`, back→front:\n1. background plate (sky/water/mountains) — or the WebGL canvas\n2. `.aura-giant` (title, `data-a/data-b`)\n3. `<img class=\"aura-cutout\">` — transparent PNG of the subject (house, character, product) sized identically to the plate.\nGenerate the cut-out by background-removing the hero render (see asset pipeline §3) — or render it from three with a depth/ID mask.\n\n**Pure WebGL:** render the title as a plane (canvas texture or `troika-three-text`) at a z *between* two layers of `createLayerStack`. Move the plane with scroll for the parallax slip.\n\n**Reflection:** `.aura-reflect` (data-text) for DOM; in WebGL reflect with `createMirrorFloor` and let the real text mesh be reflected for free.\n\n## 6 · Transitions between worlds\n(See doctrine §1 for the toolbox.) Implementation recipes:\n- **Fog pass:** `fog.density = base + pulse(p, a, b, .03, .03) * 0.25` and swap visibility of two groups at the pulse peak.\n- **Occluder wipe:** a large dark mesh (pillar/leaf cluster) crosses the camera: animate its x from −8→8 across 0.05 of the playhead; swap sets mid-cross.\n- **Dive:** at the water line, flip `scene.background`, `fog.color`, grade shadows tint, and spawn bubbles (particle band with upward velocity).\n- **Flash:** increase `bloom.strength` 0.8 → 3 → 0.8 over 0.03 range and cross-fade ambient colour at peak.\n\n## 7 · Time-of-day / colour scripting\nLerp these together along `f.p` for a free emotional arc: `scene.background`, `fog.color` (always equal), `rig.key.color`, `rig.rim.color`, `renderer.toneMappingExposure`, `grade.shadows/highlights`, and the CSS `--aura-bg`/`--aura-accent` (so the DOM palette follows the world). Use `THREE.Color.lerpColors` with 3–4 palette stops: *dusk blue → violet → ember orange → night teal*.\n\n## 8 · Pointer & touch\n- Pointer parallax is built in (`parallax` in world units; 0.4–1.2). Reduce for macro shots.\n- Hover on the hero: raycast → set a `hoverK` damped value → drive emission, scale 1→1.04, cursor `data-cursor=\"view\"`.\n- Mobile: no pointer. Use **device tilt** (opt-in, iOS requires permission) or a slow idle drift (`sin(t*.2)`) so frames never feel frozen.\n- Touch scrolling is faster: shorten `scrollVh` by ~20% on `(pointer: coarse)` or lower damping λ.\n\n## 9 · Sound (optional, opt-in)\nAutoplay is forbidden. Provide a tiny \"Sound on\" toggle in the HUD. Use 3 stems (ambient bed, tonal swell tied to `velocity`, one-shot accents at chapter hits) with WebAudio gain nodes driven by `f.p`. Fade, never cut.\n\n## 10 · Choreography QA\n- Scrub slowly and quickly, forwards and backwards: nothing pops, nothing is left in a half state.\n- Pause at 10 random `p` values: every frame is composed (subject on a third, foreground present, no text collisions).\n- Count changes per 100vh: ≥ 3 distinct things move/change, but **only one** is the hero.\n- Check the end: reaching `p = 1` must feel *final* (a held, composed image), not a cliff.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/04-visual-engine.md":"# 04 · Visual Engine — light, matter, atmosphere, post, shaders\n\nThis is the cookbook for the 70% of \"AAA\" that lives in the render. All snippets target three.js r160+ (`starter/src/aura/*`).\n\n## 0 · The non-negotiable renderer setup\n```ts\nrenderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping;  // filmic roll-off — highlights bloom naturally instead of clipping\nrenderer.toneMappingExposure = 0.9–1.2;               // dark scenes: lower; the grade pass lifts contrast\n// output colour space is sRGB (default); textures with colour data: texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace\n// MSAA off when using EffectComposer (antialias:false) → rely on DPR ≥ 1.25 + (optionally) SMAAPass\nscene.environment = ctx.env;            // PMREM studio env → PBR reflections. ALWAYS.\nscene.environmentIntensity = 0.4–0.8;   // keep low in dark scenes; the key/rim carry the drama\nscene.background = fog.color = SAME_HEX // seamless horizon, DOM background must match\n```\n\n## 1 · Lighting recipes\nUse `lightRig()` as the base, then tune per mood.\n\n| Mood | Key | Rim | Fill | Practical | Fog |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cold luxe product | 0xffffff 2.5 top-left | 0x4fa3ff 6 behind | hemi 0x0a1020 | none | `FogExp2` 0.03 |\n| Dusk estate | 0xffb27a 3 low sun | 0x5a8bff 4 | hemi 0x1a2a4a | warm window PointLights 0xffc27a | 0.018 |\n| Sci-fi / void | 0x9fd0ff 1.5 | 0xff4fd8 6 | none | emissive rings | 0.04 |\n| Temple night | moon 0x8aa4ff 1.2 | 0xff3b3b 3 (red moon) | hemi 0x120a14 | lanterns 0xff9a4a (flicker) | 0.025 |\n| Food / warm | 0xffe0b8 4 | 0xffa060 3 | hemi 0x2a1810 | candle 0xff8a3c | 0.02 |\n\n- **Rim light = the secret.** Place a strong DirectionalLight *behind and above* the subject, coloured differently from the key. It paints a bright edge that separates the subject from the dark.\n- **Practicals:** every scene gets a *source in the world*. Add a small emissive mesh + a PointLight at the same position. Flicker: `intensity = base * (1 + .06*sin(t*7 + seed) + .04*sin(t*13.3))`.\n- **Shadows:** costly. Use ONE shadow-casting light (key), `shadow.mapSize 1024–2048`, tight `shadow.camera` bounds, `shadow.bias = -0.0004`, `normalBias 0.02`. For floating objects use a fake blob shadow (radial-gradient plane) — cheaper, softer.\n- **Light linking to scroll:** lerp the colours/intensities across the storyboard (see choreography §7).\n\n## 2 · Materials (see `materials.ts`)\n- **Rule of three:** every material needs **base colour variation**, **roughness variation**, and **one special response** (clearcoat / sheen / iridescence / emission / transmission).\n- **Roughness variation** is what makes surfaces read as real: feed `roughnessMap` with `noiseTexture()` and set `roughness: 1` as a multiplier; for brushed metal stretch the noise in one axis (`texture.repeat.set(8, 1)`).\n- **Normal/bump detail:** `bumpMap` + `bumpScale 0.5–2` from noise adds micro surface for free.\n- **Transmission (glass)** is expensive (re-renders the scene). Limit to 1–3 hero meshes, `transmissionResolutionScale` 0.5, disable below quality 0.5 → swap to a cheap `MeshPhysicalMaterial({ transparent, opacity:.25, roughness:0, envMapIntensity })`.\n- **Emission + bloom:** give emissive materials `emissiveIntensity` 2–6 with bloom threshold ~0.8; everything else stays below 0.8 so only the things that glow, glow.\n- **Fresnel rim in a shader** (cheap holographic/energy look):\n```glsl\nfloat f = pow(1. - max(dot(normalize(vNormal), normalize(vViewDir)), 0.), 3.);\ncol += uRim * f;\n```\n- **Gradient maps / toon-lit stylization** via `gradientTexture()` + `MeshToonMaterial.gradientMap` if the brief is illustrative.\n\n## 3 · Atmosphere stack (every scene gets all four)\n1. **Fog:** `FogExp2(bg, 0.015–0.05)`. Distant geometry should lose contrast. Animate density with scroll.\n2. **Particles (3 bands):** far dust (1–2px, thousands), mid motes (3–5px, hundreds), **near bokeh** (10–30px, <60, low alpha — this is the cheap DOF). Use `createParticles` three times with different `extent/size/speed`.\n3. **Light shafts / god-rays (cheap):** a few large additive planes with a vertical gradient texture, rotated to the light direction, `blending: AdditiveBlending, depthWrite:false, opacity .06–.15`, slow `uv` scroll via `texture.offset.y`. Fake but convincing; add noise to break uniformity.\n4. **Glow cards / halos:** additive sprites around practicals (radial gradient from `gradientTexture` or canvas), scale pulses with the flicker.\nOptional: **heat shimmer** (screen-space distortion in the grade pass using `sin(uv.y*40 + t)`), **volumetric fog layers** (stacked alpha-noise planes), **bird/lantern boids** (InstancedMesh) for scale and life.\n\n## 4 · Reflective floors & water\n- `createMirrorFloor({ color, fadeColor })`: Reflector + radial fade. Use for polished showrooms, wet streets, lake. Costs a second scene render → `quality > 0.5` only, texture size 0.5×.\n- **Water without a Reflector:** a large plane with `MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color: dark, roughness: .05, metalness: .9, envMapIntensity: 2 })` + animated normal map (two scrolling noise normals) gives believable lake reflections of the *environment* but not the scene — pair with a mirrored duplicate of key landmarks (scale.y = −1, alpha .35, blurred via lower-res sprite) for the \"estate reflected in lake\" look.\n- Always **fade reflections with distance** and darken them (< 0.4 intensity). Perfect mirrors look fake.\n\n## 5 · Post stack (engine order)\n`RenderPass → UnrealBloomPass → OutputPass (tone-map + sRGB) → Grade (custom ShaderPass)`\n- **Bloom:** `strength 0.5–0.9`, `radius 0.6–0.85`, `threshold 0.78–0.9`. If the whole image glows, raise the threshold.\n- **Grade pass (`scene.grade`):** `contrast 1.05–1.12`, `saturation 1.0–1.12`, `shadows` tint (cool: `[0,.015,.06]`), `highlights` tint (warm: `[.07,.035,0]`) → split-toning is how you get \"cinema colour\". `vignette .6–.8`, `grain .04–.06`, `aberration .03–.06` (edges only).\n- **Velocity effects:** the engine kicks aberration and FOV with scroll speed; keep subtle.\n- **Optional extras** (add as passes before OutputPass): `BokehPass` (true DOF, focus = distance to hero, aperture small), `SMAAPass`, 3D LUT (`LUTPass`) for a brand-specific grade, `GlitchPass` only for cyber briefs & only during one transition.\n- **God-rays via post:** the `postprocessing` library's `GodRaysEffect` with the sun mesh — only if the brief hinges on sun shafts.\n\n## 6 · Shader cookbook (copy/paste fragments)\n```glsl\n// value noise / fbm\nfloat h(vec2 p){ return fract(sin(dot(p, vec2(127.1,311.7))) * 43758.5453); }\nfloat n(vec2 p){ vec2 i=floor(p), f=fract(p); f=f*f*(3.-2.*f);\n  return mix(mix(h(i),h(i+vec2(1,0)),f.x), mix(h(i+vec2(0,1)),h(i+vec2(1,1)),f.x), f.y); }\nfloat fbm(vec2 p){ float v=0., a=.5; for(int i=0;i<5;i++){ v+=n(p)*a; p*=2.02; a*=.5; } return v; }\n\n// dissolve edge (reveal an object with a glowing burn line, drive uProg with scroll)\nfloat d = fbm(vUv*4.); float edge = smoothstep(uProg-.03, uProg, d) - smoothstep(uProg, uProg+.03, d);\nif (d < uProg) discard; col += uEdgeColor * edge * 4.;\n\n// iridescent thin film (cheap): hue shift by view angle\nvec3 irid(float c){ return .5 + .5*cos(6.28318*(c + vec3(0., .33, .67))); }\ncol *= mix(vec3(1.), irid(fresnel*1.5 + uTime*.05), .6);\n\n// animated displacement for water/silk (vertex)\np.z += (fbm(uv*3. + uTime*.05) - .5) * uAmp;\n\n// scan-line / holo\ncol *= .85 + .15*sin(gl_FragCoord.y*1.5 + uTime*8.);\n```\n- **GPU particles for text/models:** sample points on a mesh surface (`MeshSurfaceSampler`) → morph between two sampled point clouds (logo ↔ sphere ↔ product) driven by `seg(p, a, b)` in the vertex shader: `mix(posA, posB, ease(uMorph))` + noise-curl in the middle of the morph. This is *the* \"dissolving into particles\" moment.\n- **Instancing:** anything repeated (shards, windows, lanterns, trees, stars) → `InstancedMesh`; update matrices in `update()` only when needed.\n- **No allocation in `update`**: reuse `Vector3`, `Color`, `Object3D` dummies.\n\n## 7 · Image-based worlds (when you have generated art)\n- **Layer stack:** `createLayerStack` for cut-out PNGs with distinct z; scale each so it fills the frustum at its depth: `w = baseW * (camDist + |z|) / camDist`.\n- **Depth-displaced plate:** `createDepthPlane` with a depth map from the generated image: a single photo becomes a navigable diorama for ±12° of camera travel. Combine with fog and particles passing *between* camera and plate for a perfect \"real 3D\" feel.\n- **Sky domes:** equirect image on an inside-out sphere + `scene.environment` from the same image (`PMREMGenerator.fromEquirectangular`) → reflections match the sky = photoreal integration.\n- **Grade to unify:** generated images from different prompts differ in colour. Fix in post via the grade pass, or pre-process with the same LUT.\n\n## 8 · Quality tiers (what to turn off, in order)\n| Quality | Disable |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1.0 | — |\n| ≤ 0.8 | DPR 0.92×, near-bokeh band halves |\n| ≤ 0.6 | mirror floor off (use dark rough plane), transmission → opaque-ish |\n| ≤ 0.45 | bloom off |\n| ≤ 0.3 | far particles halved, shadows off |\nThe engine's governor lowers `quality` automatically; make your `update(f)` read `f.quality` and gate these.\n\n## 9 · Frequent failure modes\n- **Everything looks plastic** → no env map or exposure too high → set `scene.environment`, lower exposure, add roughness variation.\n- **Banding in dark gradients/fog** → add grain (engine does) and keep fog colour identical to background.\n- **Bloom on UI text** → text is DOM (not in the render); fine. If you render text in WebGL, `toneMapped:false` + keep its colour below threshold.\n- **Flicker on instanced shards** → `frustumCulled=false` for big instanced sets and moving InstancedMesh.\n- **Transparent sorting glitches** → set `renderOrder`, `depthWrite:false` on additive/transparent things, avoid overlapping big transparents.\n- **Memory growth** → dispose geometries/materials/textures on unmount (engine's `dispose()` does for the scene graph; dispose your own render targets).\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/05-asset-pipeline.md":"# 05 · Asset Pipeline — getting AAA-looking content when you can't model\n\nAn agent usually has **no 3D artist**. That is fine. There are five reliable sources, ordered by how often you should reach for them.\n\n## Decision tree\n```\nNeed a hero object/world?\n ├─ Abstract / sci-fi / architectural primitives / particles?   → A. PROCEDURAL (code)         [zero assets, fastest, always looks consistent]\n ├─ Photoreal place / landscape / building / interior?          → B. GENERATED IMAGE → 2.5D     [highest realism per minute]\n ├─ A specific product with a real model (GLB/CAD/photoscan)?   → C. GLB                         [exact product, brand-safe]\n ├─ Organic: food, liquid, fabric, smoke, people?               → D. VIDEO / FRAME SEQUENCE     [real footage scrubbed by scroll]\n └─ Need a quick 3D prop and have nothing?                      → E. AI-GENERATED 3D → clean up\n```\n\n## A · Procedural (default)\nBuild with primitives + great materials + great light (see `04-visual-engine.md` and `scene.example.ts`).\nStrong procedural ingredients: `IcosahedronGeometry(detail)`, `TorusGeometry` rings, `LatheGeometry` (vases, bottles, columns, chess pieces), `ExtrudeGeometry` from an SVG path (logos → 3D), `TubeGeometry` along a curve (light trails, cables), `InstancedMesh` for crowds/forests/cities, `ShaderMaterial` terrains (fbm height + fog), `MeshSurfaceSampler` particles. A city = instanced boxes with emissive windows + fog + a rim light. A mountain range = 4 fbm-displaced planes at different z with fog between.\n\n## B · Generated image → 2.5D world (the \"Ardmere / Kage\" look)\nThis is the highest-leverage technique available to an agent with image generation.\n\n### B1 · Generate the hero plate\nPrompt anatomy (fill every slot, it is what makes results look *shot*, not \"AI\"):\n```\n[SUBJECT, specific], [PLACE + TIME OF DAY + WEATHER], [CAMERA: lens mm, height, angle],\n[LIGHT: key/rim/practicals with colours], [ATMOSPHERE: mist, dust, god-rays],\n[MATERIALS: wet stone, brushed bronze…], [COLOR SCRIPT: teal-orange / amber vs cobalt],\ncinematic still, anamorphic, shallow depth of field in the foreground, 35mm film grain, 16:9, ultra-detailed, no text, no people unless specified\n```\nExample (estate): *\"Four modern alpine timber-and-stone lodges with glowing amber windows beside a still glacial lake at blue hour, wide 24mm shot from lake level, mirror-calm water reflecting the lit windows, pine forest and mountains fading into mist behind, soft cobalt sky, warm interior light spilling onto terraces, cinematic still, ultra-detailed, no text\"*.\nExample (temple): *\"Torii gate path leading up stone steps to a multi-tiered Japanese temple at night, huge blood-red moon behind the roof, drifting petals, paper lanterns glowing orange, volumetric mist, low camera at the base of the stairs, foreground grass silhouettes, cinematic, 35mm, no text\"*.\n\nGenerate **2–3 variants** and pick by: strong light direction, clear foreground/mid/background separation, a calm area for type, no baked text.\n\n### B2 · Make it 3D — pick one\n\n**Option 1 — Layer cut-outs (best control, most \"dimensional\")**\n1. Ask the image model to generate the *same scene split by depth* (\"sky and distant mountains only\", \"mid-ground lake and far trees only, transparent\", \"foreground grass and rocks only, transparent background\") **or** cut the single plate with a segmentation/BG-removal model (e.g. rembg / SAM / Photoshop Select Subject) into 4–7 PNGs: `sky.png, far.png, mid.png, subject.png, near.png, fx.png`.\n2. Fill the holes behind cut-outs with generative fill/inpainting so parallax doesn't reveal gaps (only needed if camera travel is large; for ±8° it rarely matters).\n3. Load with `createLayerStack([{url, z, width, height, fog}])`. Scale layers for their depth. Far layers get `fog:true`, near layers don't.\n\n**Option 2 — Depth-displaced plane (fastest, single image)**\n1. Run a monocular depth model (Depth Anything V2, MiDaS, Marigold; or any \"depth map\" tool) → greyscale PNG, white = near.\n2. `await createDepthPlane({ colorUrl, depthUrl, width, height, depthScale: 1.6 })`.\n3. Keep camera travel within ~±12° yaw / ±1.5 units; beyond that stretching appears. Hide edges with fog + vignette + particles.\n4. Combine with Option 1: displace the *mid* plane, keep sky/near as flat layers.\n\n**Option 3 — Hybrid (best):** real 3D foreground (instanced grass/rocks/branches or procedural silhouettes) + displaced plate mid + layered sky. Foreground in true 3D is what makes the parallax feel real.\n\n### B3 · Make the type \"sandwich\"\nCut the hero subject (house/character) as a transparent PNG; place the giant title **behind** it (see choreography §5). One BG-remove call + one CSS layer = the signature look.\n\n### B4 · Light the plate\nEven flat images benefit from: animated glow sprites on windows (additive, flicker), mist planes drifting across, particles between camera and plate, a slow luminance sweep (a gradient plane with `mix-blend-mode: soft-light`), and the grade pass. This makes a still *breathe*.\n\n## C · GLB models\n**Sources (check licences; credit if CC-BY):** Sketchfab (filter *Downloadable, CC0/CC-BY, PBR*), Poly Haven (CC0 HDRIs/models/textures), Quaternius (CC0 low-poly), Kenney (CC0), Smithsonian 3D, manufacturer CAD/GLB, Blender Market (paid). For product work, ask the user for the real GLB/CAD (STEP → GLB via Blender/FreeCAD/Fusion).\n\n**Optimise before shipping (mandatory):**\n```bash\nnpx @gltf-transform/cli optimize in.glb out.glb --compress meshopt --texture-compress webp --texture-size 2048\n# or for max compression with KTX2/Basis:  --texture-compress ktx2  (needs toktx)\n```\nTargets: hero ≤ 3 MB, props ≤ 500 KB each, textures ≤ 2048², ≤ 150k tris hero / ≤ 20k props. Load via `loadGLB` + `fitModel` + `prepModel` (assets.ts). Always re-material the hero with the recipes in `materials.ts` if the stock look is flat; keep the original maps.\n\n**Make it look expensive:** set `envMapIntensity`, add a rim light behind, add a contact shadow/floor reflection, add micro-motion (slow hover bob, parts that float apart in the exploded chapter).\n\n**Exploded view:** traverse the model, group meshes by name/part, store `origin` & `exploded = origin + dir * dist`; per frame `mesh.position.lerpVectors(origin, exploded, ease(seg(p,a,b)))`; label each with a DOM mono tag tracked via `vector.project(camera)`.\n\n## D · Video / frame sequences (organic stuff)\nFor liquids, fire, smoke, fabric, people, food, cars: real or AI-generated *video* beats CG.\n1. Generate/film a 3–8s clip (AI video model, stock footage, product shoot). Keep camera locked, subject centred, background simple.\n2. Convert to frames: `ffmpeg -i clip.mp4 -vf \"fps=30,scale=1600:-2\" -q:v 3 f_%04d.webp` (or `-c:v libwebp -quality 75`). 120–240 frames is plenty; ~50–150 KB each → lazy-load in chunks.\n3. Scrub: draw frame `index = floor(seg(p,a,b) * (N-1))` onto a `<canvas>` or a `THREE.CanvasTexture`/`VideoTexture` plane (preload next 20 frames). Or `<video>` with `currentTime = duration * k` (needs `preload=\"auto\"`, keyframe-heavy encode: `-g 1`) — canvas frames are more reliable.\n4. Composite in 3D: put the frame plane in the scene with fog/particles in front. Alpha-matte (WebM VP9 alpha or per-frame WebP with alpha) lets it sit *in* the world.\n\n## E · AI-generated 3D\nImage-to-3D services (Meshy, Tripo, Rodin/Hyper3D, Hunyuan3D, Luma Genie…) → GLB. Good for props, creatures, statues, stylised hero objects. Always: retopology check (tri count), flip normals if inverted, re-material, optimise with gltf-transform, set a rim light. Don't use for mechanical products where accuracy matters.\n\n## F · Textures, HDRIs, sky\n- **HDRI:** Poly Haven (CC0). Use *only for `scene.environment`* (and optionally the sky). Keep ≤ 2k, `.hdr` → `RGBELoader` → `PMREMGenerator.fromEquirectangular`. A **night/dusk HDRI** gives immediate photoreal reflections.\n- **PBR textures:** Poly Haven / ambientCG (CC0). Albedo sRGB, others linear. Tile + roughness variation.\n- **Generated textures:** ask the image model for \"seamless tileable [material] texture, top-down, even lighting\" then make a normal map via a normal-map generator.\n\n## G · Fonts & icons\nSelf-host variable fonts (`@fontsource-variable/*`). Display: Space Grotesk, Unbounded, Syne, Instrument Serif, Bricolage Grotesque, Fraunces, Anton (condensed giant), Clash/Cabinet (Fontshare, free). Mono: JetBrains Mono, IBM Plex Mono, Geist Mono. Icons: avoid; use typography and thin lines.\n\n## H · Manifest (write `ASSETS.md`)\n```md\n| id | type | source / prompt | file | size | role | loads |\n|----|------|-----------------|------|------|------|-------|\n| plate | image | gen prompt #1 | /assets/plate.webp | 420 KB | hero background (depth-displaced) | blocking |\n| depth | image | depth-anything(plate) | /assets/plate-depth.webp | 160 KB | displacement | blocking |\n| cut | image | bg-removed subject | /assets/subject.png | 380 KB | text sandwich | blocking |\n| hdri | hdr | polyhaven dusk 1k | /assets/env.hdr | 1.4 MB | environment | after intro |\n```\n- **First-load budget ≤ 12 MB**, ≤ 4 MB for *blocking* assets (the loader covers them). Stream everything else after the first chapter is visible.\n- WebP/AVIF for images, meshopt GLB, no PNG over 1 MB (convert to WebP with alpha), no video over 6 MB.\n\n## I · Licensing & honesty\nCredit CC-BY assets in the footer/readme. Never ship watermarked/stock-licence images without clearance. State in the final message which assets are generated and which prompts produced them so the user can regenerate.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/06-typography-and-ui.md":"# 06 · Typography & UI — the layer people actually read\n\nIn an immersive site the DOM is a thin, precise, *expensive-looking* skin over the 3D. Its job: orient, tease, convert — then get out of the way.\n\n## 1 · Type system (two fonts, six roles)\n| Role | Spec | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **GIANT** (hero word) | 18–30vw, weight 600–800, line-height .8, tracking −.03 → +.02em (scrubbed) | `ARDMERE`, `KAGE`, `OBSIDIAN` |\n| **Headline** | clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4.4rem), 600, tracking −.045em, lh .96 | chapter titles (≤ 6 words) |\n| **Body** | 14–17px, lh 1.6, 55–60% opacity | ≤ 14 words |\n| **Label (mono)** | 10–11px, tracking .22–.3em, uppercase | `CHAPTER 03 · DETAIL`, coordinates, specs |\n| **Numerals** | tabular, mono or display | counters, prices, dates |\n| **CTA** | 13–14px, 600, pill or underline | one per screen at most |\n\nPairings that work: *Space Grotesk + JetBrains Mono* (tech/product), *Instrument Serif (italic accents) + Geist Mono* (luxury/architecture), *Anton or Bebas + Inter* (sport, games, speed), *Fraunces + IBM Plex Mono* (craft, hospitality), *Unbounded + DM Mono* (web3, futuristic), *Syne + Space Mono* (art/portfolio).\n**Scale contrast is the secret**: a 300px word beside a 10px label. Never use mid-sized (24–48px) type as your hero.\n\n## 2 · Giant type behaviours\n- **Tracking scrub:** `letter-spacing: calc(.02em - var(--local) * .06em)` as you push in.\n- **Scale scrub:** `scale: calc(1 + var(--local) * .18)`.\n- **Masked reveal:** `clip-path: inset(0 0 calc((1 - var(--in)) * 100%) 0)` (text rises from a baseline).\n- **Per-letter stagger:** span per char with `--i`; `opacity: clamp(0, calc(var(--in) * 8 - var(--i)), 1); translate: 0 calc((1 - clamp(0, calc(var(--in)*8 - var(--i)), 1)) * 40%)`.\n- **Behind-subject sandwich** (see choreography §5), **reflection** (`.aura-reflect`), **outline→fill** on scroll (`-webkit-text-stroke` to `color`), **lit by sweep** (a moving `linear-gradient` background-clip text driven by `--local`).\n- **Crop on purpose:** hero word wider than the viewport, letters entering from the sides as you scroll.\n- **Never** drop-shadow giant type; use blend modes / lighting from the scene instead.\n\n## 3 · Layout rules\n- Safe margins `--aura-pad` (clamp 20→64px). Align everything to a 12-col invisible grid; put copy on thirds, not centre — except the final CTA & statements.\n- **One block of copy on screen at a time**, max. Copy never covers the hero; leave negative space on the *opposite* side from the subject.\n- **Contrast over imagery:** add a soft radial or linear dark scrim behind copy (`radial-gradient(60% 50% at 20% 80%, rgb(0 0 0 / .55), transparent)`), never a box.\n- Corners are real estate: top-left logo, top-right nav, bottom-left chapter label, bottom-right scroll/sound, bottom-centre progress.\n\n## 4 · Navigation\n- A **minimal glass pill** (4–5 items max) or just logo + \"Menu\" + one CTA. Items jump the playhead to a chapter: `window.scrollTo({ top: trackTop + chapterP * (trackHeight - innerHeight), behavior: \"smooth\" })` — the engine then dollies there like a drone (and the velocity FOV-kick makes it look incredible).\n- A **chapter index** (`01 02 03 04`) on the side, current one lit; clicking jumps.\n- Always keep a real `<nav>` + `<a>` semantics. Provide a **skip-intro** link.\n- Hide nav on scroll-down fast, show on scroll-up if you must, but don't thrash.\n\n## 5 · Cursor & micro-interactions\n- Custom cursor: 8px dot + 40px ring with `lerp` follow, ring grows on `[data-cursor]` (view / drag / play). Disable on touch and for reduced motion.\n- **Magnetic CTA:** translate toward the pointer within 80px radius (strength .25), spring back with `cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1)`.\n- Hover sound-less feedback: underline wipe, letter shuffle (scramble) — use sparingly, only on nav/CTA.\n- Scroll cue: a 1px line with a travelling dot, vanishes after first movement.\n\n## 6 · Preloader (chapter 0)\nIt's the first frame people see; make it deliberate: giant counter `000→100` tied to real asset progress (`THREE.DefaultLoadingManager`), project name in mono, then a **wipe up** (the starter does this). Min display 900ms. Never a spinner. If assets are heavy, show a subtle poster behind.\n\n## 7 · Final frame & conversion\n- The last 10% of scroll is a poster with **one** CTA (large, pill or underlined giant text link) + a tiny secondary row (email, socials, legal).\n- Provide the \"real site\" *below the experience*: after the track ends, a normal footer / contact / pricing area styled in the same world (same bg colour, same type), reachable by continuing to scroll. Search engines & users who want facts get them there.\n- Secondary pages (pricing, work, contact, login) reuse the world: a **frozen**, lower-cost version of the scene as the background (static camera, slow drift) — don't use a different visual language.\n\n## 8 · Copywriting for cinematic sites\n- Voice: confident, sensory, specific. Present tense. Fragments are fine.\n- Formula for a chapter: **[eyebrow label] + [3–6 word statement] + [≤14 word proof]**.\n- Replace adjectives with nouns: not \"stunning luxury residences\" → \"Four homes. One lake. No neighbours but the mountain.\"\n- Numbers are poetry: \"2,740 m\", \"−18 dB\", \"1 of 12\", \"43 minutes from Zürich\".\n- Never: \"Welcome to\", \"Innovative solutions\", \"Seamless experience\", \"Cutting-edge\", \"Unlock\".\n- One CTA verb that matches the emotion: *Enter, Reserve, Begin, Descend, Claim, Step inside.*\n\n## 9 · Colour system\n- 1 dominant hue (60–70% of pixels, usually the dark), 1 accent (≤ 5%), warm/cool contrast pair for light, neutrals for type.\n- DOM backgrounds = fog colour exactly. Text `#eef3f7` (not pure white) + `rgb(…/.58)` for secondary.\n- Accent appears in: labels, HUD fill, CTA, emissive things in the world — repeating it ties DOM and 3D together.\n- Keep a `:root` variable set that the scene can *animate* (set `document.documentElement.style.setProperty(\"--aura-accent\", …)` from `update`) so the UI follows the time-of-day arc.\n\n## 10 · Accessibility without killing the vibe\n- Real headings in DOM order (the overlay's DOM order = reading order). One `<h1>`.\n- Overlay chapters hidden by `visibility:hidden` are out of the a11y tree unless active — also provide an `sr-only` full text transcript of all chapters.\n- Contrast ≥ 4.5:1 for body text on scrims. Focus rings visible on CTAs (`outline: 1px solid var(--aura-accent); outline-offset: 6px`).\n- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: no velocity effects, no parallax, instant playhead (engine does), no blur transitions (CSS does).\n- All interactive elements keyboard reachable. Scroll must work with keyboard (Space/PgDn/arrows — native scroll gives this for free; another reason not to hijack).\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/07-performance-and-resilience.md":"# 07 · Performance & resilience — the experience must survive the real world\n\nA gorgeous site at 22fps is a failure. A gorgeous site that is blank on a 2019 Android is a failure. Design for both.\n\n## 1 · Budgets\n| Metric | Desktop target | Mobile target |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Frame rate | 60fps (≥ 55 p95) | ≥ 40fps |\n| Blocking first load | ≤ 4 MB | ≤ 3 MB |\n| Total first-load assets | ≤ 12 MB | ≤ 8 MB |\n| Draw calls | < 150 | < 80 |\n| Triangles on screen | < 1.5M | < 400k |\n| JS (gzip) | < 350 KB + three | same |\n| DPR | ≤ 1.75 | ≤ 1.25 |\n| GPU memory | < 400 MB | < 200 MB |\n| LCP | < 2.5s (loader/poster counts) | < 3.5s |\n\n## 2 · The adaptive quality governor (built into the engine)\n`mountAura` measures average frame time after a 90-frame warm-up. If it is slower than ~26ms for 45 frames, `quality` steps down by 0.2 (min 0.2), reducing pixel ratio and disabling bloom below 0.45. **It never steps back up** (prevents flapping). Your scene should read `f.quality` and gate:\n```ts\nfloor.setEnabled(f.quality > 0.5);\nglassMesh.material = f.quality > 0.6 ? glassPhysical : glassCheap;\ndustNear.points.visible = f.quality > 0.3;\n```\nAlso decide the *initial* quality: mobile starts at 0.75; consider starting low if `navigator.hardwareConcurrency <= 4` or `navigator.deviceMemory <= 4`.\n\n## 3 · CPU-side rules\n- **No allocations in `update`** — reuse vectors/colors/dummies. GC pauses = micro-stutter in scroll.\n- Use `InstancedMesh` for repeats; `BufferGeometry` merged for static clutter (`mergeGeometries`).\n- Update only what changed: don't set `matrixWorldNeedsUpdate` for static meshes; set `matrixAutoUpdate = false` on static objects after placing them (`obj.updateMatrix()` once).\n- Animate in shaders (uniforms) rather than per-vertex in JS.\n- Throttle DOM writes: `bindDom` writes CSS variables only — never `getBoundingClientRect` inside the frame loop except the single `trackProgress` read.\n- Keep the rAF loop paused when the tab is hidden or the canvas is off-screen (the engine does both).\n\n## 4 · GPU-side rules\n- Fill-rate is the usual killer: large transparent overlapping layers, full-screen post, high DPR, transmission, reflectors. Reduce **pixels** first (DPR), geometry second.\n- One shadow-casting light, small maps. Prefer baked/fake shadows.\n- Textures: ≤ 2048², compressed (KTX2/Basis ideal, WebP acceptable), mipmaps on, `anisotropy` ≤ 8. Power-of-two helps mobile.\n- Avoid `MeshPhysicalMaterial` with *all* features on every mesh; reserve clearcoat/transmission/iridescence for 1–5 hero meshes.\n- Bloom is expensive at full-res: the engine's bloom runs at half-res internally; disable at low quality.\n- Reflector: texture ≈ 0.35–0.5 of screen; skip below quality 0.5.\n- Overdraw in particles: keep point sizes clamped (shader clamps to 64px) and counts moderate.\n\n## 5 · Loading strategy\n1. Paint the **loader** instantly (it's DOM/CSS; included in the starter).\n2. Load only *chapter 1–2* assets as blocking. Everything else after `onReady` via `requestIdleCallback`.\n3. Use `THREE.DefaultLoadingManager` so the counter reflects real progress.\n4. `<link rel=\"preload\" as=\"image\" href=\"/assets/poster.webp\">` for the poster; `fetchpriority=\"high\"`.\n5. Compile shaders before reveal: render one frame during the loader (`renderer.compile(scene, camera)` — the engine renders the first frame before the loader wipes).\n6. Serve assets with `Cache-Control: immutable`, gzip/brotli; host heavy files on a CDN.\n\n## 6 · Responsive design\n- **Portrait phones:** the engine widens FOV (+18°) when aspect < 0.8. Re-check framing: the subject must fit horizontally; move text to the bottom third; hide secondary HUD.\n- Provide a **mobile shot list** if the desktop rail relies on horizontal composition: `const shots = isMobile ? mobileShots : desktopShots`.\n- Reduce counts: particles ×0.35, shards ×0.5, no mirror floor, smaller textures (`ctx.mobile`).\n- Touch scroll is momentum-heavy: if the playhead overshoots, raise damping λ on `(pointer: coarse)`.\n- Test 360×640, 390×844, 768×1024, 1440×900, 1920×1080, 2560×1080 (ultrawide: clamp the FOV so nothing important is cropped).\n- `100svh` not `100vh` for the sticky stage (the starter uses both with fallback) to avoid mobile URL-bar jumps.\n\n## 7 · Fallbacks (non-negotiable)\n| Condition | Behaviour |\n| --- | --- |\n| No WebGL / context creation fails | `AuraExperience` shows the `poster` image and the chapters as static stacked sections (render the chapters list in a plain `<main>` as well). |\n| `prefers-reduced-motion` | Playhead is near-instant, no velocity FOV/aberration, no pointer parallax, no blur transitions, reduced grain; scene still scrubs. |\n| Context lost (`webglcontextlost`) | `preventDefault()`, show poster, attempt re-mount on `webglcontextrestored`. |\n| Very low-end (quality hits 0.2 within 3s) | Swap to a **lite** mode: static camera + CSS parallax over the poster layers. |\n| JS disabled | `<noscript>` with the poster + text content + CTA. |\n| Save-Data / slow network | Skip video/frame sequences; load 1k textures; no HDRI. |\n\n## 8 · Memory & lifecycle\n- `dispose()` on unmount (engine disposes geometry, material, textures, composer, renderer; forces context loss). In React StrictMode dev the effect double-runs: the starter handles it via the `dead` flag.\n- Dispose your own `WebGLRenderTarget`s, `Reflector`s (`mirror.getRenderTarget().dispose()`), and loaders' caches (`THREE.Cache.clear()`).\n- Don't create new materials per frame or per particle.\n\n## 9 · SEO & sharing\n- The immersive page needs real content for crawlers: `<title>`, meta description, one `<h1>` (the hero word as visually-hidden text or the real element), OG image (a 1200×630 screenshot of the poster frame), structured data if relevant.\n- Put the factual content (address, pricing, specs, contact) in real HTML after the experience.\n- Provide `/` (experience) and, if useful, `/lite` (simplified, no WebGL) for sharing in contexts that block WebGL (some in-app browsers).\n\n## 10 · Measuring\n```js\n// quick FPS probe in the console / Playwright\nlet n=0,t0=performance.now(); const f=()=>{n++; performance.now()-t0<5000?requestAnimationFrame(f):console.log('fps',n/5)}; f();\n// then scroll programmatically:  window.scrollTo({top: document.body.scrollHeight, behavior:'smooth'})\n```\nUse Chrome DevTools → Performance (check long frames, GC), Rendering → FPS meter, `renderer.info.render.calls / triangles` and `renderer.info.memory` printed from a debug overlay (`?debug`).\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/08-qa-and-polish.md":"# 08 · QA & Polish — the part that makes it shippable (and Awwwards-able)\n\nThe agent's biggest handicap is that it can't *see* its work unless it makes itself look. **Make yourself look.** Every phase ends with screenshots.\n\n## 1 · The audit loop (`scripts/audit.mjs`) — run it, then LOOK\n\nThe agent's biggest handicap is that it can't *see* its work unless it makes itself look. **Make yourself look — and let a machine catch the mechanical failures first.**\n\n```bash\nnpm i -D playwright pngjs && npx playwright install chromium\nnpm run dev &                                     # or serve a build\nnode scripts/audit.mjs http://localhost:5173 --out shots      # add --gpu on a real machine for true FPS\n```\n\nIt is **deterministic**: the engine exposes `window.__aura.seek(p)` (and `?p=0.37` in the URL) which locks the playhead and settles instantly — identical input, identical frame. No scroll timing, no waiting for damping. In your own debugging use `__aura.info()` to read the live camera position, look target, fov, draw calls and triangles while you tune shots.\n\nWhat it measures at 14 stops × 3 viewports (a fresh browser per viewport):\n| Check | Catches |\n| --- | --- |\n| luma / contrast σ / clipped % / edge energy | black frames, washed-out frames, flat frames, blown bloom, empty frames |\n| frame-to-frame Δ | **dead zones** (scroll that does nothing) and **jump cuts** (a cut where there should be a glide) |\n| DOM chapters at every stop | copy colliding with copy, copy leaving the viewport (measured on the *text*, not the box), **empty first frame**, **empty last frame**, **no CTA in the last frame** |\n| shot-list lint | not covering 0→1, no HOLD, no wide/close alternation, teleporting cuts, too few shots |\n| chapters | late opening, early closing, long silences, headlines > 14 words |\n| budgets | > 160 draw calls, > 1.8 M triangles |\n| resilience | page with WebGL disabled (must not crash or hang on the loader), reduced-motion load |\n\nIt writes `shots/REPORT.md`, `report.json`, `shots/contact-desktop.png|mobile|wide` and exits non-zero on any **BLOCKER**. It also prints an *automated* score out of 80 (atmosphere 25 · unbroken 20 · rhythm 20 · craft 15). **The remaining 20 points — Authorship — and the real taste judgement are yours.**\n\n> **Gate:** 0 blockers → open `contact-desktop.png` and `contact-mobile.png` with your image tool → critique (§2) → fix → re-run. Do this **at least three times** (blockout, light/material, polish).\n>\n> A green audit proves the film is *mechanically sound*, never that it is *beautiful*. Both reference worlds scored 80/80 only after a real critique pass fixed things the numbers could not see (square stars, water-looking dunes, a hot corona).\n\nSoftware GL (SwiftShader) is slow but pixel-accurate for composition; ignore its FPS.\nIf every screenshot is black: the WebGL context failed (flags / poster fallback), the loader is still up, or exposure/fog is wrong — the audit reports `dark` at p=0 as a BLOCKER for this reason.\n\n## 2 · Critique protocol (be a harsh art director)\nFor each screenshot, answer in one line each:\n1. **Poster test:** would this frame make a good wallpaper? If not → why (flat light? clutter? empty?).\n2. **Depth:** can I name 5 distinct depth planes?\n3. **Light:** where is the key/rim? Is there a colour contrast? Is anything blown out or crushed *unintentionally*?\n4. **Type:** is it placed on a third, legible, interacting with the world? Any collision with the subject's focal point?\n5. **Composition:** subject on a third line? negative space for copy? horizon not at dead centre?\n6. **Continuity:** does it connect to the previous/next frame?\nWrite issues as a numbered list with the *specific fix* (\"rim light too weak → DirectionalLight 3 → 6, colour 0x5ea2ff\"), apply, re-shoot, repeat.\n\n## 3 · Technical test matrix\n| Test | Pass criterion |\n| --- | --- |\n| Scrub fwd/back/fast/slow | no pops, no stuck states, copy never overlaps |\n| Jump (click nav → far chapter) | smooth dolly, no teleport, no blank frames |\n| Resize (drag window, rotate device) | no stretch, camera aspect correct, HUD ok |\n| Hidden tab 30s → return | no time-jump glitches (dt clamped), no memory spike |\n| Reduced motion on | works, calm, complete |\n| WebGL off (`--disable-gpu` or block context) | poster + readable content + CTA |\n| Slow CPU (DevTools 6× throttle) | quality governor drops DPR; still interactive |\n| Mobile touch | momentum scroll OK; text legible; CTA tappable (44px) |\n| Console | zero errors, zero warnings from your code |\n| Lighthouse (desktop) | Performance ≥ 70 (WebGL sites rarely hit 90), A11y ≥ 95, SEO ≥ 95 |\n| Memory | `renderer.info.memory.geometries/textures` stable after 3 full scrolls |\n\n## 4 · The Aura Score (100 points) — ship at ≥ 85\nScore honestly; a 90 is rare.\n\n| # | Category | Pts | 0 = | Full marks = |\n|---|----------|-----|-----|--------------|\n| **A** | **Atmosphere** | **25** | | |\n| A1 | Lighting design (key/rim/practical, colour contrast) | 8 | default light | designed, moving, coloured |\n| A2 | Depth layers & parallax (≥5 planes) | 6 | flat | strong occlusion + fg/bg |\n| A3 | Materials (variation, env, special response) | 5 | plastic | tactile, believable |\n| A4 | Air (fog, particles bands, shafts) | 6 | void | alive atmosphere |\n| **U** | **Unbroken** | **20** | | |\n| U1 | Single continuous camera, spatial transitions | 8 | sections | seamless |\n| U2 | Scroll mapping (scrub, reversible, damped, no hijack) | 6 | triggers | pure playhead |\n| U3 | World coherence (one idea, no style drift) | 6 | collage | authored |\n| **R** | **Rhythm** | **20** | | |\n| R1 | Pacing (wide/close alternation, hold, crescendo) | 8 | conveyor belt | edited like film |\n| R2 | Scroll length & beats (earned, 500–1200vh) | 4 | too short/long | right |\n| R3 | Final frame & CTA | 4 | cliff | poster + single CTA |\n| R4 | Loader / entrance | 4 | spinner | deliberate chapter 0 |\n| **A** | **Authorship** | **20** | | |\n| A5 | Concept specificity (fails competitor-swap test?) | 8 | generic | unmistakable |\n| A6 | Typography as architecture | 8 | PowerPoint | interacts with scene |\n| A7 | Copy quality | 4 | filler | sensory & minimal |\n| **P** | **Production** | **15** | | |\n| P1 | Perf (fps, DPR governor, budgets) | 6 | stutters | 60fps |\n| P2 | Responsive & mobile design | 4 | broken | designed variant |\n| P3 | Resilience & a11y (fallbacks, reduced motion, SEO) | 5 | none | complete |\n\nOutput the filled table in your final message. For any category < 70% of its points, write the fix you applied.\n\n## 5 · Polish passes (do all three)\n\n### Pass A — Motion\n- Are entrances **eased** (outExpo/outCubic), exits faster than entrances?\n- Do holds last long enough to read the copy at 3 words/sec + 0.5s?\n- Add ± ambient motion everywhere (breathing hero 0.05u, drifting dust, flicker) so frozen frames feel alive.\n- Velocity effects: FOV kick 3–7°, aberration kick small. If it makes you queasy, halve it.\n- Check ease-in/out seams between shots — if you feel a \"bump\", add an intermediate shot or change the `ease`.\n\n### Pass B — Image\n- Crush blacks slightly, protect highlights (ACES + grade contrast 1.06–1.1).\n- Split-tone: cool shadows / warm highlights (or the brief's pair).\n- Add a **hero highlight**: a specular sweep, glow, or rim brightest where the eye should land.\n- Unify colour: generated images + procedural geometry must share a palette → adjust grade, fog colour, tint far layers.\n- Kill any frame with: blown whites, banding gradients, texture stretching, z-fighting, visible plane edges.\n- Vignette to focus; grain to unify.\n\n### Pass C — Details\n- Favicon, OG image (screenshot of the best frame), `<title>`, theme-color = background.\n- Cursor, magnetic CTA, hover states, focus rings, selection colour = accent.\n- Scroll cue that disappears; chapter counter; a quiet \"Sound\" toggle if audio exists.\n- 404 page in-world. Footer that matches.\n- Console clean. No layout shift (the stage is `100svh`, fonts preloaded with `font-display: swap` + `size-adjust`).\n- Delete dead code, unused assets, debug overlays (`?debug` can stay).\n\n## 6 · Fix-it table (symptom → first thing to try)\n| Symptom | First fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| Frame is boring | move subject off-centre; add fg silhouette; narrow the lens; add rim light |\n| Subject doesn't pop | raise rim light intensity ×2 + colour contrast; lower env intensity; darker bg |\n| Scene feels empty | add scale cues & far dust band; lower fog density slightly; add distant lit windows/lanterns |\n| Copy hard to read | scrim gradient behind; reduce simultaneous elements; increase size, not weight |\n| Scroll feels \"mushy\" | raise damping λ 3.2 → 4.5; shorten `scrollVh` |\n| Scroll feels \"jerky/snappy\" | lower λ → 2.6; add `ease:\"inOut\"` into holds only |\n| Camera clips through geometry | add an intermediate shot; increase distance; set `camera.near` 0.3 |\n| Banding in fog/sky | add grain, ensure bg == fog colour, dither gradient textures |\n| Mobile too heavy | `ctx.mobile` branch: counts ×0.4, no reflector, DPR ≤ 1.1 |\n| It \"looks AI\" | stop centring; add a cropped giant word, an imperfection (asymmetry, light leak, grain), and a specific real-world detail |\n\n## 7 · Definition of Done\n- [ ] `AURA_BRIEF.md`, `STORYBOARD.md`, `ASSETS.md` in the repo.\n- [ ] 12 screenshots × 3 viewports reviewed; all issues closed.\n- [ ] Aura Score ≥ 85 posted.\n- [ ] `npm run build` passes; production build tested (not just dev).\n- [ ] FPS ≥ 55 desktop on a mid GPU; governor verified.\n- [ ] Fallbacks verified (no WebGL, reduced motion, mobile).\n- [ ] README explains: run, build, swap assets, edit shots, change copy.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/09-prompt-pack.md":"# 09 · Prompt Pack — kickoff prompts for any agent\n\nThe skill works best when the human gives it a *subject* and a *feeling*. These prompts are ready to paste. Replace `[BRACKETS]`.\nIf your agent supports skills (Claude Code, Claude.ai Skills, Cursor Rules, Codex AGENTS.md), install the `aura-3d-web` folder first and use the short prompt. If it doesn't, paste the **Master prompt** — it is a compressed form of the whole skill.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Short prompt (skill installed)\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Build a scroll-driven immersive 3D website for [SUBJECT].\nAudience: [WHO]. They should feel [ONE EMOTION]. Final action: [CTA].\nBrand assets: [logo/colors/copy/images or \"none — art-direct it yourself\"].\nDon't ask me questions unless something is truly blocking — make decisions, state your assumptions, build it, run `node scripts/audit.mjs <url>` until there are 0 blockers, open the contact sheets and critique them frame by frame (≥3 loops), score it honestly, polish it.\n```\n\n## 🧠 Master prompt (no skill support — paste this)\n```\nYou are a creative director, cinematographer and technical director. Build [SUBJECT] as a scroll-driven, immersive 3D website that feels like a high-budget game trailer / Awwwards Site of the Year — not a normal website. I (the visitor) drive a single continuous camera with my scroll wheel.\n\nNON-NEGOTIABLES\n1. ONE idea / ONE world / ONE continuous camera. No visible sections. Transitions are spatial (fly through, occluder wipe, fog pass, dive, scale-jump).\n2. Scroll is the PLAYHEAD, not a trigger: scroll progress (0..1, critically damped) drives camera position/look-at/FOV/roll via a keyframed shot list (Catmull-Rom), plus light, fog, particles, hero transforms, and text. Fully scrubbed & reversible. Never hijack scroll.\n3. Depth: ≥5 planes every frame (fg occluder, subject, mid, bg, atmosphere). Parallax + fog + 3 particle bands (far dust, mid motes, near bokeh).\n4. Designed light: key + coloured rim + fill + a practical light in-world. Dark-field, high bloom threshold (≥0.8), ACES tone-mapping, split-toned grade, film grain, vignette, subtle chromatic aberration. scene.environment always set. Background colour == fog colour.\n5. Materials with variation: roughness maps, clearcoat/sheen/iridescence/emissive. Nothing default.\n6. Typography as architecture: GIANT display type (18–30vw) that the scene interacts with (behind the subject, reflected, scrubbed tracking), tiny mono HUD labels, two fonts max, ≤12 words per beat.\n7. Rhythm: alternate wide/close shots, vary lenses (24°/38°/52°/60°), at least one HOLD, a crescendo, a composed final frame with ONE CTA. Scroll length 600–1000vh.\n8. Performance: three.js + EffectComposer, DPR cap, instancing, adaptive-quality governor, mobile variant, reduced-motion + no-WebGL fallbacks, 60fps.\n9. Deliver a stunning preloader (giant counter + title wipe).\n\nPROCESS (do in order, write files): AURA_BRIEF.md (world metaphor, hero, emotional arc, palette hex, type, lens language, light, atmosphere, never-list) → STORYBOARD.md (table: chapter | scroll range | shot+lens | world change | copy ≤12 words | type treatment | transition) → ASSETS.md (procedural vs generated image layers vs GLB; ≤12MB) → scaffold (Vite+React+TS+three) → blockout shots first → light → materials/hero → atmosphere → typography+DOM choreography with data-a/data-b windows bound to the same playhead → post/grade → interaction → conversion → 3 polish passes.\nVERIFY: expose window.__aura.seek(p) from the engine so every frame is reproducible; use Playwright to screenshot 14 scroll positions on desktop+mobile (measure luminance/contrast/clipping, detect text collisions, require copy + a CTA in the first and last frame), inspect them like a harsh art director, fix, repeat. Score yourself out of 100 (Atmosphere 25, Unbroken 20, Rhythm 20, Authorship 20, Production 15). Ship only ≥85.\n\nBAN LIST: spinning-logo hero with a normal site below, fade-up cards, OrbitControls turntable, purple-blue blob gradients, glass cards on mesh gradient, centered Inter 64px, flat MeshBasic, bloom on everything, 200vh scroll for 8 moments, autoplay sound, lorem ipsum.\n\nSUBJECT: [DESCRIBE]. AUDIENCE: [WHO]. FEELING: [ONE EMOTION]. CTA: [ACTION]. ASSETS: [LIST OR \"art-direct yourself\"].\nMake decisions yourself; state assumptions; don't ask me anything unless blocked.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Archetype kickoff prompts\n\n### Product launch\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Product: [NAME — what it is, material, colour]. Build a launch site where the product is the world: silhouette in darkness with a coloured rim light → material macro → exploded anatomy with mono labels → in-world use (the environment that proves its benefit) → scrubbed spec numbers → slow 90° hero orbit with a specular sweep and the CTA \"[Pre-order / Buy]\". Emotion: [desire/awe]. Palette: black + [one rich hue]. If I provide a GLB use it (optimise it); otherwise build it procedurally with premium materials or generate hero plates and depth-displace them.\n```\n\n### Portfolio\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. I'm [NAME], a [ROLE]. Make my portfolio an immersive place that expresses my craft: [WORLD IDEA or \"choose one that fits me\"]. Name as giant type. Each of my [N] projects is a stop on the camera rail (plate floats in depth, tilts on hover, camera holds). About = entering a door/hatch. Contact = the exit of the world. Projects: [LIST with image paths]. Emotion: [intimacy/wonder].\n```\n\n### Real estate / hospitality / architecture\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Property: [NAME, location, type, key features]. Create an arrival film: dusk aerial → descent to the water/garden → approach with the giant name [NAME] reflected in still water behind/under the buildings → windows igniting sequentially (stagger) → threshold → interior glide with warm practicals → terrace view → reserve CTA. Colour script: warm amber windows vs cool cobalt sky. Generate a hero plate and layer/depth it (text sandwich: name behind the building).\n```\n\n### Brand / studio\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Studio: [NAME], we do [SERVICES]. Build a manifesto in motion: the camera tunnels through an abstract material world ([e.g. liquid chrome / paper / glass / smoke]); each manifesto line is giant type that the world interacts with; case studies drift past as depth-displaced plates; end card with a bold contact line. Tempo: fast with one long hold. Palette: [..]. Authorship test: this must be unmistakably OURS.\n```\n\n### Game / film / entertainment\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Title: [NAME]. Genre: [..]. Build the trailer as a website: cold open (black, ember particles) → title slam with light → world vista → [N] faction/character beats each in a distinct colour world → rapid montage (FOV kick, whip pans) → final shot + release date + wishlist CTA. Low camera angles, Dutch roll on tension beats, hard backlight and fog.\n```\n\n### SaaS / AI product\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Product: [NAME] — [one-line]. Place my real product screenshots ([paths]) as floating planes in depth; data as light trails/particles; each feature = the camera glides to a plane that \"activates\" (glow, scale 1.04, copy appears); final plane is flown through into the CTA. Dark, precise, calm; [accent colour] as the only colour. No fake dashboards-in-dashboards.\n```\n\n### Restaurant / food / lifestyle\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Venue: [NAME, cuisine, city]. Appetite film: steam and embers in darkness → macro on [ingredient] with shallow DOF → heat shimmer → slow-motion [pour/slice/flame] as a scrubbed frame sequence (I'll provide/generate clips: [paths]) → the table at golden hour → reserve a table CTA. Warm amber light, tactile materials, grain.\n```\n\n### Event / campaign / non-profit\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Event/cause: [NAME, date, place]. The date as giant type; a glowing path (line) the camera follows through a stylised map/landscape; one human moment as a single depth-displaced photo with a hold; impact numbers as scrubbed counters; RSVP/donate CTA as the final frame. Emotion: [hope/urgency/belonging].\n```\n\n---\n\n### Product launch — hero object world\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Build a scroll-driven immersive 3D website for [PRODUCT] by [BRAND]. Direction: 01 Product Altar. Place the product in a story-bearing environment that expresses [BENEFIT] (not a studio void). Build it from primitives or the client GLB, with designed studio lighting and an exploded-ascent chapter with projected labels. Signature move: the Exploded Ascent. Palette: near-black + one accent taken from the product. Finish to the $10,000 standard in references/14 (end-state, /buy route, metadata, mobile edit). Show me the contact sheet and score before you say done.\n```\n\n### Instrument / precision object\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Direction 04 The Instrument for [PRODUCT]. A machined 3D dial is the hero (see objects.ts dial()), scroll calibrates it, HUD readouts in mono, detents swap the headline. Dark, trustworthy, tactile. End on a pre-order frame.\n```\n\n### Light-mode luxury (not dark)\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Direction 12 Glass Atrium for [BRAND]. LIGHT mode — bone white, one muted pigment, frosted glass and refraction, real contact shadows. Signature move: Refraction Reveal. It must look expensive and calm, not like a tech demo.\n```\n\n### Liquid particles studio\n```\nUse the aura-3d-web skill. Direction 06 Liquid Particles for [STUDIO]. A figure of ~200k GPU points that dissolves, streams and re-forms into the studio glyph and a planet with scroll; pointer pushes the points; glass panels carry copy. Follow references/15 §2.\n```\n\n## 🔁 Refinement prompts (after the first build)\n- **\"Make it feel more expensive\"** → `Run the Aura Score; fix the lowest category. Prioritise lighting (rim light colour/intensity), depth separation, type scale, and grade (split-toning, contrast, bloom threshold). Re-screenshot all 12 stops.`\n- **\"More cinematic\"** → `Add a hold, vary lenses, add one Dutch roll beat, add a spatial transition between chapters 3 and 4, add foreground silhouettes and a near-bokeh particle band.`\n- **\"Too busy\"** → `Delete until every chapter has exactly one hero effect and ≤12 words. Give one chapter zero copy.`\n- **\"It lags\"** → `Profile with renderer.info. Lower DPR cap, gate mirror/transmission on f.quality, instance repeated meshes, compress textures to webp/KTX2, reduce particle counts on mobile. Report before/after FPS.`\n- **\"Mobile is broken\"** → `Add a mobile shot list, widen FOV, move copy to the bottom third, reduce counts ×0.4, drop the mirror floor, test at 390×844 and 360×640 with screenshots.`\n- **\"Make the type interact\"** → `Implement the text sandwich: hero word behind the subject cut-out, tracking scrubbed with --local, reflected on the floor.`\n- **\"Add a new chapter\"** → `Insert it in STORYBOARD.md first; re-space the shot list (at values) and DOM windows; keep one hold; re-score pacing.`\n\n## 🖼 Asset generation prompts\n- **Hero plate:** see `05-asset-pipeline.md §B1` (anatomy + examples).\n- **Depth layers:** `\"[SCENE]. Only the [far mountains and sky] layer, everything else removed, transparent background, same lighting and colour grade, 16:9\"` (repeat per layer: mid-ground, subject, foreground).\n- **Foreground silhouettes:** `\"Dark silhouette of [pine branches / tall grass / rocks] hanging from the top of frame, pure black shapes with soft rim light, transparent background\"`.\n- **Texture:** `\"Seamless tileable [wet black basalt] texture, top-down, even lighting, PBR albedo, 2048\"`.\n- **Sky:** `\"Equirectangular 360° [blue hour alpine sky with mist], high dynamic range look\"`.\n- **Product plate:** `\"[PRODUCT] on [surface], studio shot, single hard key from top-left, coloured rim light behind, deep black background, macro 100mm, razor-sharp, reflective floor\"`.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/10-frontier-techniques.md":"# 10 · Frontier techniques — going past the starter\n\nThe starter gets you a *very good* film in an afternoon. This file is what you reach for when the brief says \"better than anything I've seen\". Every technique lists **what it buys**, **the cost**, and **where it breaks**. Use **one hero technique per chapter** (law 8) — never all of them.\n\n> Rule of the frontier: *a technique earns its place only if the audit contact sheet looks better with it than without it.* Screenshot before and after.\n\n---\n\n## 0 · What the starter already gives you (don't rebuild)\n| Need | Use |\n| --- | --- |\n| Sky / sun / **eclipse** / stars / horizon haze | `createSky()` — 1 draw call; animate `uniforms.uEclipse`, `uHaze` with the playhead |\n| Volumetric god-rays | `createLightShafts({from,to,color,spread,count})` + `setIntensity(f(p))` |\n| Halos, lamps, sun flare | `createGlow(color,size,opacity)` |\n| Banded depth in valleys / streets / forest | `createHaze({layers,zStart,zStep})` |\n| Ground you can fly over (+ `heightAt` for camera & props) | `createDunes()` — swap the height function for mountains / snow / lava crust / swell |\n| Wet-floor reflections | `createMirrorFloor()` (gate on `quality`) |\n| Dust, bokeh, embers | `createParticles()` ×2–3 bands, placed **along the path** (the camera travels; fields don't) |\n| Foreground parallax plane | a particle band whose `position` *lerps toward the camera* (see `motes` in `scene.umbra.ts`) |\n| Giant type that never crops | `data-fit` + `fitType()` |\n| Exact frames for QA | `window.__aura.seek(p)`, `info()`; `?p=0.4` |\n\n---\n\n## 1 · Raymarched hero (SDF) — liquid chrome, metaballs, impossible geometry\n**Buys:** shapes no mesh can do (smooth-union blobs, fractals, melting forms), perfect reflections of *its own* environment. The single biggest \"this isn't a template\" signal.\n**Cost:** a full-screen fragment shader. Render at 0.5–0.75 resolution, never on mobile at full DPR.\n**Pattern:** a camera-facing quad (or the inside of a sphere shell) with a `ShaderMaterial`; pass `uP` (playhead), `uCamPos`, `uCamMat` from `update(f)`; march ≤ 64 steps; shade with the *same* palette as the scene and write fog colour on miss so it composites with real meshes.\n```glsl\nfloat sdSphere(vec3 p,float r){return length(p)-r;}\nfloat smin(float a,float b,float k){float h=max(k-abs(a-b),0.)/k;return min(a,b)-h*h*k*.25;}\nfloat map(vec3 p){\n  float d=1e9;\n  for(int i=0;i<5;i++){ float fi=float(i);\n    vec3 c=vec3(sin(uP*6.28+fi*1.7),cos(uP*4.+fi*2.3),sin(fi+uP*3.))*.9;   // blobs orbit with SCROLL\n    d=smin(d,sdSphere(p-c,.55),.6);\n  } return d; }\nvec3 nrm(vec3 p){vec2 e=vec2(.002,0);return normalize(vec3(map(p+e.xyy)-map(p-e.xyy),map(p+e.yxy)-map(p-e.yxy),map(p+e.yyx)-map(p-e.yyx)));}\n// shade: fresnel * envColour(reflect(rd,n)) + a coloured rim — do NOT output flat normals.\n```\n**Breaks:** banding in the gradient (add grain — the post pass already does), and mobile thermals. Provide a lower-step variant when `ctx.mobile`.\n\n## 2 · GPU particle simulation — 100k–1M points that flow, swirl and *form shapes*\n**Buys:** particles that assemble into a logo / product silhouette as you scroll, curl-noise rivers, galaxies. **Cost:** `GPUComputationRenderer` (position + velocity float textures); needs `EXT_color_buffer_float` (check; fall back to the CPU `createParticles`).\n**Pattern:** target positions baked from a mesh (`MeshSurfaceSampler`) or text (sample a 2D canvas); the sim's attractor strength is `ease.inOutCubic(seg(p,a,b))` — so the *scroll* assembles the shape and un-assembles it on scroll-up. Render as `Points` with additive blending and size attenuation; colour by velocity.\n**Breaks:** iOS Safari float-texture limits → cap at 128×128 (16k) on mobile.\n\n## 3 · Generated-image worlds (2.5D) — photoreal in minutes\nUse `layers.ts` (`createLayerStack`, `createDepthPlane`) with images from `05-asset-pipeline.md`.\n- **Hero plate + depth displacement:** one art-directed image → depth estimate (Depth-Anything) → dense plane displaced in the vertex shader; camera moves ±12° only. Add fog *between* 3 duplicated planes at different z for volumetric feel.\n- **Layer stack:** 4–7 cut-outs (sky / far / mid / subject / foreground). Parallax between layers + particles in the gaps = AAA matte-painting. This is how you get *photographic* worlds with no modelling.\n- **Always** add real 3D on top (particles, a light shaft, a glow, a floating mesh) — pure 2.5D reads as a parallax slideshow.\n\n## 4 · Type that lives in the world\n| Level | How | When |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| CSS sandwich | `.aura-giant` between canvas and a transparent cut-out PNG of the subject | fastest \"letters behind the building\" |\n| **3D text** | `troika-three-text` (SDF, any font) as a mesh in the scene at a z between depth planes; `fog:false`, `depthWrite:false`; fade `material.opacity` with `pulse(p,a,b)` | type that parallaxes with the camera, gets occluded by the subject, reflected by the mirror floor |\n| Per-letter | split to spans, drive `--i` index, stagger with `calc(var(--local) * N - var(--i))` | kinetic reveals; keep to ONE per film |\n| Type as light | an emissive text plane + bloom threshold below its intensity | titles that glow in the dark |\nMeasure, don't guess: giant DOM type → `data-fit`. 3D text → compute `troikaText.geometry.boundingBox`, scale so projected width ≈ 0.86 of the frustum at that z.\n\n## 5 · Camera craft (cheap, huge)\n- **Handheld breath:** add `sin(t*0.7)*0.015` positional noise at ≤ 1% amplitude to *holds only* (scaled by `1-f.velocity*8`) so a hold feels alive, never static.\n- **Focus pull:** `BokehPass` is heavy; instead blur the DOM-text layer and fade particle size by distance from the focal plane (`uFocus` uniform) — the eye reads depth of field.\n- **Lens language:** wide (50–65°) = speed / scale; 24–30° = intimacy + compression. Never fly the *same* lens through two consecutive shots.\n- **Dutch roll** ±2–4° during the \"pass\" shot. Bigger reads as a gimmick.\n- **Anticipation:** pull back 3% of the rail distance *before* a push-in (a real dolly operator does this). One extra shot, enormous feel.\n\n## 6 · Light & matter upgrades\n- **Colour contrast over brightness:** warm rim vs cold fill (Umbra: ember rim `#ff8a45` vs `#6f8fc8` key at 0.55). Complementary light separates planes better than any blur.\n- **Emissive discipline:** `emissive(color, 2–5)` + bloom threshold **0.88–0.9** → only the intended things glow. If `blown > 2%` in the audit, lower emissive, don't lower bloom strength.\n- **Roughness variance:** `roughnessMap` from `noiseTexture()`; wear on edges. Uniform roughness is the #1 \"CG\" tell.\n- **Transmission/refraction** (`glass()`): 1–3 meshes, `quality > 0.6` only.\n- **Wet look:** `clearcoat 1`, `clearcoatRoughness 0.08`, plus the mirror floor at `color: 0x2a2f3a` (< 1 so it reads as a surface).\n- **Fake caustics / light-through-water:** an additive `MeshBasicMaterial` plane with a scrolling noise texture, projected from a spot light direction.\n- **Contact shadows:** one blurred dark radial sprite under each hero object — costs nothing, grounds everything.\n\n## 7 · Scroll as a *director*, not a slider\n- **Non-linear scroll → playhead:** apply `ease.inOutSine` to `trackProgress` for ceremony at the ends, or a piecewise map that *slows* the scroll inside holds (fewer vh per `p` there = a longer felt hold).\n- **Chapter snap-assist (soft):** within 0.02 of a chapter's centre, nudge the target toward it after 600 ms idle. **Never** scroll-jack — only a gentle settle, cancelled instantly by any input.\n- **Velocity as a material:** `f.velocity` already kicks FOV and aberration. Also drive particle streaking, ring spin, bloom strength (`+0.15*velocity`).\n- **Scrub video textures:** `video.currentTime = p * duration` on a `VideoTexture` (all-intra H.264/WebM, keyframe every frame) — pre-rendered AAA footage (Blender / Veo / Runway) used *inside* the 3D world on a screen or as a sky.\n\n## 8 · Interaction that feels authored\n- **Pointer = light:** move the practical `PointLight` toward the pointer ray (world-space unproject) at 0.12 damping → the world responds to the hand.\n- **Magnetic CTA:** translate `.aura-cta` toward the pointer within 90px (damped), scale the ring.\n- **Hero hover:** raycast the hero only when the pointer moves; on hit, raise `emissiveIntensity` and ease a +3% scale.\n- **Opt-in sound:** a 🔊 toggle (default off), one ambient loop + a low swell on holds; duck on scroll velocity. Never autoplay.\n- **Audio-reactive:** `AnalyserNode` → low-band energy → bloom strength / particle size. Only when the user opted into sound.\n\n## 9 · WebGPU / TSL (progressive enhancement)\n`three/webgpu` + TSL gives compute shaders for 1M-particle sims, better bloom/DoF, node-based materials. **Only as an enhancement:** feature-detect `navigator.gpu`; keep the WebGL path as the default, identical in composition. Never ship a WebGPU-only film.\n\n## 10 · Gaussian splats / photogrammetry\nReal captured places (a restaurant, a house, a sculpture) via `@sparkjsdev/spark` or Luma's web library: load a compressed `.spz`/`.ksplat`, put the camera rail through it, composite with real meshes + particles. Budget ≤ 6 MB compressed, cull by distance, show the poster until the first sort completes.\n\n## 11 · World recipes (starting specs — then make them *yours*)\n| World | Sky | Ground / hero | Atmosphere | Light | Type & palette |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Deep ocean descent** | none; `FogExp2` teal→black, density grows with `p` | seabed `createDunes` (low H, dark), a colossal silhouette (whale / wreck) | caustics plane, marine snow (`createParticles` size 1.2 falling), `createLightShafts` from above that *weaken with depth* | surface light fades; bioluminescent practicals (emissive + glow sprites) | cyan `#52e6d8` on `#02121a`; condensed grotesk |\n| **Night city / neon rain** | `createSky` zenith `#05060d`, magenta horizon | wet street `createMirrorFloor` (reflects neon), instanced boxes for blocks with emissive window texture | haze cards in magenta/cyan, rain as stretched additive particles | coloured rims from signs; everything else crushed | magenta/cyan on near-black, mono type |\n| **Alpine / glacier dawn** | `createSky` with warm horizon, `uHaze` high | ridged terrain (snow colour, low roughness), a lodge or peak as hero | long banded haze, spindrift particles | low sun key + blue shadow fill | cold white + one warm accent, thin serif display |\n| **Interior / architecture** | none (HDRI-ish `env`) | room boxes, glossy floor, instanced furniture | dust in shafts from windows (`createLightShafts` from window → floor) | one hard window key + warm practicals | restrained neutrals, large light serif |\n| **Space / station** | stars only (`uStars` 1, dark horizon) | planet = sphere with fresnel atmosphere shell; station = instanced modules | volumetric nebula cards, engine glows | single hard sun + blue earth-bounce | white mono on black, one signal colour |\n| **Product studio** | gradient backdrop plane | hero on `chrome()`/`glass()` with turntable *driven by scroll*, exploded view | very light haze, sparse bokeh | 3 strip lights + rim; sweep a specular with `p` | brand colour as a single rim; huge product name |\n| **Forest / mist** | pale `createSky`, fog = sky | instanced trunks (1 draw call), terrain with `heightAt` for placement | 5 haze layers, god-rays between trunks, floating spores | one shaft key; green-teal fill | low-saturation greens + one gold |\n\n## 12 · Failure modes seen in the wild (and the fix)\n| Symptom in the contact sheet | Cause | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Opening frame is a glowing white wash | glow sprite / horizon haze additive on a bright sky | cut glow opacity to ≤ 0.1, lower `uHaze`, bloom threshold 0.9 |\n| Stars look like square confetti | stars from a per-cell hash without a distance falloff | round & soft: `smoothstep(.2,0.,length(f-off))` (fixed in `createSky`) |\n| Sand looks like water | bump map too strong / repeating | `bumpScale ≤ 0.6`, repeat ≤ 40, sheen ≤ 0.6 |\n| Hero competes with its own debris | fragments too large | fragment scale ≤ 40% of hero width |\n| Last frame is empty space | the final shot flies away from the subject | final shot = composed return to the hero; CTA inside the image |\n| Title cropped / overlapping | guessed `vw` sizes | `data-fit`; run the audit (it measures the text) |\n| Dead scroll in the middle | shots with near-identical pos AND no copy | either it's a HOLD with copy, or move the next shot closer |\n| Mobile looks like a different, worse site | desktop rail reused | halve particles, `maxDpr 1.25`, taller type, shorter rail, drop mirror floor |\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/11-art-directions.md":"# 11 · Art directions — pick a lane BEFORE you pick a feature\n\nMost \"3D websites\" fail because the agent never chose an **art direction**; it chose *technology*. A $10,000 site has a lane, a palette, a camera personality and one signature move you could describe in a sentence. This file is the catalogue. **Phase 1 of the skill makes you pick one (or blend two, with one clearly dominant) and write it into `AURA_BRIEF.md` as the _Direction Lock_.**\n\n## How to choose (60 seconds)\n\n| The client sells… | The visitor must feel… | Default direction |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| a physical product (audio, wearable, device, bottle, shoe, cosmetics) | desire, precision, \"I need that\" | **01 Product Altar** — or **12 Glass Atrium** for light/soft goods |\n| a car, watch, jewellery, a hero luxury object | status, polish, control | **02 Showroom Sweep** |\n| a place (estate, hotel, resort, development, venue) | arrival, belonging, calm envy | **03 Dusk Arrival** |\n| a tool with a dial: navigation, audio, finance, aviation, fitness, automation | competence, mastery | **04 The Instrument** |\n| culture, hospitality, game, film, heritage, a restaurant with a soul | reverence, mystery, story | **05 Night Walk** |\n| AI, an agency, a studio, entertainment, a bold brand | \"this team is from the future\" | **06 Liquid Particles** |\n| a person (designer, dev, photographer, director) | taste, range, memorability | **07 Observatory Folio** |\n| a manifesto, a label, a festival, a launch with attitude | adrenaline, belonging | **08 Void Tunnel** |\n| a journey, event, outdoor brand, expedition, campaign | scale, awe, destination | **09 Terrain Epic** |\n| food, drink, fragrance, a ritual | appetite, warmth, touch | **10 Macro Appetite** |\n| software, fintech, data, infrastructure | clarity, power, trust | **11 Interface Cosmos** |\n| fashion, wellness, skincare, a clinic, architecture studio, anything that should NOT be dark | serenity, luxury, air | **12 Glass Atrium** (LIGHT mode) |\n\nIf the brief doesn't fit: take the closest, then change **world**, **palette** and **signature move** until it's yours (Authorship test). Never ship a direction's reference world untouched.\n\n> Every direction below lists a **Build recipe** referencing starter helpers. Everything is achievable zero-asset; where a generated plate or GLB makes it 3× better, it says so.\n\n---\n\n## 01 · Product Altar\n*Feels like: a flagship earbuds / headphone / device launch where the object floats over a hostile, beautiful landscape.*\n- **World:** the product placed in a **non-studio, story-bearing environment** — cratered asteroid, wet black basalt, frozen lake, desert dune at blue hour, brushed-steel machine hall. The environment says *how it feels to own it* (silence → asteroid; resilience → basalt; clarity → ice).\n- **Hero:** ONE product with believable materials. Hold the money shot for ≥ 12 % of scroll.\n- **Palette:** near-black dominant, desaturated cold environment, ONE accent taken from the product's glowing detail (LED, seam, lens).\n- **Type:** tiny technical nav (mono, 10 px caps), a micro-spec rail on the side, one *huge* wordmark that the product overlaps (type behind object, object occludes). Feature names as small captions pinned to the object with leader lines.\n- **Light:** hard cool key from a low angle that rakes the environment's relief, razor rim on the product edge, a tiny warm practical (the LED).\n- **Camera:** macro orbit → pull back to reveal environment → exploded-anatomy (parts separate along an axis) → hero hold → end on a slow dolly toward a reserved CTA.\n- **Signature move:** **the Exploded Ascent** — on a scroll window the product separates into 5–8 labelled parts that levitate on one axis with micro-rotations, then re-assemble as the camera pulls out.\n- **Skeleton:** `0–.14` silhouette emerges from dark · `.14–.34` material macros (two) · `.34–.58` exploded anatomy · `.58–.72` environment reveal · `.72–.9` in-use / spec numerals counting · `.9–1` hero frame + CTA.\n- **Build recipe:** `studio.ts → createStudioEnv + productRig`, `objects.ts → earbuds()`, `rock.ts → createRockGround` + `createBoulders`, `scene.halo.ts` is the finished reference.\n- **Failure modes:** product too small in frame · glossy black on black (no rim) · env map missing (looks like plastic) · specs as a feature grid mid-scroll.\n\n## 02 · Showroom Sweep\n*Feels like: a hypercar reveal, a watch film, a jewellery campaign.*\n- **World:** an infinite dark cyclorama with a mirror floor, and **light as the actor** — long softboxes sweep across the object's surfaces as the camera crawls.\n- **Palette:** black, graphite, one metal tone (champagne / titanium / copper). Colour appears only inside highlights.\n- **Light:** animated strip lights (`RectAreaLight` or emissive planes reflected in the clearcoat) moving along the body with scroll — the *reflection* tells the form.\n- **Camera:** very slow 85–135 mm-equivalent (fov 14–24°) pushes with tiny lateral drift. Wide shots rare and deliberate.\n- **Signature move:** **the Light Pass** — a single hard highlight line travels the full silhouette during one hold. People will scroll back to watch it again.\n- **Build recipe:** `createMirrorFloor` with low roughness, `chrome()/obsidian()` + animated emissive strips, bloom threshold ≥ 0.92, `grade.contrast` 1.15.\n- **Failure modes:** flat studio light · turntable rotation instead of camera moves · dust particles (dirty); keep the air *clean* here.\n\n## 03 · Dusk Arrival\n*Feels like: a $5k real-estate/hospitality reel — giant name, warm windows, still water.*\n- **World:** a place at blue-hour with **water in the foreground**. The giant name sits BEHIND the building and is mirrored in the water.\n- **Palette:** deep teal-navy sky, amber window glow, stone neutrals; the amber is the accent and the CTA colour.\n- **Hero:** the building as 3 massed volumes with emissive windows that ignite sequentially with scroll (one per ~2 %).\n- **Camera:** aerial descent → low skim over water → threshold (door/gate light) → interior golden-hour peek → final exterior frame.\n- **Signature move:** **Windows Ignite** — each window lights on a stagger tied to scroll; reflections ignite below with 70 % opacity and ripple distortion.\n- **Plate upgrade:** generate a photoreal dusk plate + depth (see `05-asset-pipeline.md`) and put procedural water, mist and ember particles in front. This is the single biggest quality jump of any direction.\n- **Build recipe:** `createMirrorFloor` (water, with ripple normal), `createSky`, `createHaze`, instanced window quads (`emissive`), `createDepthPlane` for the plate.\n- **Failure modes:** flat daylight · no foreground plane · a floor-plan carousel in the middle of the film (park it after the end frame).\n\n## 04 · The Instrument\n*Feels like: a nav compass / mixing-desk knob / watch complication — a precision dial as a hero, with HUD furniture.*\n- **World:** a dark cockpit/bench with a single beautifully machined dial; ambient bokeh of the room behind (generated plate or blurred emissive sprites).\n- **Hero:** a **dial with real knurling, bezel chamfers, engraved ticks** (see `objects.ts → dial()`), and a live numeric readout (HTML overlay) driven by scroll (heading 000→360°).\n- **Palette:** teal-black body, amber/orange needle + tick glow, bone-white type.\n- **Type:** mono readouts, tiny tracked labels, big \"Find your HEADING.\" display line at left with the object at right.\n- **Camera:** front-on hero, then oblique macro of the knurl, rotate the *dial* (not the camera) with scroll so numbers and ring spin in sync.\n- **Signature move:** **Calibration** — scroll rotates the dial; a ring of ticks lights progressively; at hold points the dial \"detents\" (spring ease) and a section title swaps. The UI feels *mechanical*.\n- **Build recipe:** `objects.ts → dial()`, `ring of instanced ticks`, `data-attributes → HUD`, snap using `choreo.snap()`.\n- **Failure modes:** a flat 2D SVG dial (must be lit 3D) · readout out of sync with rotation · too many HUD elements (max 6 on screen).\n\n## 05 · Night Walk\n*Feels like: a Kyoto shrine at night, a game's opening, a ramen bar with a soul.*\n- **World:** a **2.5D diorama** of 6–9 depth-separated layers (sky + moon, far mountains, shrine, mid gates, foreground grass, lantern bokeh) with real parallax from the camera dolly. Use generated layers (see `layers.ts`) when you have image generation; otherwise silhouettes with gradient + noise.\n- **Palette:** bruise indigo, charcoal, ONE blood-red (moon, gates, particles), warm lantern amber as the only warm light.\n- **Type:** giant serif or wide grotesk letters sliding *between* depth layers — letters pass behind the foreground grass and in front of the shrine.\n- **Camera:** a slow forward walk with gentle vertical bob, FOV breath, roll ≤ 1°.\n- **Signature move:** **Type Between Layers** — the title is sandwiched in z between foreground and background plates; scroll splits the letters apart as the camera passes through them.\n- **Atmosphere:** embers/petals (two speeds), ground mist, a pulsing moon glow. Chapters are named places (\"Temples / Gardens / Rituals / Afterglow\").\n- **Failure modes:** one flat illustration with CSS parallax (needs ≥ 6 planes) · red everywhere.\n\n## 06 · Liquid Particles\n*Feels like: an AI/agency site where a sculpture of light dissolves, reforms and reacts to the cursor.*\n- **World:** a void with a **hero form made of ~120–400k GPU points** (statue silhouette, face, mask, planet, logo) that morphs between 3–4 target shapes with scroll, with floating glass panels carrying copy.\n- **Palette:** blue-black, violet/ice cyan accent, a hot coral counter-light. Additive blending only on points.\n- **Type:** wide, light-weight display; copy on glass cards with `backdrop-filter` + thin 1 px borders; micro labels.\n- **Camera:** gentle parallax orbit driven by pointer, pushes in on scroll; form tilts to follow the cursor.\n- **Signature move:** **The Dissolve** — at a chapter boundary the points curl-noise away, stream across screen, and settle into the next shape (see `15-shader-recipes.md → particle morph`).\n- **Build recipe:** `createParticles` for dust; custom `Points` + `ShaderMaterial` morph (recipe), `layers.ts` for glass panels (canvas-textured quads or DOM overlay), bloom threshold 0.6.\n- **Failure modes:** random noisy cloud with no readable form · every chapter the same shape · points too big (looks like confetti).\n\n## 07 · Observatory Folio\n*Feels like: a top designer's portfolio — a place that is the author's mind, with projects as objects on a rail.*\n- **World:** an abstract architecture (observatory, gallery, atelier, library of light). Each project is an **object or framed plane in 3D space** the rail flies to; the cursor carries a soft light that reveals surface detail.\n- **Palette:** 1 hue + neutrals. The *author's* taste in colour is the product — commit hard.\n- **Type:** name as giant fitted type in frame one; project titles as small-cap labels; an index list (01—07) as the nav.\n- **Camera:** looks at each project from a *different shot size* (wide, close, high, low), with a hold on each; transitions are whip-pan-style fast eases.\n- **Signature move:** **Project Rooms** — entering a project dims the world, lights the plane with a practical, and swaps copy/arrows in the HUD; scrolling out reverses it.\n- **Always include:** an \"About\" beat with a face/silhouette and a *Contact* end-card with an email as the CTA.\n- **Failure modes:** a grid of thumbnails in a 3D room · all projects at the same distance/angle · no contact.\n\n## 08 · Void Tunnel\n*Feels like: a label drop, a music festival, a bold brand manifesto — velocity.*\n- **World:** an infinite corridor of rings/slabs/typographic panels, lit by coloured strips, camera speed tied to scroll velocity.\n- **Palette:** one saturated hue (acid green, signal red, ultramarine) on near-black; white type.\n- **Camera:** fast, long, FOV kicks with velocity (`f.velocity → fov +`), chromatic aberration only at speed.\n- **Signature move:** **Velocity Type** — words fly past as 3D slabs and stretch (scaleZ) with scroll velocity; stop scrolling and they settle into a legible stack.\n- **Build recipe:** `InstancedMesh` of rings along z, emissive bars, `grade.aberration` driven by velocity (see 10-frontier).\n- **Failure modes:** nausea (cap FOV kick ≤ +14°, roll ≤ 2°) · unreadable copy (give every beat a *rest*).\n\n## 09 · Terrain Epic\n*Feels like: an expedition film, game cinematic or festival eclipse — scale.*\n- **World:** terrain with a character sky (eclipse, aurora, storm front, twin suns) and one human-scale landmark. `scene.umbra.ts` is the finished reference.\n- **Camera:** crane / walk / worm's-eye telephoto hero / pass-through / final symmetrical frame.\n- **Signature move:** **Totality** — sky uniforms (eclipse, haze) animate with scroll so the *whole world's mood* changes across the film.\n- **Build recipe:** `createDunes`, `createSky`, `createLightShafts`, `createGlow`, 3 particle bands.\n\n## 10 · Macro Appetite\n*Feels like: a premium sauce / whisky / perfume / cafe — warm, tactile, slow.*\n- **World:** extreme macro of the ingredient with heat/steam; background pure dark with one warm key from the side.\n- **Palette:** burnt amber, cream, deep brown; accent the colour of the food.\n- **Camera:** macro (fov 12–20°) with shallow-DOF *feel* (blurred foreground sprites, bokeh points); slow push.\n- **Signature move:** **The Pour** — a frame-sequence (generated video → 120 webp frames) scrubbed by scroll on a canvas plane; steam particles rise with velocity.\n- **Failure modes:** stock-photo grids · cool lighting · fast cuts. Appetite needs *slowness*.\n\n## 11 · Interface Cosmos\n*Feels like: the product as a place — real UI floating in depth, data as light.*\n- **World:** a dark volumetric space with **real product UI screenshots** (or live HTML rendered to texture / CSS3D) as planes at different depths; data points as light trails between them.\n- **Palette:** deep navy, one electric accent, white at 8 % for UI chrome.\n- **Camera:** glides plane to plane; each feature = one plane scaling forward with a glow; the last plane IS the sign-up form.\n- **Signature move:** **Plane Activation** — the next UI plane's border draws, its content un-blurs, a light trail connects it to the previous.\n- **Build recipe:** `createLayerStack`, CSS3DRenderer (optional), `createParticles` as trails, bloom 0.7.\n- **Failure modes:** pricing tables inside the 3D · screenshots with no depth separation.\n\n## 12 · Glass Atrium (LIGHT MODE)\n*Feels like: a high-end skincare / fashion / clinic / architecture studio — bright, calm, expensive.*\n- **World:** a luminous, bright space: frosted glass slabs, soft caustics, floating spheres/arches of translucent material, pale sky gradient. **Proves the skill is not only dark scenes.**\n- **Palette:** warm white / bone, one muted pigment (sage, clay, powder blue), black type.\n- **Light:** huge soft key + subtle bounce; contact shadows (blurred planar) *must* exist or objects float.\n- **Materials:** `glass()` (transmission), frosted roughness 0.35, subtle iridescence, paper-like matte for balance.\n- **Camera:** slow, symmetrical, architectural; dolly + gentle rise.\n- **Signature move:** **Refraction Reveal** — a glass slab passes over type; the text beneath refracts/shifts as it slides by.\n- **Failure modes:** looks like Apple-clone (add one weird, authored object) · overexposed whites with no contrast · bloom on bright surfaces (use threshold 1.0).\n\n---\n\n## Blending rules\n- Max **two** directions; one is 80 %.\n- Never mix a dark direction with a light one inside one film unless the *transition itself* is the story (e.g. descent from daylight into a cave).\n- Direction ≠ genre. A law firm can be *Instrument*; a bank can be *Dusk Arrival*. The surprise is the point — as long as it still sells.\n\n## The Direction Lock (paste into AURA_BRIEF.md)\n```\nDIRECTION: 01 Product Altar (80%) + 05 Night Walk atmosphere (20%)\nWORLD: …one sentence, concrete nouns…\nHERO: …the single object/image…\nSIGNATURE MOVE: …named, one sentence…\nPALETTE: #… (dominant) #… (accent) #… (neutral)\nCAMERA PERSONALITY: …\nFORBIDDEN: …three things this site will never do…\n```\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/12-product-hero-playbook.md":"# 12 · Product-hero playbook — websites that revolve around a 3D render of the product\n\nUse this when the object is the reason the site exists (earbuds, headphones, watch, bottle, sneaker, phone, camera, instrument, jewellery, gadget, supplement jar). The goal: the visitor believes they are looking at a **marketing render from a $200k Cinema4D/Octane pipeline** — in real time.\n\n## 1 · Where does the product come from? (pick the best you can get)\n\n| Source | Quality | When |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Client GLB / CAD export** | ★★★★★ | Ask for it first. Run `gltf-transform optimize --compress meshopt --texture-compress webp`. Re-author materials (see §3) — client materials are never good enough. |\n| **AI image → 3D** (Meshy, Tripo, Hunyuan3D, Rodin, Luma) | ★★★☆☆ | Generate 4 clean orthographic reference images first (white background, 3/4 view, front, side, top). Export GLB, then **retopo-light**: decimate to < 80k tris, re-bake normals, replace materials. Always inspect for melted details; hide flaws with camera distance + rim light. |\n| **Procedural, coded from primitives** | ★★★★☆ for hard-surface | Earbuds, dials, bottles, rings, lenses, knobs, cases: lathe + capsule + bevelled boxes + boolean-looking details. `starter/src/aura/objects.ts` has working builders you can restyle in minutes. Looks *cleaner* than a bad scan. |\n| **Generated hero plate (image) + depth plane** | ★★★★☆ | When you only need ~±8° of parallax. Generate the product on its environment, depth-displace (`createDepthPlane`), put real particles in front. |\n\n**Rule:** never ship an AI-generated mesh straight from the generator. Never ship a client mesh with its original materials.\n\n## 2 · The environment is half the product\nHero-in-a-void is a template. Hero-in-a-**place** is a campaign. Pick an environment that *expresses the benefit* (see direction 01). Build it from `rock.ts` (cratered displaced ground, boulders), `terrain.ts` (dunes), or a mirror floor + backdrop. Use `scene.fog` to bury the far field so you only light what you need.\n\n**Studio environment map (mandatory):** `createStudioEnv(renderer, {...})` in `studio.ts` builds a PMREM from a *procedural room of softboxes*: it gives metal and clearcoat something to reflect. A product without a designed environment map looks like a toy. Tune the **strip lights** in the env: long thin rectangles produce the luxury \"highlight line\" down curved surfaces.\n\n## 3 · Material recipes that read as expensive\n| Surface | Recipe |\n| --- | --- |\n| Piano-black lacquer | `obsidian()`: roughness .2–.3, clearcoat 1, clearcoatRoughness .03 |\n| Anodised aluminium | metalness 1, roughness .32–.45 + `noiseTexture` as roughnessMap (±.08), subtle anisotropy |\n| Brushed steel | roughnessMap from stretched noise (repeat 1×60), anisotropy .8 |\n| Frosted glass / silicone | `MeshPhysicalMaterial` transmission .6, roughness .5, thickness 1, `sheen` 1 for soft-touch |\n| Rubber/ear tip | roughness .75, sheen .6, sheenColor = accent 20 % |\n| Emissive LED | `emissive(accent, 3)` + a tiny `PointLight` + `createGlow` sprite |\n| Engraving / text | bump/normal from a canvas texture; never flat decals |\nAdd **micro-bevel everywhere** (round 0.01–0.03 units): sharp edges are the #1 tell of cheap CG — highlights live on bevels.\n\n## 4 · Light rig for a product (Altar / Showroom)\n1. **Key:** hard, cool, low-angle from the side opposite the environment's relief.\n2. **Rim:** the strongest light. Behind and above; colour = accent or cold white. It separates the product from a dark background.\n3. **Kicker strip:** an emissive plane / `RectAreaLight` (long & thin) grazing the curve — drives the highlight line.\n4. **Fill:** hemisphere at 0.2–0.4 — never more; you want shadows.\n5. **Practical:** the product's own light (LED, display, seam) — animate its intensity on a hold.\n6. **Contact shadow:** a blurred dark disc under the product if it rests on something — mandatory for grounding.\n\n## 5 · The five camera shots every product film needs\n1. **Reveal** — silhouette/edge-light in the dark; product barely there.\n2. **Macro A** — the signature material (fov 14–20°, 10–15 cm away, rack the highlight across it).\n3. **Anatomy** — exploded view, camera slightly high, parts levitate in order of assembly.\n4. **Context** — pull back into the environment; the object is small but still the brightest thing.\n5. **Hero** — three-quarter, slightly low, perfectly lit, **hold ≥ 12 % of scroll**; the wordmark behind, the CTA reserved.\n\n## 6 · Exploded-view technique (reuse)\n- Model the product as a `Group` of named parts with a `rest` position and an `explode` vector (in local axis, usually Y or Z).\n- Each part has its own **stagger window** within the explode chapter: `t_i = seg(p, a + i*δ, a + i*δ + w)`.\n- Position = rest + explode × ease(t_i) · spread; add tiny rotation (≤ 6°) and a *label* (HTML, pinned via `project(part)`), drawn as a 1 px line + mono caption.\n- Keep the parts aligned on ONE axis. A chaotic burst reads as \"broken\".\n\n## 7 · Spec & copy rules for products\n- 3 claims, not 12. Each a number + unit + noun (\"48 dB\", \"30 h\", \"5.4 g\").\n- Numbers **count up** with the playhead (scrubbed, reversible).\n- Spec table, reviews, FAQ, pricing → *after* the final frame on the same page, or on `/specs`, `/buy`. The film never has a content wall.\n- The CTA says what happens: \"Pre-order — ships March\" beats \"Learn more\".\n\n## 8 · Product film QA add-ons\n- Take a still of frames at p = 0.05 / 0.45 / 0.8 and ask: *could this be in the brand's Instagram?* If not: lighting or materials.\n- Zoom the screenshot to 200 %: are edges bevelled and highlights continuous? Aliasing on a hero edge = raise `msaa` / pixel ratio for that shot.\n- Product occupies ≥ 38 % of frame height in the hero frame on desktop, ≥ 45 % on mobile.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/13-portfolio-and-brand-playbook.md":"# 13 · Portfolio, studio & brand playbook\n\nPeople hire a designer/studio for **taste and range**. The site is the audition. These rules are what separate \"cool Three.js demo\" from \"I'd pay $10k for this.\"\n\n## The structure of a portfolio film (7–9 min of scroll at normal pace → 700–1000 vh)\n| Range | Beat | Camera |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0–.10 | **Name** — fitted giant type, one line of positioning, cursor light | slow push, pointer parallax |\n| .10–.22 | **Door** — the world opens (a slit of light, a lid lifts, the room assembles) | pull-through |\n| .22–.74 | **Project rooms ×4–6** — each with a different shot size & angle, a HOLD, a title, role, year, one-line result | rail through the space |\n| .74–.84 | **Craft** — 3 skills as *objects of light*, not progress bars | orbit |\n| .84–.92 | **About** — face or silhouette, 2 sentences, a human detail | close |\n| .92–1 | **Contact** — email as giant type + \"Available from March\" + one button | static, composed |\n\n## Project rooms (the core pattern)\n- A project is a **plane or object with its own pool of light**. Entering: world dims 20 %, practical light rises, HUD swaps (title, index 03/07, arrows).\n- Use the **real project imagery** (ask for it; if none, generate polished placeholder plates and label them clearly as placeholders in the hand-off notes).\n- Each room differs in **three of**: distance, angle, light colour, plate aspect ratio, camera speed.\n- Hover (desktop) tilts the plane toward the cursor ≤ 6°, the light follows; click opens a full-screen case-study overlay (HTML) with the camera continuing to drift behind it.\n\n## Cursor & pointer\n- A custom cursor is *light*, not an arrow: an 80 px soft glow that blends `screen`, scales on interactive targets, and carries one-word labels (\"View\", \"Play\", \"Drag\").\n- Pointer parallax on the camera ≤ 0.6° and on near-plane objects ≤ 12 px. It's what makes it feel *alive* when the user isn't scrolling.\n\n## Studio / agency / brand variant\n- **Manifesto in motion:** 6–8 lines of ≤ 6 words each, set in giant type, each line *entering the world differently* (rising from floor, passing as slab, assembling from points, mirrored in water).\n- **Case studies** drift past as framed planes on a loop ribbon; the hovered one comes to the front and plays 3 s of muted video.\n- **Clients** are not a logo bar. A *wall of light* where logos are engraved/emissive on a surface the camera passes once.\n- **Team** is one beat: a row of silhouettes; hover reveals names. Never a 12-photo grid.\n\n## Navigation that doesn't break the illusion\n- Persistent 3-item pill nav: Work · About · Contact (mono, 11 px caps) — clicking *scrolls the playhead* to that chapter with an eased `scrollTo` that takes 1.6–2.4 s (the camera flies there).\n- A **chapter index** on the left or bottom (01 — 07) with the active one lit; hover shows the chapter name.\n- A sound toggle only if you actually ship audio (§ audio in 10-frontier).\n\n## Copy\n- Verbs & specifics. \"Rebranded a 40-year-old Swiss watchmaker for TikTok\" > \"Brand identity & strategy\".\n- One number per project. Years in mono.\n- The contact line is a *human sentence*: \"Tell me what you're making.\" not \"Get in touch\".\n\n## Variants of the world (pick one, own it)\n- **Observatory** (instrument-lit, brass + indigo) · **Atelier** (paper, wood, a sunlit table, objects on it) · **Gallery of light** (white cube, glass, soft caustics) · **Server cathedral** (dark nave of racks, cool LEDs) · **Greenhouse** (botanical, humid air, god-rays) · **Dune studio** (warm minimal, long shadows). Each defines palette + materials + atmosphere for free.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/14-premium-delivery.md":"# 14 · The $10,000 delivery standard\n\nA client paying $10k is not buying a hero animation. They are buying **a finished, trustworthy, fast, findable, convertible website with a hero animation that ends careers of competitors.** Check every item before you say done.\n\n## A · Scope of a complete deliverable\n1. **The Film** — the scroll-driven 3D experience (the part everyone remembers).\n2. **The Landing end-state** — after the final frame, the page continues as a normal, fast, well-designed site: proof, details, FAQ, footer (see below). Same type, same grade, same light — no style break.\n3. **Secondary routes** — at minimum: `/contact` (or booking/reserve/checkout), one detail page template (project / product spec / unit), and `404`. Each is HTML-first, pretty, and shares the film's tokens.\n4. **A fallback** — designed poster for no-WebGL, a calm reduced-motion variant, and a mobile shot list.\n5. **Metadata** — title/description/OG image (render a frame of the film at p≈.9 as the OG image), favicon, theme-color, JSON-LD for the business/product.\n6. **Performance report** — first-load KB, LCP, FPS on a throttled CPU, drawCalls. Put in the hand-off notes.\n7. **Hand-off README** — how to edit copy, swap images, change colours, add a chapter; every knob lives in ONE `config.ts`.\n\n## B · Loader = chapter zero\n- The loader is **designed**: a 1.8–3 s sequence that uses the brand (wordmark drawing, counter 000→100, a light sweep) and ends by *becoming* the first frame (no white flash, no layout jump).\n- Preload only what chapter 1–2 need; stream the rest while the user reads.\n- Never show a blank page: SSR the title + poster behind the canvas.\n\n## C · Micro-detail checklist (what makes it feel \"AAA\")\n- [ ] Film grain ≥ 0.04 and a vignette ≥ 0.6 — they glue 3D to type.\n- [ ] Depth-of-field *feel*: blurred foreground sprites, bokeh points on practicals.\n- [ ] Lens: gentle breathing (±0.4° fov), tiny handheld drift (0.002 u) — not zero.\n- [ ] Easing everywhere: no linear. Scroll damping 0.08–0.12 per frame at 60 fps.\n- [ ] Hover states that cost light, not colour change.\n- [ ] Focus rings that match the brand (a11y is part of luxury).\n- [ ] Text never overlaps a bright specular highlight (add a 10 % scrim or move the beat).\n- [ ] Number formatting, ligatures, tabular figures on readouts.\n- [ ] A 404 that is on-brand and has a way home.\n- [ ] Tab title changes per chapter (\"— Chapter 3 · The ring\").\n- [ ] The URL hash updates per chapter (`#/chapter-3`) and deep-links seek the playhead.\n\n## D · Audio (optional, off by default)\n- 90–150 s ambient bed + 3 UI one-shots (hover tick, chapter swell, CTA hit), 128 kbps, preloaded after interaction.\n- Toggle top-right, remembered in `localStorage`. **Never autoplay.** Crossfade the bed's low-pass with scroll velocity for free drama.\n\n## E · Mobile is a different edit\n- Re-cut the shot list: 60 % of the length, taller compositions, giant type stacked in 2–3 lines (`data-fit`).\n- DPR cap 1.5, particles ÷ 3, no transmission materials, no mirror floor (fake with a gradient), bloom half-res.\n- Touch: native scroll only; pointer parallax replaced by *gyroscope-free* idle drift.\n- Test at 390×844 and 360×740. Nothing cropped, nothing colliding.\n\n## F · SEO & accessibility without killing the film\n- All story copy exists in the DOM as real headings/paragraphs (visually positioned, `aria-live` off) — crawlers and screen readers get the text.\n- A visible \"Skip the film\" link jumping to the end-state.\n- `prefers-reduced-motion`: render 5 still compositions as a vertical story with simple fades.\n- Meaningful `alt` on any plate. Contrast ≥ 4.5:1 on all copy against its *actual* backdrop at that scroll position (audit at 3 positions).\n\n## G · Conversion\n- ONE primary action; it appears in the last frame AND as a quiet persistent pill after chapter 3.\n- The end-state includes social proof placed *after* desire is built: 1 testimonial, 3 numbers, 4 logos.\n- Forms: 3 fields max; success state is part of the world (a light pulse), not an alert().\n- Add tracking hooks as `data-evt` attributes (`chapter_enter`, `cta_click`, `scroll_depth`) — leave analytics wiring documented.\n\n## H · The review you give yourself (be brutal)\n1. Poster test: pause on any frame — is it Instagram-worthy?\n2. Squint test: is there a clear value hierarchy (subject ≫ type ≫ rest)?\n3. Silence test: mute your brain — does the *motion alone* tell the story?\n4. Competitor test: would this look weird on a competitor's domain? (It should.)\n5. Grandma test: can someone scroll it with a trackpad and never feel lost?\n6. Price test: if this were an invoice for $10,000, would you feel guilty? If yes → another pass.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/15-shader-recipes.md":"# 15 · Shader & effect recipes (copy-paste, then art-direct)\n\nAll GLSL targets WebGL2 / three r170+. Every recipe lists *where it belongs* — effects without a story get deleted.\n\n## 1 · Fresnel rim (cheap, universal)\n```glsl\n// fragment\nfloat fres = pow(1.0 - saturate(dot(normalize(vNormalW), normalize(vViewDir))), 3.0);\nvec3 col = base + rimColor * fres * uRim;\n```\nUse on: silhouettes in dark scenes, ghostly forms, holographic panels.\n\n## 2 · Particle morph (the \"Liquid Particles\" signature)\nPack N target positions into `DataTexture`s (or attributes `aA`, `aB`). Blend in the vertex shader with a curl-noise detour so the particles *stream* instead of linearly lerp:\n```glsl\nuniform float uMix;            // 0..1 scroll-driven, eased\nuniform float uTime;\nattribute vec3 aTarget;        // shape B (position = shape A)\nattribute float aSeed;\nvec3 curl(vec3 p){ /* 3 offset snoise samples, cross() */ }\nvoid main(){\n  float t = smoothstep(0.0, 1.0, clamp(uMix * 1.4 - aSeed * 0.4, 0.0, 1.0));   // staggered start per particle\n  vec3 detour = curl(position * 0.6 + uTime * 0.1) * sin(t * 3.14159) * 1.8;   // arc away mid-flight\n  vec3 pos = mix(position, aTarget, t) + detour;\n  vec4 mv = modelViewMatrix * vec4(pos, 1.0);\n  gl_PointSize = uSize * (1.0 / -mv.z) * mix(1.0, 1.8, sin(t * 3.14159));\n  gl_Position = projectionMatrix * mv;\n}\n```\nSample target shapes from: a mesh surface (`MeshSurfaceSampler`), text (`canvas → pixels → positions`), SDF functions, or a GLB. 120–300k points at 60 fps with `gl.POINTS` + additive; cut to 40k on mobile.\n\n## 3 · Dissolve (objects burn/erode away)\n```glsl\nfloat n = fbm(vWorldPos * 2.0);\nfloat edge = smoothstep(uProg - 0.04, uProg, n);\nif (n < uProg - 0.04) discard;\ncol += emberColor * (1.0 - edge) * 4.0;      // glowing edge → bloom picks it up\n```\nUse on: transitions between hero forms, a chapter swap, \"ignition\".\n\n## 4 · Raymarched hero (SDF) — one full-screen quad\nUse when the hero is an organic/impossible form (blobs, metaballs, fractal cores, liquid chrome). 64–96 steps, 2–3 smooth-union spheres + domain-warped noise, env reflection by sampling the `scene.environment` cube (pass as uniform) with the normal. Render at 0.5–0.75 resolution and upsample behind type. See `10-frontier-techniques.md §1`.\n\n## 5 · Liquid chrome surface\n`MeshPhysicalMaterial({ metalness:1, roughness:0.04 })` on a high-res icosphere/plane whose vertex shader (via `onBeforeCompile`) adds `fbm(position*1.2 + t)` along the normal, and recomputes the normal by finite differences. The *environment map is the entire look* — use `createStudioEnv` with colourful strips (magenta/cyan) for oil-slick chrome.\n\n## 6 · Heat-haze / refraction distortion (post)\nAdd a `ShaderPass` before output: offset UV by `normalTex(uv*3 + t*0.05).xy * strength * mask`, where `mask` is a low-res render of \"hot\" objects (or just the lower 30 % of the frame for horizon shimmer). Strength ≤ 0.004 — more reads as underwater.\n\n## 7 · Caustics on floors (light-mode glass worlds)\nProject a scrolling, twice-sampled voronoi `abs(sin)` pattern onto the floor material's `emissiveMap` with additive 0.15 intensity. 1 draw call, huge perceived realism.\n\n## 8 · Volumetric light cheat (when `createLightShafts` isn't enough)\nStack 3 additive, camera-facing, noise-modulated cone meshes (`depthWrite:false`), intensity tied to the angle between camera and light direction (`pow(dot(view, lightDir), 6)`). Fog colour = shaft colour × 0.3.\n\n## 9 · Holographic panel\nFresnel rim + horizontal scanline `sin(vUv.y * 600. + t*5.)*0.04` + chromatic offset on edges + `uFlicker` rare spike. Always carry *real* content (UI, numbers) — the effect is garnish.\n\n## 10 · Depth-of-field *feel* (without a DOF pass)\nNear sprites: large soft discs (`createGlow`) at z closer than camera+1.5, alpha .12–.3, slowly drifting opposite to the camera; far practicals: bokeh discs (hexagonal sprite texture) sized by distance. Looks like a real lens at 1 % of the cost.\n\n## 11 · Grade (LUT-lite)\nUse the engine's `grade` (contrast, saturation, shadows/highlights tint). Tint shadows toward the *complement* of the accent (teal shadows / amber highlights; indigo / coral; violet / lime). That one choice is 30 % of \"cinematic\".\n\n## 12 · Anti-banding\nAdd noise ≥ 0.035 in the final pass and render dark gradients with 10-bit `HalfFloatType` targets (the engine already does for the composer). Visible banding in a dark sky = instant \"cheap\".\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/references/16-modes-world-folio-hybrid.md":"# 16 · Modes — World, Folio, Hybrid (and which to pick)\n\nAURA builds two fundamentally different kinds of site. Choosing the wrong one is the most common reason a result feels generic.\n\n| | **WORLD** (environment) | **FOLIO** (editorial gallery in depth) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| The subject is… | a place, an object, an event, a feeling | a body of work, a roster, a sequence of proofs |\n| Camera | one continuous journey *through* a place | a climb / orbit / dolly *between* plates |\n| Hero | the environment + one hero object | the work itself (screens, photos, cases) |\n| Light | designed sky, key/rim, practicals | the plates are the light; one travelling accent |\n| Type | giant world-occluded title | giant name + one-line chapter per project |\n| Starter reference | `?world=halo` · `umbra` · default (Obsidian Gate) | `?world=folio` (Mira) |\n| Best for | products, estates, events, games, brands | portfolios, studios, agencies, case-study sites, menus, lookbooks |\n| Failure mode | pretty void with nothing to read | a carousel with no depth or rhythm |\n\n**Rule:** if the visitor's job is to *judge the work*, use FOLIO. If it is to *feel a place or want an object*, use WORLD. A product with a feature tour is WORLD. A studio with six case studies is FOLIO. **HYBRID** = a WORLD for the first 60 % (the promise) that resolves into a FOLIO helix for the proof (the last 40 %) — e.g. a hotel: arrive through the estate, then six rooms as plates.\n\n## The FOLIO recipe (see `starter/src/aura/scene.folio.ts`, ~150 lines, no assets)\n\n1. **N plates on a helix** around a spine of light. `plateAt(i)`, `ang(i)`, `RISE` define the spiral; `folioAt(i)` is the playhead position where plate *i* is centred. Six is the sweet spot (four to eight).\n2. **Two shots per plate: arrive + HOLD.** The hold is a second shot with the *same look-at and a ~2 % position drift* — the camera all but stops while the work is read. Hold duration ≈ 0.07 of the playhead. This is what makes it feel authored, not scrolled.\n3. **Distance by frame height, not by feel.** At vertical FOV 30° a plate of height *h* fills ≈ 70 % of the frame at distance ≈ 3 *h*. (A first draft at distance 1.8 *h* cropped every plate — the audit contact sheet caught it.)\n4. **Only the active plate is lit.** `focus = 1 − |p − folioAt(i)| / 0.11` drives plate brightness, edge opacity, ring opacity and scale. A single point light travels to the active plate and takes its hue. Sleeping plates stay at ~28 % so the spiral is still legible.\n5. **Chapters = the holds.** The DOM chapter for project *i* is `data-a = folioAt(i) − 0.045`, `data-b = folioAt(i) + 0.045`, alternating left/right. Never let two chapters overlap (the audit's collision check will fail).\n6. **Finish with a pull-back and a held final frame**: the whole spiral in one image, CTA live on top. Never end on a plate.\n7. **Real work replaces `plateTexture()`** with `loadTexture(\"/work/x.jpg\")` (or a looping `<video>` texture). The fake UI is a stand-in — shipping it is a failure.\n8. Depth planes: foreground dust that lags the lens, far motes, tethers + rings (mid), the mirror-ish floor (back), fog (atmosphere). Five planes, as ever.\n\n## Tooling notes (learned by running the starter headlessly)\n\n- `node scripts/audit.mjs <url>` now lives in `starter/scripts/` (it used to sit outside the starter, so `npm run audit` could not find it).\n- Software GL is slow: use `--scale 0.5 --stops 8 --only desktop` for a fast first pass, `--timeout 180000` on slow boxes, and run the full three-viewport pass last.\n- No root / missing system libs? Point `AURA_CHROME=/path/to/chromium` at any Chromium build (e.g. `@sparticuz/chromium`, with its bundled libs on `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`).\n- Small viewports (`--scale 0.5`) over-report text collisions. Re-check any BLOCKER at full size before restructuring a chapter.\n- The audit only measures; it cannot see that a plate is cropped or that a frame is empty-but-bright. **Always open `contact-desktop.png`.**\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/index.html":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#03050b\" />\n    <title>AURA starter</title>\n    <style>\n      html, body { margin: 0; background: #03050b; }\n      body { overflow-x: hidden; -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased; }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.tsx\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/package.json":"{\n  \"name\": \"aura-starter\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"vite\",\n    \"build\": \"tsc --noEmit && vite build\",\n    \"preview\": \"vite preview\",\n    \"audit\": \"node scripts/audit.mjs http://localhost:5173\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"react\": \"^19.0.0\",\n    \"react-dom\": \"^19.0.0\",\n    \"three\": \"^0.170.0\",\n    \"@fontsource-variable/space-grotesk\": \"^5.2.0\",\n    \"@fontsource/jetbrains-mono\": \"^5.2.0\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"@types/react\": \"^19.0.0\",\n    \"@types/react-dom\": \"^19.0.0\",\n    \"@types/three\": \"^0.170.0\",\n    \"@vitejs/plugin-react\": \"^5.0.0\",\n    \"typescript\": \"^5.6.0\",\n    \"vite\": \"^7.0.0\",\n    \"playwright\": \"^1.50.0\",\n    \"pngjs\": \"^7.0.0\"\n  }\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/scripts/audit.mjs":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · audit.mjs — the QA gate an agent can actually run.\n\n   npm i -D playwright pngjs && npx playwright install chromium\n   node scripts/audit.mjs http://localhost:5173 [--out shots] [--stops 14] [--gpu] [--only desktop] [--scale 0.5] [--timeout 120000]\n\n   What it does (deterministically — it uses window.__aura.seek(p), not scroll timing):\n     1. loads the page at 3 viewports (desktop 1440×900 · mobile 390×844 · wide 1920×1080)\n     2. seeks the playhead to N stops, screenshots each, and MEASURES every frame\n        (luminance, contrast, blown highlights, crushed blacks, edge energy, frame-to-frame change)\n     3. reads the DOM chapters at every stop → copy that collides, copy that leaves the viewport,\n        an empty opening frame, an empty / CTA-less closing frame\n     4. lints the shot list (covers 0→1, has a HOLD, wide/close alternation, no teleports)\n     5. reloads with WebGL disabled and with prefers-reduced-motion → proves the fallbacks\n     6. writes  <out>/REPORT.md  <out>/report.json  <out>/contact-<viewport>.png  and exits 1 if it finds BLOCKERS.\n\n   It cannot judge taste. After it passes, OPEN contact-desktop.png and critique it like an art director (references/08).\n   NOTE: software GL (no --gpu) gives real pixels but meaningless FPS — only trust fps with --gpu on a real machine.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\nimport { chromium } from \"playwright\";\nimport { PNG } from \"pngjs\";\nimport { mkdirSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\n\nconst argv = process.argv.slice(2);\nconst flag = (n, d) => { const i = argv.indexOf(`--${n}`); return i < 0 ? d : argv[i + 1] && !argv[i + 1].startsWith(\"--\") ? argv[i + 1] : true; };\nconst url = argv.find((a) => /^https?:/.test(a)) ?? \"http://localhost:5173\";\nconst out = flag(\"out\", \"shots\");\nconst N = Number(flag(\"stops\", 14));\nconst gpu = !!flag(\"gpu\", false);\nconst only = flag(\"only\", null);\n// --scale 0.5 halves every viewport (4× faster on software GL); --timeout raises the per-action limit for slow machines\nconst SCALE = Number(flag(\"scale\", 1));\nconst TIMEOUT = Number(flag(\"timeout\", 120000));\nconst VIEWPORTS = [{ n: \"desktop\", w: 1440, h: 900 }, { n: \"mobile\", w: 390, h: 844 }, { n: \"wide\", w: 1920, h: 1080 }].filter((v) => !only || v.n === only).map((v) => ({ ...v, w: Math.round(v.w * SCALE), h: Math.round(v.h * SCALE) }));\nconst stops = Array.from({ length: N }, (_, i) => +(i / (N - 1)).toFixed(4)).map((p) => (p >= 1 ? 1 : p));\nmkdirSync(out, { recursive: true });\n\nconst issues = []; // { level: \"BLOCKER\" | \"WARN\", vp, p, code, msg }\nconst add = (level, vp, p, code, msg) => issues.push({ level, vp, p, code, msg });\n\n/* ── image metrics (on a 1/4 downsample) ── */\nfunction metrics(png) {\n  const { width: W, height: H, data } = png;\n  const step = 4, lum = [];\n  let hi = 0, lo = 0, edge = 0, n = 0, sat = 0;\n  for (let y = 0; y < H; y += step) for (let x = 0; x < W; x += step) {\n    const i = (y * W + x) * 4, r = data[i] / 255, g = data[i + 1] / 255, b = data[i + 2] / 255;\n    const l = 0.2126 * r + 0.7152 * g + 0.0722 * b;\n    lum.push(l); n++;\n    if (l > 0.97) hi++;\n    if (l < 0.02) lo++;\n    sat += Math.max(r, g, b) - Math.min(r, g, b);\n    if (x + step < W) { const j = (y * W + x + step) * 4; edge += Math.abs(l - (0.2126 * data[j] + 0.7152 * data[j + 1] + 0.0722 * data[j + 2]) / 255); }\n  }\n  const mean = lum.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / n;\n  const sd = Math.sqrt(lum.reduce((a, b) => a + (b - mean) ** 2, 0) / n);\n  return { mean, sd, blown: hi / n, crushed: lo / n, edge: edge / n, sat: sat / n, small: { lum, W: Math.ceil(W / step), H: Math.ceil(H / step) } };\n}\nconst delta = (a, b) => { let s = 0; const L = Math.min(a.length, b.length); for (let i = 0; i < L; i++) s += Math.abs(a[i] - b[i]); return (s / L) * 100; };\n\nfunction sheet(pngs, cols, tw, file) {\n  const th = Math.round((tw * pngs[0].height) / pngs[0].width), rows = Math.ceil(pngs.length / cols);\n  const o = new PNG({ width: cols * tw, height: rows * th });\n  pngs.forEach((im, k) => {\n    const ox = (k % cols) * tw, oy = Math.floor(k / cols) * th;\n    for (let y = 0; y < th; y++) for (let x = 0; x < tw; x++) {\n      const si = (Math.floor((y * im.height) / th) * im.width + Math.floor((x * im.width) / tw)) * 4, di = ((oy + y) * o.width + ox + x) * 4;\n      o.data[di] = im.data[si]; o.data[di + 1] = im.data[si + 1]; o.data[di + 2] = im.data[si + 2]; o.data[di + 3] = 255;\n    }\n  });\n  writeFileSync(file, PNG.sync.write(o));\n}\n\nconst ARGS = gpu ? [\"--ignore-gpu-blocklist\", \"--enable-webgl\"] : [\"--use-gl=angle\", \"--use-angle=swiftshader\", \"--enable-unsafe-swiftshader\", \"--ignore-gpu-blocklist\", \"--enable-webgl\"];\n// AURA_CHROME=/path/to/chromium lets locked-down sandboxes (no root, missing system libs) use any Chromium build\nconst launch = () => chromium.launch({ args: ARGS, executablePath: process.env.AURA_CHROME || undefined }); // a fresh browser per viewport: software GL leaks memory across big canvases\nlet browser;\nconst frames = {}, meta = {};\n\n/* ── 1–4 · the main pass ── */\nfor (const vp of VIEWPORTS) {\n  browser = await launch();\n  const page = await browser.newPage({ viewport: { width: vp.w, height: vp.h }, deviceScaleFactor: 1 });\n  page.setDefaultTimeout(TIMEOUT);\n  const logs = [];\n  page.on(\"console\", (m) => (m.type() === \"error\" || (m.type() === \"warning\" && /\\[aura\\]/.test(m.text()))) && logs.push(m.text()));\n  page.on(\"pageerror\", (e) => logs.push(\"pageerror: \" + e.message));\n  await page.goto(url, { waitUntil: \"networkidle\" });\n  try { await page.waitForFunction(() => window.__aura, null, { timeout: 25000 }); }\n  catch { add(\"BLOCKER\", vp.n, null, \"no-engine\", \"window.__aura never appeared — WebGL failed to start or the experience did not mount. Check console output.\"); await browser.close(); continue; }\n  await page.waitForTimeout(1800); // loader wipe\n\n  const info = await page.evaluate(() => window.__aura.info());\n  meta[vp.n] = { shots: info.shots, size: info.size };\n  const chapters = await page.evaluate(() => [...document.querySelectorAll(\"[data-a]\")].map((e) => ({ a: +e.dataset.a, b: +e.dataset.b, fade: +(e.dataset.fade ?? 0.05), text: (e.textContent || \"\").trim().replace(/\\s+/g, \" \"), words: ((e.querySelector(\"h1,h2,h3\")?.textContent || e.textContent || \"\").trim().split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean).length), giant: e.classList.contains(\"aura-giant\") })));\n  meta[vp.n].chapters = chapters;\n\n  const rows = [];\n  for (const p of stops) {\n    await page.evaluate((q) => window.__aura.seek(q), p);\n    await page.evaluate(() => new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(() => requestAnimationFrame(r))));\n    await page.waitForTimeout(gpu ? 120 : 380);\n    const dom = await page.evaluate(() => {\n      const vw = innerWidth, vh = innerHeight;\n      const vis = [...document.querySelectorAll(\"[data-a]\")].filter((e) => getComputedStyle(e).visibility !== \"hidden\" && +getComputedStyle(e).opacity > 0.35)\n        .map((e) => { const rg = document.createRange(); rg.selectNodeContents(e); const r = rg.getBoundingClientRect(); /* measure the TEXT, not the (often full-width) element box */ return { t: (e.textContent || \"\").trim().slice(0, 28), x: r.left, y: r.top, r: r.right, b: r.bottom, giant: e.classList.contains(\"aura-giant\"), cta: !!e.querySelector(\"a,button\") }; });\n      const links = [...document.querySelectorAll(\".aura-overlay a, .aura-overlay button\")].filter((e) => { const s = getComputedStyle(e); const w = e.closest(\"[data-a]\"); return s.visibility !== \"hidden\" && (!w || +getComputedStyle(w).opacity > 0.5); }).length;\n      return { vw, vh, vis, links };\n    });\n    const buf = await page.screenshot({ path: `${out}/${vp.n}-${String(Math.round(p * 100)).padStart(3, \"0\")}.png` });\n    const png = PNG.sync.read(buf);\n    const m = metrics(png);\n    rows.push({ p, ...m, dom, png });\n    frames[vp.n] = frames[vp.n] ?? [];\n    frames[vp.n].push(png);\n  }\n\n  /* per-frame checks */\n  rows.forEach((r, i) => {\n    const tag = `p=${r.p}`;\n    if (r.mean < 0.035) add(i === 0 ? \"BLOCKER\" : \"WARN\", vp.n, r.p, \"dark\", `frame is nearly black (mean luma ${r.mean.toFixed(3)}) — expose, add a rim/practical, or this is a loader leak`);\n    if (r.mean > 0.62) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, r.p, \"bright\", `frame is very bright (${r.mean.toFixed(2)}) — the genre wants light with intent; lower exposure, raise bloom threshold`);\n    if (r.sd < 0.045 && r.mean > 0.02) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, r.p, \"flat\", `flat frame (contrast σ ${r.sd.toFixed(3)}) — add a rim light, depth separation or atmosphere`);\n    if (r.blown > 0.025) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, r.p, \"blown\", `${(r.blown * 100).toFixed(1)}% of pixels clipped to white — bloom threshold too low or emissive too hot`);\n    if (r.edge < 0.004 && r.mean > 0.02) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, r.p, \"empty\", `almost no detail (edge energy ${r.edge.toFixed(4)}) — the frame is empty; where is the subject?`);\n    const { vis, vw, vh, links } = r.dom;\n    for (const e of vis) {\n      if (e.x < -2 || e.r > vw + 2 + (e.giant ? 0 : 0) || e.y < -2 || e.b > vh + 2) add(e.giant ? \"BLOCKER\" : \"WARN\", vp.n, r.p, \"clipped-copy\", `“${e.t}” leaves the viewport (${Math.round(e.x)},${Math.round(e.y)} → ${Math.round(e.r)},${Math.round(e.b)} of ${vw}×${vh}) — use data-fit for giant type, check mobile layout`);\n    }\n    for (let a = 0; a < vis.length; a++) for (let b = a + 1; b < vis.length; b++) {\n      const A = vis[a], B = vis[b];\n      const ox = Math.min(A.r, B.r) - Math.max(A.x, B.x), oy = Math.min(A.b, B.b) - Math.max(A.y, B.y);\n      if (ox > 6 && oy > 6) add(\"BLOCKER\", vp.n, r.p, \"collision\", `“${A.t}” overlaps “${B.t}” by ${Math.round(ox)}×${Math.round(oy)}px — re-position or re-window the chapters`);\n    }\n    if (i === 0 && vis.length === 0) add(\"BLOCKER\", vp.n, r.p, \"empty-open\", \"opening frame has no type on screen — chapter 1 must start at data-a=0 (it is visible in frame one)\");\n    if (i === rows.length - 1) {\n      if (vis.length === 0) add(\"BLOCKER\", vp.n, r.p, \"empty-close\", \"closing frame has no copy — the last chapter must end at data-b=1 and STAY\");\n      else if (links === 0) add(\"BLOCKER\", vp.n, r.p, \"no-cta\", \"closing frame has no visible link/button — the final image needs ONE obvious action (.aura-cta inside .aura-hit)\");\n    }\n    void tag;\n  });\n  /* motion between stops */\n  for (let i = 1; i < rows.length; i++) {\n    const d = delta(rows[i - 1].small.lum, rows[i].small.lum);\n    rows[i].delta = d;\n    if (d < 0.35) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, rows[i].p, \"dead-zone\", `frame barely changes from p=${rows[i - 1].p} (Δ${d.toFixed(2)}) — fine if this is a deliberate HOLD, otherwise the scroll is dead here`);\n    if (d > 26) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, rows[i].p, \"jump-cut\", `frame changes violently from p=${rows[i - 1].p} (Δ${d.toFixed(1)}) — a cut, not a glide. Check shot spacing / teleporting position`);\n  }\n  /* shot-list lint (once) */\n  if (vp.n === VIEWPORTS[0].n) {\n    const s = info.shots, dist = (a, b) => Math.hypot(a[0] - b[0], a[1] - b[1], a[2] - b[2]);\n    if (s.length < 5) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"few-shots\", `only ${s.length} shots — a 7–10 shot rail reads as a film; 3–4 reads as a tour`);\n    if (s[0].at !== 0 || s[s.length - 1].at !== 1) add(\"BLOCKER\", vp.n, null, \"rail-range\", \"shots must start at at:0 and end at at:1\");\n    for (let i = 1; i < s.length; i++) if (s[i].at <= s[i - 1].at) add(\"BLOCKER\", vp.n, null, \"rail-order\", `shot ${i} (at ${s[i].at}) is not after shot ${i - 1}`);\n    let hold = false; for (let i = 1; i < s.length; i++) if (dist(s[i].pos, s[i - 1].pos) < 1.2 && dist(s[i].look, s[i - 1].look) < 1.2 && s[i].at - s[i - 1].at > 0.05) hold = true;\n    if (!hold) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"no-hold\", \"no HOLD found (two consecutive shots with ~identical pos+look). Every hero moment needs a stretch where the camera nearly stops\");\n    const fovs = s.map((x) => x.fov ?? 40); let swings = 0; for (let i = 1; i < fovs.length; i++) if (Math.abs(fovs[i] - fovs[i - 1]) >= 8) swings++;\n    if (swings < 2) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"no-alternation\", \"lens never changes character (fov swings < 2) — alternate wide (45+) and telephoto (≤30) shots\");\n    for (let i = 1; i < s.length; i++) if (dist(s[i].pos, s[i - 1].pos) > 70) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"teleport\", `shots ${i - 1}→${i} move ${dist(s[i].pos, s[i - 1].pos).toFixed(0)} units — over a short scroll range that will whip. Space them out or add an intermediate shot`);\n    const ch = chapters.filter((c) => !c.giant).sort((a, b) => a.a - b.a);\n    if (!ch.length) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"no-chapters\", \"no [data-a] chapters found\");\n    else {\n      if (ch[0].a > 0.001) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"late-open\", `first chapter starts at ${ch[0].a}, not 0`);\n      if (ch[ch.length - 1].b < 0.999) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"early-close\", `last chapter ends at ${ch[ch.length - 1].b}, not 1`);\n      let reach = 0; for (const c of ch) { if (c.a - reach > 0.2) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"silence\", `no copy between p=${reach.toFixed(2)} and ${c.a.toFixed(2)} (${((c.a - reach) * 100).toFixed(0)}% of the film) — intentional silence is good up to ~15%`); reach = Math.max(reach, c.b); }\n      ch.forEach((c) => c.words > 14 && add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"wordy\", `chapter “${c.text.slice(0, 30)}…” headline has ${c.words} words — max ~12 per beat; the 3D does the talking`));\n    }\n    if (info.drawCalls > 160) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"draw-calls\", `${info.drawCalls} draw calls — instance repeated meshes, merge statics`);\n    if (info.triangles > 1.8e6) add(\"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"triangles\", `${(info.triangles / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M triangles — decimate / LOD`);\n  }\n  meta[vp.n].rows = rows.map(({ p, mean, sd, blown, crushed, edge, sat, delta: d }) => ({ p, mean: +mean.toFixed(3), sd: +sd.toFixed(3), blown: +blown.toFixed(4), crushed: +crushed.toFixed(3), edge: +edge.toFixed(4), sat: +sat.toFixed(3), delta: d === undefined ? null : +d.toFixed(2) }));\n  meta[vp.n].fps = (await page.evaluate(() => window.__aura.info().fps));\n  logs.forEach((l) => add(/pageerror|Error/i.test(l) ? \"BLOCKER\" : \"WARN\", vp.n, null, \"console\", l.slice(0, 220)));\n  sheet(frames[vp.n], vp.n === \"mobile\" ? 7 : 4, vp.n === \"mobile\" ? 190 : 420, `${out}/contact-${vp.n}.png`);\n  await browser.close();\n}\n\n/* ── 5 · resilience: no WebGL, reduced motion ── */\n{\n  browser = await launch();\n  const ctx = await browser.newContext({ viewport: { width: 1280, height: 800 } });\n  await ctx.addInitScript(() => { const g = HTMLCanvasElement.prototype.getContext; HTMLCanvasElement.prototype.getContext = function (t, ...a) { return /webgl/i.test(t) ? null : g.call(this, t, ...a); }; });\n  const pg = await ctx.newPage(); const errs = []; pg.on(\"pageerror\", (e) => errs.push(e.message));\n  await pg.goto(url, { waitUntil: \"networkidle\" }); await pg.waitForTimeout(1500);\n  const body = await pg.evaluate(() => ({ text: document.body.innerText.trim().length, loaderUp: !!document.querySelector(\".aura-loader:not(.is-done)\"), poster: !!document.querySelector(\".aura-poster\") }));\n  if (errs.length) add(\"BLOCKER\", \"no-webgl\", null, \"fallback-crash\", \"page throws without WebGL: \" + errs[0].slice(0, 160));\n  else if (body.loaderUp) add(\"BLOCKER\", \"no-webgl\", null, \"fallback-stuck\", \"without WebGL the loader never clears — the visitor sees a permanent loading screen\");\n  else if (body.text < 20 && !body.poster) add(\"WARN\", \"no-webgl\", null, \"fallback-empty\", \"without WebGL the page is empty — pass a poster image and keep copy in the DOM\");\n  await ctx.close();\n  const rm = await browser.newContext({ viewport: { width: 1280, height: 800 }, reducedMotion: \"reduce\" });\n  const pr = await rm.newPage(); const e2 = []; pr.on(\"pageerror\", (e) => e2.push(e.message));\n  await pr.goto(url, { waitUntil: \"networkidle\" }); await pr.waitForTimeout(2500);\n  if (e2.length) add(\"BLOCKER\", \"reduced-motion\", null, \"rm-crash\", e2[0].slice(0, 160));\n  await rm.close();\n}\nawait browser.close();\n\n/* ── score (the half a machine can judge) ── */\nconst vpm = VIEWPORTS[0].n, rowsD = meta[vpm]?.rows ?? [];\nconst cnt = (code, vp) => issues.filter((i) => i.code === code && (!vp || i.vp === vp)).length;\nconst blockers = issues.filter((i) => i.level === \"BLOCKER\").length;\nconst frac = (bad) => Math.max(0, 1 - bad / Math.max(1, rowsD.length));\nconst atmosphere = Math.round(25 * (frac(cnt(\"flat\", vpm) + cnt(\"empty\", vpm)) * 0.4 + frac(cnt(\"blown\", vpm) + cnt(\"bright\", vpm)) * 0.2 + frac(cnt(\"dark\", vpm)) * 0.4));\nconst unbroken = Math.max(0, Math.round(20 - cnt(\"jump-cut\") * 3 - cnt(\"console\") * 4 - cnt(\"rail-range\") * 10 - cnt(\"rail-order\") * 5 - cnt(\"teleport\") * 3 - cnt(\"no-engine\") * 20));\nconst rhythm = Math.max(0, Math.round(20 - cnt(\"no-hold\") * 6 - cnt(\"no-alternation\") * 4 - cnt(\"few-shots\") * 3 - cnt(\"silence\") * 2 - cnt(\"dead-zone\", vpm) * 1.2 - cnt(\"wordy\") * 1.5 - cnt(\"empty-open\") * 6 - cnt(\"empty-close\") * 6 - cnt(\"no-cta\") * 4 - cnt(\"late-open\") * 2 - cnt(\"early-close\") * 2));\nconst craft = Math.max(0, Math.round(15 - cnt(\"collision\") * 3 - cnt(\"clipped-copy\") * 2 - cnt(\"fallback-crash\") * 8 - cnt(\"fallback-stuck\") * 8 - cnt(\"fallback-empty\") * 3 - cnt(\"draw-calls\") * 3 - cnt(\"triangles\") * 3));\nconst auto = atmosphere + unbroken + rhythm + craft;\n\nconst grouped = {};\nissues.forEach((i) => { const k = `${i.level}|${i.code}|${i.vp}`; (grouped[k] = grouped[k] ?? { ...i, ps: [] }).ps.push(i.p); });\nconst lines = Object.values(grouped).sort((a, b) => (a.level === b.level ? 0 : a.level === \"BLOCKER\" ? -1 : 1)).map((g) => `- **${g.level}** \\`${g.code}\\` · ${g.vp}${g.ps.some((p) => p !== null) ? ` · p=${g.ps.filter((p) => p !== null).join(\", \")}` : \"\"} — ${g.msg}`);\nconst md = `# AURA audit — ${url}\\n\\n**Automated score: ${auto}/80** (atmosphere ${atmosphere}/25 · unbroken ${unbroken}/20 · rhythm ${rhythm}/20 · craft ${craft}/15) · blockers: ${blockers}\\n\\n` +\n  `The remaining **20 points (Authorship)** and the real judgement of every other pillar are YOURS: open \\`${out}/contact-desktop.png\\` and \\`${out}/contact-mobile.png\\`, then critique like a harsh art director (references/08-qa-and-polish.md). Ship only when the human-judged total is ≥ 85.\\n\\n` +\n  `## Issues\\n${lines.join(\"\\n\") || \"_none_\"}\\n\\n## Per-frame (${vpm})\\n| p | luma | σ | blown | edge | Δ prev |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n${rowsD.map((r) => `| ${r.p} | ${r.mean} | ${r.sd} | ${r.blown} | ${r.edge} | ${r.delta ?? \"—\"} |`).join(\"\\n\")}\\n\\n` +\n  `Shots: ${(meta[vpm]?.shots ?? []).length} · draw-call/triangle/fps numbers are in report.json (fps only meaningful with --gpu).\\n`;\nwriteFileSync(`${out}/REPORT.md`, md);\nwriteFileSync(`${out}/report.json`, JSON.stringify({ url, auto: { total: auto, atmosphere, unbroken, rhythm, craft }, blockers, issues, meta: Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(meta).map(([k, v]) => [k, { ...v, shots: undefined, rows: v.rows }])) }, null, 2));\nconsole.log(md);\nconsole.log(`\\n→ ${out}/REPORT.md · ${out}/contact-*.png   (now LOOK at the contact sheets)`);\nprocess.exit(blockers ? 1 : 0);\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/AuraExperience.tsx":"\"use client\";\n\nimport { useEffect, useRef, useState, type ReactNode } from \"react\";\nimport * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport { mountAura, type Builder, type AuraHandle } from \"./engine\";\nimport { bindDom, fitType, trackProgress } from \"./choreo\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · AuraExperience — drop-in React shell.\n\n   <AuraExperience build={myScene} scrollVh={700} title=\"ARDMERE\">\n     <div className=\"aura-copy\" data-a=\"0.05\" data-b=\"0.30\">…chapter 1…</div>\n     <div className=\"aura-copy\" data-a=\"0.35\" data-b=\"0.60\">…chapter 2…</div>\n   </AuraExperience>\n\n   · A tall section (scrollVh) provides the scroll distance; a sticky stage holds the canvas.\n     (Sticky, not fixed → you can embed the experience anywhere in a normal page.)\n   · Children are the DOM overlay. Elements with data-a / data-b are choreographed from the SAME damped playhead as the camera.\n   · Includes: preloader (tracks real asset loading), WebGL-failure fallback, reduced-motion handling, progress HUD.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nfunction hasWebGL() {\n  try {\n    const c = document.createElement(\"canvas\");\n    return !!(c.getContext(\"webgl2\") || c.getContext(\"webgl\"));\n  } catch {\n    return false;\n  }\n}\n\nexport interface AuraExperienceProps {\n  build: Builder;\n  children?: ReactNode;\n  /** scroll distance of the whole experience, in viewport heights. 500 = short film · 800–1200 = feature */\n  scrollVh?: number;\n  title?: string;\n  /** shown if WebGL is unavailable or fails — a designed still, never a blank page */\n  poster?: string;\n  hud?: boolean;\n  className?: string;\n  onReady?: (h: AuraHandle) => void;\n}\n\nexport function AuraExperience({ build, children, scrollVh = 700, title = \"\", poster, hud = true, className = \"\", onReady }: AuraExperienceProps) {\n  const track = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const overlay = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const bar = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const [pct, setPct] = useState(0);\n  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);\n  const [failed, setFailed] = useState(false);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!hasWebGL()) { setFailed(true); return; }\n    const t = track.current!, hostEl = host.current!, ov = overlay.current!;\n    let handle: AuraHandle | null = null, dead = false;\n    const t0 = performance.now();\n    THREE.DefaultLoadingManager.onProgress = (_u, l, n) => setPct(n ? l / n : 1);\n    const update = bindDom(ov);\n    const unfit = fitType(ov);\n    mountAura(hostEl, build, { progress: trackProgress(t) })\n      .then((h) => {\n        if (dead) { h.dispose(); return; }\n        handle = h;\n        h.onFrame((f) => {\n          update(f.p);\n          if (bar.current) bar.current.style.transform = `scaleX(${f.p})`;\n        });\n        onReady?.(h);\n        // never flash: keep the loader up for at least 900ms so the entrance is a moment, not a pop\n        setTimeout(() => !dead && setReady(true), Math.max(0, 900 - (performance.now() - t0)));\n      })\n      .catch(() => !dead && setFailed(true));\n    return () => { dead = true; unfit(); handle?.dispose(); };\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, []);\n\n  return (\n    <section ref={track} className={`aura-root ${className}`} style={{ height: `${scrollVh}vh` }} aria-label={title || \"Immersive experience\"}>\n      <div className=\"aura-stage\">\n        <div ref={host} className=\"aura-canvas\" aria-hidden />\n        {failed && poster && <img src={poster} alt=\"\" className=\"aura-poster\" />}\n        <div ref={overlay} className=\"aura-overlay\">{children}</div>\n        {hud && (\n          <div className=\"aura-hud\" aria-hidden>\n            <span className=\"aura-hud-title\">{title}</span>\n            <span className=\"aura-hud-line\"><span ref={bar} className=\"aura-hud-fill\" /></span>\n            <span className=\"aura-hud-hint\">Scroll</span>\n          </div>\n        )}\n        <div className={`aura-loader ${ready || failed ? \"is-done\" : \"\"}`} aria-hidden={ready}>\n          <span className=\"aura-loader-n\">{String(Math.round(pct * 100)).padStart(3, \"0\")}</span>\n          <span className=\"aura-loader-t\">{title}</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/ExampleExperience.tsx":"\"use client\";\n\nimport { AuraExperience } from \"./AuraExperience\";\nimport { obsidianGate } from \"./scene.example\";\nimport \"./aura.css\";\n\n/* The reference page: one scene + five chapters whose [data-a, data-b] windows line up with the shot list in scene.example.ts.\n   Copy this file, rename the chapters, swap the scene. That is the whole integration. */\nexport default function ExampleExperience({ scrollVh = 720 }: { scrollVh?: number }) {\n  return (\n    <AuraExperience build={obsidianGate} scrollVh={scrollVh} title=\"Obsidian Gate\">\n      {/* shot 1 · establish (0 → 0.2): giant type, almost no copy */}\n      <div className=\"aura-giant\" data-fit=\"0.86\" data-a=\"0\" data-b=\"0.2\" data-fade=\"0.05\" aria-hidden>OBSIDIAN</div>\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-left\" data-a=\"0\" data-b=\"0.19\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Chapter 01 · Arrival</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Some places<br />you have to fall into.</h2>\n      </div>\n\n      {/* shot 2 · push-in (0.2 → 0.4) */}\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-right\" data-a=\"0.22\" data-b=\"0.4\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Chapter 02 · Approach</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Closer.</h2>\n        <p className=\"aura-p\">The camera never cuts. Every scroll tick moves one lens through one world.</p>\n      </div>\n\n      {/* shot 3+hold · macro (0.4 → 0.6) */}\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-left\" data-a=\"0.43\" data-b=\"0.62\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Chapter 03 · Detail</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Matter, lit<br />like it matters.</h2>\n        <p className=\"aura-p\">Telephoto lens, thin-film iridescence, a warm practical light that breathes.</p>\n      </div>\n\n      {/* shot 4 · crane (0.62 → 0.8) */}\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-center\" data-a=\"0.64\" data-b=\"0.84\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Chapter 04 · Scale</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\" style={{ fontSize: \"clamp(2.4rem,7vw,6.5rem)\" }}>Now see<br />all of it.</h2>\n      </div>\n\n      {/* shot 5 · FINAL IMAGE (0.88 → 1): stays on screen, one CTA */}\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-center aura-hit\" data-a=\"0.88\" data-b=\"1\" data-fade=\"0.05\" style={{ top: \"auto\", bottom: \"8vh\", translate: \"-50% 0\" }}>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\" style={{ fontSize: \"clamp(2.4rem,6vw,5.5rem)\" }}>Make yours.</h2>\n        <a className=\"aura-cta\" href=\"#start\">Start a build</a>\n      </div>\n    </AuraExperience>\n  );\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/FolioExperience.tsx":"\"use client\";\n\nimport { AuraExperience } from \"./AuraExperience\";\nimport { folio, folioAt, PROJECTS } from \"./scene.folio\";\nimport \"./aura.css\";\n\n/* World #4 — a FOLIO. One chapter per project, each locked to its HOLD in scene.folio.ts (folioAt(i) ± 0.05):\n   0–.07 name + wide · six project chapters on alternating sides · .9–1 pull-back + CTA (stays on screen). */\nexport default function FolioExperience({ scrollVh = 1000 }: { scrollVh?: number }) {\n  return (\n    <AuraExperience build={folio} scrollVh={scrollVh} title=\"Mira — folio\">\n      <div className=\"aura-giant\" data-fit=\"0.9\" data-a=\"0\" data-b=\"0.07\" data-fade=\"0.03\" aria-hidden>MIRA</div>\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-left\" data-a=\"0\" data-b=\"0.07\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Independent design studio · 2026</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Work, in the order<br />it was made.</h2>\n      </div>\n      {PROJECTS.map((pr, i) => (\n        <div key={pr.title} className={`aura-copy ${i % 2 ? \"aura-right\" : \"aura-left\"}`} data-a={(folioAt(i) - 0.045).toFixed(3)} data-b={(folioAt(i) + 0.045).toFixed(3)} data-fade=\"0.02\" style={{ bottom: \"9vh\" }}>\n          <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, \"0\")} / 06 · {pr.kind}</span>\n          <h2 className=\"aura-h\">{pr.title}</h2>\n        </div>\n      ))}\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-center aura-hit\" data-a=\"0.94\" data-b=\"1\" data-fade=\"0.04\" style={{ top: \"auto\", bottom: \"9vh\", translate: \"-50% 0\" }}>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\" style={{ fontSize: \"clamp(2.2rem,5.5vw,5rem)\" }}>Your project next.</h2>\n        <a className=\"aura-cta\" href=\"#contact\">Start a conversation</a>\n      </div>\n    </AuraExperience>\n  );\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/HaloExperience.tsx":"\"use client\";\n\nimport { AuraExperience } from \"./AuraExperience\";\nimport { halo, haloAnchors } from \"./scene.halo\";\nimport type { AuraHandle } from \"./engine\";\nimport \"./aura.css\";\n\n/* World #3 — a PRODUCT site. Chapter windows are locked to the shot list in scene.halo.ts:\n   0–.12 reveal · .12–.28 macro · .28–.42 crane + open · .42–.6 EXPLODED ASCENT (projected labels) · .6–.7 settle · .7–.82 context · .82–.92 HERO HOLD · .92–1 final + CTA. */\n\nconst LABELS = [\n  { id: \"pcb\", k: \"01 — Signal\", v: \"48 dB\", d: \"Hybrid noise cancelling\" },\n  { id: \"battery\", k: \"02 — Power\", v: \"30 h\", d: \"With the case, one charge\" },\n  { id: \"driver\", k: \"03 — Sound\", v: \"11 mm\", d: \"Graphene-coated driver\" },\n] as const;\n\n/** Projects the live 3D anchors to the screen each frame and positions the leader-line labels (no layout thrash: transform only). */\nfunction trackLabels(h: AuraHandle) {\n  const els = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(\"[data-anchor]\"));\n  h.onFrame((f) => {\n    const host = els[0]?.offsetParent as HTMLElement | null;\n    const W = host?.clientWidth ?? innerWidth, H = host?.clientHeight ?? innerHeight;\n    for (const el of els) {\n      const a = haloAnchors[el.dataset.anchor as \"battery\" | \"pcb\" | \"driver\"];\n      const v = a.clone().project(f.camera);\n      const behind = v.z > 1;\n      el.style.transform = `translate(${(v.x * 0.5 + 0.5) * W}px, ${(-v.y * 0.5 + 0.5) * H}px)`;\n      el.style.opacity = behind ? \"0\" : String(haloAnchors.k);\n    }\n  });\n}\n\nexport default function HaloExperience({ scrollVh = 1000 }: { scrollVh?: number }) {\n  return (\n    <AuraExperience build={halo} scrollVh={scrollVh} title=\"NOCTA\" onReady={trackLabels}>\n      <div className=\"aura-giant\" data-fit=\"0.9\" data-a=\"0\" data-b=\"0.16\" data-fade=\"0.05\" aria-hidden>NOCTA</div>\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-left\" data-a=\"0\" data-b=\"0.12\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Active noise cancelling · 2026</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Silence,<br />engineered.</h2>\n      </div>\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-right\" data-a=\"0.14\" data-b=\"0.27\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Finish</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Lacquer, polished<br />fourteen times.</h2>\n      </div>\n\n      {LABELS.map((l, i) => {\n        const dx = i === 1 ? -150 : 130, dy = i === 0 ? -90 : 80;\n        return (\n          <div key={l.id} className=\"halo-anchor\" data-anchor={l.id} data-a=\"0.42\" data-b=\"0.62\" data-fade=\"0.04\" style={{ position: \"absolute\", left: 0, top: 0, willChange: \"transform,opacity\" }}>\n            <i className=\"halo-line\" style={{ width: Math.hypot(dx, dy), transform: `rotate(${Math.atan2(dy, dx)}rad)` }} />\n            <div className=\"halo-label\" style={{ transform: `translate(${dx}px, ${dy}px) translate(${i === 1 ? \"-100%\" : \"0\"}, -50%)`, textAlign: i === 1 ? \"right\" : \"left\" }}>\n              <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">{l.k}</span>\n              <b>{l.v}</b>\n              <small>{l.d}</small>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        );\n      })}\n\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-left\" data-a=\"0.7\" data-b=\"0.8\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Chapter 04</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Made for<br />the quiet hours.</h2>\n      </div>\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-right\" data-a=\"0.83\" data-b=\"0.92\">\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">30 hours.<br />One charge.</h2>\n      </div>\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-center aura-hit\" data-a=\"0.93\" data-b=\"1\" data-fade=\"0.04\" style={{ top: \"auto\", bottom: \"8vh\", translate: \"-50% 0\" }}>\n        <a className=\"aura-cta\" href=\"#preorder\">Pre-order — ships March</a>\n      </div>\n\n      <style>{`\n        .halo-label { position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; min-width: 190px; padding: 0 12px; }\n        .halo-line { position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; height: 1px; transform-origin: 0 0; background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--aura-accent), rgb(125 255 217 / 0.15)); }\n        .halo-label b { display: block; font: 600 clamp(1.8rem, 3.4vw, 3rem)/1 var(--aura-display); letter-spacing: -0.04em; margin-top: 10px; }\n        .halo-label small { display: block; margin-top: 6px; font: 500 11px/1.4 var(--aura-mono); letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--aura-dim); }\n        .halo-anchor::after { content: \"\"; position: absolute; left: -4px; top: -4px; width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--aura-accent); box-shadow: 0 0 14px var(--aura-accent); }\n        :root { --aura-accent: #7dffd9; }\n        @media (max-width: 760px) { .halo-label { min-width: 130px; } .halo-label small { display: none; } }\n      `}</style>\n    </AuraExperience>\n  );\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/UmbraExperience.tsx":"\"use client\";\n\nimport { AuraExperience } from \"./AuraExperience\";\nimport { umbra } from \"./scene.umbra\";\nimport \"./aura.css\";\n\n/* World #2 — an EVENT site. Chapter windows are locked to the shot list in scene.umbra.ts:\n   0–.2 establish · .2–.4 walk · .4–.56 hero + HOLD · .56–.76 pass · .76–.9 crane · .9–1 final frame (stays on screen, CTA live). */\nexport default function UmbraExperience({ scrollVh = 900 }: { scrollVh?: number }) {\n  return (\n    <AuraExperience build={umbra} scrollVh={scrollVh} title=\"Umbra\">\n      <div className=\"aura-giant\" data-fit=\"0.86\" data-a=\"0\" data-b=\"0.2\" data-fade=\"0.05\" aria-hidden>UMBRA</div>\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-left\" data-a=\"0\" data-b=\"0.19\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">21 · 08 · Mojave</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">The day the desert<br />goes quiet.</h2>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-right\" data-a=\"0.22\" data-b=\"0.38\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Chapter 02 · The walk</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Walk toward<br />the dark.</h2>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-left\" data-a=\"0.43\" data-b=\"0.6\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Chapter 03 · Totality</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Four minutes.<br />No sun. No signal.</h2>\n        <p className=\"aura-p\">One monolith, three thousand people, a single shared silence.</p>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-right\" data-a=\"0.64\" data-b=\"0.84\">\n        <span className=\"aura-eyebrow\">Chapter 04 · The ring</span>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\">Light comes back<br />at the edge.</h2>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"aura-copy aura-center aura-hit\" data-a=\"0.9\" data-b=\"1\" data-fade=\"0.05\" style={{ top: \"auto\", bottom: \"9vh\", translate: \"-50% 0\" }}>\n        <h2 className=\"aura-h\" style={{ fontSize: \"clamp(2.4rem,6vw,5.5rem)\" }}>Be there.</h2>\n        <a className=\"aura-cta\" href=\"#reserve\">Reserve a place</a>\n      </div>\n    </AuraExperience>\n  );\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/assets.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport { GLTFLoader, type GLTF } from \"three/examples/jsm/loaders/GLTFLoader.js\";\nimport { DRACOLoader } from \"three/examples/jsm/loaders/DRACOLoader.js\";\nimport { MeshoptDecoder } from \"three/examples/jsm/libs/meshopt_decoder.module.js\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · assets.ts — load compressed GLB models.\n   Compress BEFORE shipping (see references/05-asset-pipeline.md):\n     npx gltf-transform optimize in.glb out.glb --compress meshopt --texture-compress webp\n   Budget: hero model ≤ 3 MB, whole experience ≤ 12 MB on first load (stream the rest after the intro).\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nlet gltf: GLTFLoader | null = null;\nfunction loader() {\n  if (gltf) return gltf;\n  const draco = new DRACOLoader().setDecoderPath(\"https://www.gstatic.com/draco/versioned/decoders/1.5.7/\");\n  gltf = new GLTFLoader().setDRACOLoader(draco).setMeshoptDecoder(MeshoptDecoder);\n  return gltf;\n}\n\nexport const loadGLB = (url: string): Promise<GLTF> => loader().loadAsync(url);\n\n/** Centre a model on the origin and scale it so its largest dimension equals `size` world units. */\nexport function fitModel(obj: THREE.Object3D, size = 2) {\n  const box = new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(obj);\n  const c = box.getCenter(new THREE.Vector3());\n  const s = size / Math.max(...box.getSize(new THREE.Vector3()).toArray());\n  obj.position.sub(c);\n  const wrap = new THREE.Group();\n  wrap.add(obj);\n  wrap.scale.setScalar(s);\n  return wrap;\n}\n\n/** Give every mesh in a loaded model correct env intensity + shadow flags in one call. */\nexport function prepModel(obj: THREE.Object3D, envIntensity = 1.2) {\n  obj.traverse((o) => {\n    const m = o as THREE.Mesh;\n    if (!m.isMesh) return;\n    m.castShadow = m.receiveShadow = true;\n    const mat = m.material as THREE.MeshStandardMaterial;\n    if (mat && \"envMapIntensity\" in mat) mat.envMapIntensity = envIntensity;\n  });\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/atmosphere.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · atmosphere.ts — the air between the camera and the world.\n   Depth planes 4 and 5 of the doctrine (background + atmosphere) live here:\n\n     createSky()          gradient dome · sun / ECLIPSE disc with corona · stars · horizon haze  (1 draw call, fog-immune)\n     createLightShafts()  volumetric god-rays — soft additive cones that breathe and fade at the edges\n     createGlow()         a billboarded radial glow (lens bloom stand-in, practical-light halo, sun flare)\n     createHaze()         layered fog cards: the cheap way to get *banded* depth in a valley / street / forest\n\n   Everything is additive or fog-immune and depthWrite:false, so it never fights the subject and costs almost nothing.\n   Drive uniforms from AuraScene.update(f) using the playhead — a sky that never changes across 800vh is a still image.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\ntype Col = THREE.ColorRepresentation;\n\nconst NOISE = /* glsl */ `\n  float luma(vec3 c){ return dot(c, vec3(.2126, .7152, .0722)); }\n  float h21(vec2 p){ p = fract(p * vec2(123.34, 456.21)); p += dot(p, p + 45.32); return fract(p.x * p.y); }\n  float vn(vec2 p){ vec2 i = floor(p), f = fract(p); f = f*f*(3.-2.*f);\n    return mix(mix(h21(i), h21(i+vec2(1,0)), f.x), mix(h21(i+vec2(0,1)), h21(i+vec2(1,1)), f.x), f.y); }\n  float fbm(vec2 p){ float v = 0., a = .5; for(int i=0;i<4;i++){ v += vn(p)*a; p *= 2.02; a *= .5; } return v; }\n`;\n\nexport interface SkyOptions {\n  /** zenith colour */ top?: Col;\n  /** colour at the horizon line (the hottest band of the sky) */ horizon?: Col;\n  /** colour BELOW the horizon (what the terrain fades into) */ ground?: Col;\n  /** direction TO the sun/moon (normalised for you) */ sunDir?: [number, number, number];\n  sunColor?: Col;\n  /** angular radius of the disc in radians. 0.03 = sun, 0.09 = huge, 0 = none */ sunSize?: number;\n  /** 0 = open sun · 1 = totally eclipsed: a black disc ringed by a corona */ eclipse?: number;\n  stars?: number;\n  radius?: number;\n}\n\nexport function createSky(o: SkyOptions = {}) {\n  const sun = new THREE.Vector3(...(o.sunDir ?? [0.2, 0.18, -1])).normalize();\n  const uniforms = {\n    uTop: { value: new THREE.Color(o.top ?? 0x05060d) },\n    uHorizon: { value: new THREE.Color(o.horizon ?? 0xff7a3d) },\n    uGround: { value: new THREE.Color(o.ground ?? 0x05060d) },\n    uSunDir: { value: sun },\n    uSunCol: { value: new THREE.Color(o.sunColor ?? 0xffc48a) },\n    uSunSize: { value: o.sunSize ?? 0.05 },\n    uEclipse: { value: o.eclipse ?? 0 },\n    uStars: { value: o.stars ?? 0.6 },\n    uTime: { value: 0 },\n    uHaze: { value: 1 },\n  };\n  const mat = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n    uniforms,\n    side: THREE.BackSide,\n    depthWrite: false,\n    fog: false,\n    vertexShader: /* glsl */ `varying vec3 vDir; void main(){ vDir = normalize(position); vec4 p = projectionMatrix * mat4(mat3(modelViewMatrix)) * vec4(position, 1.); gl_Position = p.xyww; }`,\n    fragmentShader: /* glsl */ `\n      varying vec3 vDir;\n      uniform vec3 uTop, uHorizon, uGround, uSunDir, uSunCol; uniform float uSunSize, uEclipse, uStars, uTime, uHaze;\n      ${NOISE}\n      void main(){\n        vec3 d = normalize(vDir);\n        float y = d.y;\n        // gradient: a tight hot band at the horizon with a long falloff to the zenith\n        float up = smoothstep(-0.02, 0.8, y);\n        vec3 col = mix(uHorizon, uTop, pow(up, 0.5));\n        col = mix(col, uGround, smoothstep(0.0, -0.2, y));\n        // horizon haze, thicker toward the sun\n        float sd = max(dot(d, uSunDir), 0.);\n        col += uSunCol * pow(sd, 5.) * 0.2 * uHaze * (1. - up);\n        col += uSunCol * pow(sd, 40.) * 0.28 * (1. - uEclipse * .6);\n        // disc + corona\n        float ang = acos(clamp(dot(d, uSunDir), -1., 1.));\n        float disc = 1. - smoothstep(uSunSize * .97, uSunSize, ang);\n        float rr = max(ang - uSunSize, 0.) / uSunSize;\n        float corona = exp(-rr * 2.4) * .9 + exp(-rr * .7) * .22;\n        // streamers: the corona is not a perfect ring\n        vec2 q = d.xy - uSunDir.xy;\n        float streak = .65 + .35 * vn(vec2(atan(q.y, q.x) * 4., uTime * .12));\n        vec3 open = uSunCol * (disc * 8. + corona * 0.9);\n        vec3 eclipsed = uSunCol * corona * (1.0 + 0.8 * streak) * step(uSunSize, ang);\n        col += mix(open, eclipsed, uEclipse);\n        col = mix(col, vec3(0.), disc * uEclipse);\n        // stars: ROUND, soft, only where the sky is dark, twinkling, never below the horizon (square cells read as confetti)\n        vec2 sp = d.xz / (abs(d.y) + .35) * 70.;\n        vec2 cell = floor(sp), f = fract(sp) - .5;\n        float st = h21(cell);\n        vec2 off = (vec2(h21(cell + 7.1), h21(cell + 3.3)) - .5) * .6;\n        float star = smoothstep(.2, 0., length(f - off)) * step(.93, st) * smoothstep(0.0, .2, y) * (.55 + .45 * sin(uTime * 2. + st * 60.));\n        col += vec3(.85, .92, 1.) * star * uStars * (1. - smoothstep(.1, .4, luma(col)));\n        gl_FragColor = vec4(col, 1.);\n      }`,\n  });\n  const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(o.radius ?? 900, 48, 24), mat);\n  mesh.frustumCulled = false;\n  mesh.renderOrder = -10;\n  return {\n    mesh,\n    uniforms,\n    /** world-space position of the sun/eclipse centre at the given distance — place glows / lights here */\n    sunPosition: (dist = 400) => sun.clone().multiplyScalar(dist),\n    update(time: number) { uniforms.uTime.value = time; },\n  };\n}\n\n/* ── volumetric light shafts ─────────────────────────────────── */\nexport interface ShaftOptions {\n  /** where the beam starts (a window, a lamp, the sun) */ from: [number, number, number];\n  /** where it points */ to: [number, number, number];\n  color?: Col;\n  /** radius at the far end */ spread?: number;\n  /** number of overlapping cones (2–4 gives a layered, non-geometric look) */ count?: number;\n  intensity?: number;\n}\n\nexport function createLightShafts(o: ShaftOptions) {\n  const group = new THREE.Group();\n  const from = new THREE.Vector3(...o.from), to = new THREE.Vector3(...o.to);\n  const len = from.distanceTo(to);\n  const shared = { uTime: { value: 0 }, uIntensity: { value: o.intensity ?? 1 }, uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(o.color ?? 0xffd9a8) } };\n  const n = o.count ?? 3;\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    const mat = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      uniforms: { ...shared, uSeed: { value: i * 17.3 } },\n      transparent: true, depthWrite: false, blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending, side: THREE.DoubleSide, fog: false,\n      vertexShader: /* glsl */ `varying vec2 vUv; varying vec3 vN; varying vec3 vV;\n        void main(){ vUv = uv; vec4 mv = modelViewMatrix * vec4(position, 1.); vN = normalize(normalMatrix * normal); vV = normalize(-mv.xyz); gl_Position = projectionMatrix * mv; }`,\n      fragmentShader: /* glsl */ `varying vec2 vUv; varying vec3 vN; varying vec3 vV; uniform float uTime, uIntensity, uSeed; uniform vec3 uColor;\n        ${NOISE}\n        void main(){\n          // fresnel: brightest down the axis, melting at the silhouette → reads as a volume, not a mesh\n          float f = pow(abs(dot(normalize(vN), normalize(vV))), 1.4);\n          float along = vUv.y;                       // cylinder uv.y: 0 = bottom (far end) · 1 = top (source)\n          float fade = smoothstep(0., .35, 1. - along) * smoothstep(0., .06, along);\n          float streak = fbm(vec2(vUv.x * 12. + uSeed, along * 2.5 - uTime * .05));\n          float a = f * fade * (.3 + streak * 1.1) * uIntensity * .3;\n          gl_FragColor = vec4(uColor * a, a);\n        }`,\n    });\n    const r = (o.spread ?? 3) * (0.7 + i * 0.28);\n    const m = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.05, r, len, 40, 1, true), mat); // top = source (tiny), bottom = far end (wide)\n    m.frustumCulled = false;\n    m.renderOrder = 5;\n    m.rotation.y = i * 1.3;\n    group.add(m);\n  }\n  // the cylinder's +Y end (small) must sit at `from`\n  group.position.copy(from.clone().add(to).multiplyScalar(0.5));\n  group.quaternion.setFromUnitVectors(new THREE.Vector3(0, 1, 0), from.clone().sub(to).normalize());\n  return {\n    group,\n    uniforms: shared,\n    update(time: number) { shared.uTime.value = time; },\n    setIntensity(v: number) { shared.uIntensity.value = v; },\n  };\n}\n\n/* ── glow billboard ──────────────────────────────────────────── */\nlet glowTex: THREE.CanvasTexture | null = null;\nfunction glowTexture() {\n  if (glowTex) return glowTex;\n  const c = document.createElement(\"canvas\");\n  c.width = c.height = 256;\n  const x = c.getContext(\"2d\")!;\n  const g = x.createRadialGradient(128, 128, 0, 128, 128, 128);\n  g.addColorStop(0, \"rgba(255,255,255,1)\");\n  g.addColorStop(0.12, \"rgba(255,255,255,.55)\");\n  g.addColorStop(0.4, \"rgba(255,255,255,.12)\");\n  g.addColorStop(1, \"rgba(255,255,255,0)\");\n  x.fillStyle = g; x.fillRect(0, 0, 256, 256);\n  glowTex = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c);\n  glowTex.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n  return glowTex;\n}\n\n/** Additive halo. Use for lamps, windows, embers, the sun. `size` in world units. */\nexport function createGlow(color: Col, size = 6, opacity = 0.8) {\n  const mat = new THREE.SpriteMaterial({ map: glowTexture(), color, blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending, transparent: true, depthWrite: false, opacity, fog: false });\n  const s = new THREE.Sprite(mat);\n  s.scale.setScalar(size);\n  return s;\n}\n\n/* ── haze layers ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n/** Stacked soft fog sheets. Put 3–5 between camera and background at increasing distance; the parallax between them is depth. */\nexport function createHaze(o: { color?: Col; layers?: number; width?: number; height?: number; zStart?: number; zStep?: number; y?: number; opacity?: number } = {}) {\n  const group = new THREE.Group();\n  const n = o.layers ?? 4;\n  const mats: THREE.ShaderMaterial[] = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    const mat = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      transparent: true, depthWrite: false, fog: false,\n      uniforms: { uTime: { value: 0 }, uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(o.color ?? 0xff9a60) }, uOp: { value: (o.opacity ?? 0.22) * (1 - i * 0.12) }, uSeed: { value: i * 9.1 } },\n      vertexShader: /* glsl */ `varying vec2 vUv; void main(){ vUv = uv; gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(position, 1.); }`,\n      fragmentShader: /* glsl */ `varying vec2 vUv; uniform float uTime, uOp, uSeed; uniform vec3 uColor;\n        ${NOISE}\n        void main(){\n          float band = smoothstep(0., .45, vUv.y) * (1. - smoothstep(.5, 1., vUv.y));   // dense in the middle, melts top and bottom\n          float n = fbm(vec2(vUv.x * 3. + uTime * .01 + uSeed, vUv.y * 2.));\n          float edge = smoothstep(0., .2, vUv.x) * (1. - smoothstep(.8, 1., vUv.x));\n          gl_FragColor = vec4(uColor, band * edge * n * uOp * 1.8);\n        }`,\n    });\n    mats.push(mat);\n    const m = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(o.width ?? 160, o.height ?? 22), mat);\n    m.position.set(0, (o.y ?? 5) + i * 0.6, (o.zStart ?? -20) - i * (o.zStep ?? 24));\n    m.renderOrder = 2;\n    group.add(m);\n  }\n  return { group, update(time: number) { mats.forEach((m) => (m.uniforms.uTime.value = time)); } };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/aura.css":"/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · aura.css — structure + choreography classes. Theme it with the variables.\n   The JS (bindDom) writes --in / --out / --vis / --local on every [data-a] element each frame;\n   everything below is pure CSS reading those numbers. No per-element JS, no layout thrash.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n:root {\n  --aura-bg: #03050b;\n  --aura-fg: #eef3f7;\n  --aura-dim: rgb(238 243 247 / 0.58);\n  --aura-accent: #7fd6ff;\n  --aura-display: \"Space Grotesk Variable\", \"Inter Variable\", system-ui, sans-serif;\n  --aura-mono: \"JetBrains Mono\", ui-monospace, monospace;\n  --aura-pad: clamp(20px, 4vw, 64px);\n  --aura-ease: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);\n}\n\n.aura-root { position: relative; background: var(--aura-bg); color: var(--aura-fg); }\n.aura-stage { position: sticky; top: 0; height: 100vh; height: 100svh; overflow: hidden; }\n.aura-canvas { position: absolute; inset: 0; }\n.aura-overlay { position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none; }\n.aura-overlay .aura-hit { pointer-events: auto; }\n.aura-poster { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; }\n\n/* ── a chapter's copy block: rises in, drifts up and dissolves out. Position it with the helper classes. */\n.aura-copy {\n  position: absolute;\n  max-width: min(520px, 86vw);\n  opacity: var(--vis, 0);\n  transform: translate3d(0, calc((1 - var(--in, 0)) * 56px - var(--out, 0) * 40px), 0);\n  filter: blur(calc((1 - var(--in, 0) + var(--out, 0)) * 10px));\n  will-change: opacity, transform, filter;\n}\n.aura-left { left: var(--aura-pad); bottom: 11vh; }\n.aura-right { right: var(--aura-pad); bottom: 11vh; text-align: right; }\n.aura-center { left: 50%; top: 50%; translate: -50% -50%; text-align: center; max-width: min(900px, 90vw); }\n@media (max-width: 760px) { .aura-left, .aura-right { left: var(--aura-pad); right: var(--aura-pad); bottom: 12vh; text-align: left; } }\n\n.aura-eyebrow { font: 500 11px/1 var(--aura-mono); letter-spacing: 0.28em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--aura-accent); display: inline-flex; gap: 12px; align-items: center; }\n.aura-eyebrow::before { content: \"\"; width: 28px; height: 1px; background: currentColor; }\n.aura-h { font: 600 clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4.4rem)/0.96 var(--aura-display); letter-spacing: -0.045em; margin: 18px 0 0; }\n.aura-p { font-size: clamp(14px, 1.2vw, 17px); line-height: 1.6; color: var(--aura-dim); margin: 18px 0 0; }\n\n/* ── GIANT TYPE. The signature of the genre: letters bigger than the viewport, scrubbed by scroll.\n   Letter-spacing tightens and the word scales as --local runs 0→1; it also dissolves via --vis. */\n.aura-giant {\n  position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; top: var(--aura-giant-top, 32%);\n  translate: 0 -50%;\n  text-align: center;\n  /* add data-fit=\"0.86\" and fitType() sizes it by measurement; this clamp is only the pre-measure fallback */\n  font: 700 clamp(4rem, 16vw, 22rem)/0.8 var(--aura-display);\n  letter-spacing: calc(0.02em - var(--local, 0) * 0.04em);\n  scale: calc(1 + var(--local, 0) * 0.1);\n  filter: blur(calc((1 - var(--in, 0) + var(--out, 0)) * 14px));\n  opacity: var(--vis, 0);\n  color: var(--aura-fg);\n  mix-blend-mode: normal;\n  user-select: none;\n  white-space: nowrap;\n}\n/* ── THE END CARD. One obvious action, set in the final image. Give the wrapper class \"aura-hit\" so it is clickable. */\n.aura-cta { position: relative; display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; margin-top: 30px; padding: 18px 30px; border-radius: 999px; color: var(--aura-fg); text-decoration: none; font: 600 13px/1 var(--aura-mono); letter-spacing: 0.22em; text-transform: uppercase; border: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 0.28); background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.06); backdrop-filter: blur(14px); transition: transform 0.5s var(--aura-ease), background 0.3s, border-color 0.3s, box-shadow 0.4s; }\n.aura-cta:hover { background: var(--aura-accent); color: #02040a; border-color: transparent; box-shadow: 0 0 60px -6px var(--aura-accent); transform: translateY(-2px); }\n.aura-cta::after { content: \"→\"; transition: transform 0.4s var(--aura-ease); }\n.aura-cta:hover::after { transform: translateX(6px); }\n\n/* variant: outlined giant type that sits \"inside\" the scene instead of on top */\n.aura-giant.is-outline { color: transparent; -webkit-text-stroke: 1px rgb(255 255 255 / 0.35); }\n\n/* ── TEXT SANDWICH — the \"letters behind the building\" trick.\n   Layer order (back → front):  canvas/bg  →  .aura-giant  →  a transparent PNG cut-out of the subject  → foreground fx\n   Put the subject cut-out in .aura-overlay AFTER the giant type with the same sizing as the bg plate.\n   In pure WebGL: render the type as a plane mesh at z between two layers of createLayerStack. */\n\n/* ── REFLECTED TYPE (wet floor / still water). Duplicate the word, flip it, mask it. */\n.aura-reflect { position: relative; }\n.aura-reflect::after {\n  content: attr(data-text);\n  position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; top: 100%;\n  transform: scaleY(-1);\n  opacity: 0.28;\n  -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to top, #000 0%, transparent 70%);\n  mask-image: linear-gradient(to top, #000 0%, transparent 70%);\n  filter: blur(1.5px);\n  pointer-events: none;\n}\n\n/* ── HUD: tiny, mono, always there. Micro-typography is 30% of \"expensive\". */\n.aura-hud { position: absolute; inset: auto var(--aura-pad) 28px var(--aura-pad); display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 18px; font: 500 10px/1 var(--aura-mono); letter-spacing: 0.26em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--aura-dim); pointer-events: none; }\n.aura-hud-line { flex: 1; height: 1px; background: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.14); position: relative; overflow: hidden; }\n.aura-hud-fill { position: absolute; inset: 0; background: var(--aura-accent); transform-origin: left; transform: scaleX(0); }\n\n/* ── PRELOADER: the first chapter. Big counter + title, wipes up when ready. */\n.aura-loader { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 5; background: var(--aura-bg); display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: flex-end; padding: var(--aura-pad); transition: transform 1.1s var(--aura-ease), opacity 0.6s 0.5s; }\n.aura-loader.is-done { transform: translateY(-100%); opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; }\n.aura-loader-n { font: 700 clamp(4rem, 16vw, 14rem)/0.8 var(--aura-display); letter-spacing: -0.05em; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }\n.aura-loader-t { font: 500 11px/1 var(--aura-mono); letter-spacing: 0.3em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--aura-dim); margin-top: 18px; }\n\n/* ── grain over everything (cheap film texture) */\n.aura-stage::after { content: \"\"; position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none; opacity: 0.04; background-image: url(\"data:image/svg+xml;utf8,<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' width='200' height='200'><filter id='n'><feTurbulence type='fractalNoise' baseFrequency='.9' numOctaves='2' stitchTiles='stitch'/></filter><rect width='100%' height='100%' filter='url(%23n)'/></svg>\"); }\n\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .aura-copy { filter: none; transform: none; }\n  .aura-loader { transition-duration: 0.01s; }\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/choreo.ts":"/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · choreo.ts\n   Pure helpers for turning one number (the scroll playhead, 0..1)\n   into everything on screen. No dependencies.\n\n   RULE: there is ONE playhead. The camera, the DOM copy, the\n   particles and the grade all read the same damped value.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nexport const clamp = (v: number, a = 0, b = 1) => Math.min(b, Math.max(a, v));\nexport const lerp = (a: number, b: number, t: number) => a + (b - a) * t;\n\n/** Frame-rate independent damping. `lambda` ≈ responsiveness (3 = floaty dolly, 8 = snappy). */\nexport const damp = (a: number, b: number, lambda: number, dt: number) => a + (b - a) * (1 - Math.exp(-lambda * dt));\n\nexport const smoothstep = (a: number, b: number, v: number) => {\n  const t = clamp((v - a) / (b - a));\n  return t * t * (3 - 2 * t);\n};\n\n/** Normalised progress of `p` through the window [a, b], clamped to 0..1. The workhorse. */\nexport const seg = (p: number, a: number, b: number) => clamp((p - a) / (b - a));\n\n/** 0 → 1 → 0 window: ramps up over `fin`, holds, ramps down over `fout`. Perfect for chapter visibility. */\nexport const pulse = (p: number, a: number, b: number, fin = 0.04, fout = 0.04) =>\n  Math.min(seg(p, a, a + fin), 1 - seg(p, b - fout, b));\n\nexport const ease = {\n  linear: (t: number) => t,\n  inCubic: (t: number) => t * t * t,\n  outCubic: (t: number) => 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3),\n  inOutCubic: (t: number) => (t < 0.5 ? 4 * t * t * t : 1 - Math.pow(-2 * t + 2, 3) / 2),\n  inOutQuint: (t: number) => (t < 0.5 ? 16 * t ** 5 : 1 - Math.pow(-2 * t + 2, 5) / 2),\n  outExpo: (t: number) => (t >= 1 ? 1 : 1 - Math.pow(2, -10 * t)),\n  inOutSine: (t: number) => -(Math.cos(Math.PI * t) - 1) / 2,\n  outBack: (t: number) => 1 + 2.70158 * Math.pow(t - 1, 3) + 1.70158 * Math.pow(t - 1, 2),\n};\n\n/* ── DOM choreography ──────────────────────────────────────────\n   Mark any element with  data-a (start) and data-b (end) in 0..1 scroll progress.\n   Every frame we write CSS variables on it:\n     --in     0→1 over the first `data-fade` of the window  (entrance)\n     --out    0→1 over the last  `data-fade`                (exit)\n     --vis    0→1→0 combined opacity window\n     --local  0→1 across the whole window                   (use for scrubbed text, counters, bars)\n   and toggle visibility:hidden outside the window (cheap, and keeps focus order sane).\n   Style them in aura.css with calc() — no JS per element, no layout thrash. */\nexport function bindDom(root: HTMLElement) {\n  const nodes = [...root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(\"[data-a]\")].map((el) => {\n    const a = parseFloat(el.dataset.a ?? \"0\"), b = parseFloat(el.dataset.b ?? \"1\");\n    return {\n      el, a, b,\n      f: parseFloat(el.dataset.fade ?? \"0.05\"),\n      // a chapter that starts at 0 is ALREADY on screen in frame one; one that ends at 1 STAYS for the final image.\n      // (Scroll-fading the first/last chapter produces an empty opening frame and an empty closing frame.)\n      first: a <= 0.001,\n      last: b >= 0.999,\n    };\n  });\n  return (p: number) => {\n    for (const n of nodes) {\n      const fin = n.first ? 1 : seg(p, n.a, n.a + n.f);\n      const fout = n.last ? 0 : seg(p, n.b - n.f, n.b);\n      const vis = Math.min(fin, 1 - fout);\n      const s = n.el.style;\n      s.setProperty(\"--in\", fin.toFixed(4));\n      s.setProperty(\"--out\", fout.toFixed(4));\n      s.setProperty(\"--vis\", vis.toFixed(4));\n      s.setProperty(\"--local\", seg(p, n.a, n.b).toFixed(4));\n      s.visibility = vis > 0.001 ? \"visible\" : \"hidden\";\n    }\n  };\n}\n\n/** FIT TYPE — make `[data-fit]` words exactly as wide as a fraction of the viewport (default 0.86), at any aspect ratio.\n *  Giant type must be sized by MEASUREMENT, not by guessing vw: font metrics differ and a cropped title reads as a bug.\n *  <div className=\"aura-giant\" data-fit=\"0.86\">OBSIDIAN</div>   (re-fits on resize and when web fonts finish loading) */\nexport function fitType(root: HTMLElement) {\n  const run = () => {\n    const vw = root.clientWidth || innerWidth;\n    root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(\"[data-fit]\").forEach((el) => {\n      const target = vw * parseFloat(el.dataset.fit || \"0.86\");\n      el.style.fontSize = \"100px\";\n      const r = document.createRange();\n      r.selectNodeContents(el);\n      const w = r.getBoundingClientRect().width;\n      if (w > 0) el.style.fontSize = `${Math.min((100 * target) / w, (root.clientHeight || innerHeight) * 0.5)}px`;\n    });\n  };\n  run();\n  const ro = new ResizeObserver(run);\n  ro.observe(root);\n  document.fonts?.ready.then(run);\n  return () => ro.disconnect();\n}\n\n/** Scroll progress (0..1) of a tall track element: 0 when its top hits the viewport top, 1 when its bottom hits the viewport bottom. */\nexport function trackProgress(track: HTMLElement) {\n  return () => {\n    const r = track.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const range = r.height - innerHeight;\n    return range > 0 ? clamp(-r.top / range) : 0;\n  };\n}\n\n/** Mechanical detent: pulls v toward the nearest stop when within `width`, so a scrubbed dial/knob \"clicks\" into place.\n *  Pass the scrubbed value (0..1) and the stops; use the result for rotation, not for the camera. */\nexport function snap(v: number, stops: number[], width = 0.035) {\n  let best = stops[0];\n  for (const s of stops) if (Math.abs(v - s) < Math.abs(v - best)) best = s;\n  const d = v - best, a = Math.abs(d);\n  if (a >= width) return v;\n  const t = a / width;\n  return best + Math.sign(d) * width * t * t * (3 - 2 * t);\n}\n\n/** Critically-underdamped spring step (for needles, dials, labels that should overshoot a little). Mutates and returns state. */\nexport function spring(s: { x: number; v: number }, target: number, dt: number, k = 60, c = 9) {\n  s.v += ((target - s.x) * k - s.v * c) * dt;\n  s.x += s.v * dt;\n  return s.x;\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/engine.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport { EffectComposer } from \"three/examples/jsm/postprocessing/EffectComposer.js\";\nimport { RenderPass } from \"three/examples/jsm/postprocessing/RenderPass.js\";\nimport { UnrealBloomPass } from \"three/examples/jsm/postprocessing/UnrealBloomPass.js\";\nimport { OutputPass } from \"three/examples/jsm/postprocessing/OutputPass.js\";\nimport { ShaderPass } from \"three/examples/jsm/postprocessing/ShaderPass.js\";\nimport { RoomEnvironment } from \"three/examples/jsm/environments/RoomEnvironment.js\";\nimport { clamp, damp, ease } from \"./choreo\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · engine.ts — the cinematic runtime.\n\n   · The camera rides a keyframed SHOT LIST (position + look-at + fov + roll), Catmull-Rom smoothed.\n     Each shot has an `at` ∈ [0,1] so you control PACING: put two shots close together to rush,\n     repeat a position across two `at` values to HOLD.\n   · Scroll progress is the playhead. It is critically damped → the camera glides, overshoots, settles.\n   · Velocity kicks the FOV, adds chromatic fringing and banks the horizon (a real dolly cam).\n   · Pointer adds parallax. Reduced-motion users get an instant, calm playhead.\n   · A post stack (bloom → tonemap → grade/grain/vignette/aberration) gives the filmic finish.\n   · An adaptive quality governor protects 60fps on weak GPUs by lowering the pixel ratio.\n   A scene is just a function that returns an AuraScene. See scene.example.ts.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nexport type V3 = [number, number, number];\n\nexport type Shot = {\n  /** playhead position 0..1 at which the camera reaches this shot */\n  at: number;\n  pos: V3;\n  look: V3;\n  /** vertical FOV in degrees (lens). 24–32 = telephoto drama · 45 = natural · 60+ = wide / speed */\n  fov?: number;\n  /** degrees of Dutch tilt */\n  roll?: number;\n  /** easing applied to the move INTO the next shot. default linear (continuous glide) */\n  ease?: \"linear\" | \"inOut\" | \"out\";\n};\n\nexport type Grade = {\n  vignette?: number;\n  grain?: number;\n  aberration?: number;\n  contrast?: number;\n  saturation?: number;\n  /** colour pushed into the shadows / highlights (split-toning). 0..1 per channel, keep it subtle */\n  shadows?: V3;\n  highlights?: V3;\n};\n\nexport interface Frame {\n  /** seconds since mount */\n  time: number;\n  dt: number;\n  /** the damped playhead 0..1 — drive EVERYTHING from this */\n  p: number;\n  /** playhead speed (≈ scroll velocity, always ≥ 0) */\n  velocity: number;\n  /** pointer in -1..1 (smoothed) */\n  pointer: { x: number; y: number };\n  camera: THREE.PerspectiveCamera;\n  /** 0..1 — current adaptive quality; gate expensive effects on it */\n  quality: number;\n  reduced: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface AuraScene {\n  scene: THREE.Scene;\n  shots: Shot[];\n  fov?: number;\n  exposure?: number;\n  bloom?: { strength: number; radius: number; threshold: number };\n  grade?: Grade;\n  /** pointer parallax in world units (default 0.8) */\n  parallax?: number;\n  update(f: Frame): void;\n  dispose?(): void;\n}\n\nexport interface BuildContext {\n  renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n  /** pre-filtered neutral studio environment (PMREM). Assign to scene.environment for instant PBR realism */\n  env: THREE.Texture;\n  mobile: boolean;\n  reduced: boolean;\n  quality: number;\n}\n\nexport type Builder = (ctx: BuildContext) => AuraScene | Promise<AuraScene>;\n\nexport interface AuraOptions {\n  /** ceiling for devicePixelRatio. 1.75 desktop · 1.25 mobile are sane defaults */\n  maxDpr?: number;\n  /** called every frame BEFORE damping to read the raw target progress (e.g. trackProgress(el)) */\n  progress?: () => number;\n}\n\nexport interface AuraInfo {\n  p: number;\n  locked: boolean;\n  fps: number;\n  quality: number;\n  size: [number, number];\n  shots: Shot[];\n  /** the camera's current world position / look target / fov — handy when an agent is tuning shots from screenshots */\n  camera: { pos: V3; look: V3; fov: number };\n  drawCalls: number;\n  triangles: number;\n}\n\nexport interface AuraHandle {\n  /** DETERMINISTIC SEEK — lock the playhead to p and settle instantly (no damping, no pointer drift, no velocity effects).\n   *  Identical input → identical frame. This is how agents screenshot a scroll position exactly. Also available as window.__aura.seek(p). */\n  seek(p: number): void;\n  /** hand control back to the scroll position */\n  release(): void;\n  info(): AuraInfo;\n  setProgress(p: number): void;\n  progress(): number;\n  /** dim the world (0..1), e.g. while a modal is open */\n  setDim(d: number): void;\n  /** subscribe to every rendered frame (after the scene update). Returns unsubscribe */\n  onFrame(cb: (f: Frame) => void): () => void;\n  quality(): number;\n  dispose(): void;\n}\n\nconst FX = {\n  uniforms: {\n    tDiffuse: { value: null as THREE.Texture | null },\n    uTime: { value: 0 },\n    uFade: { value: 0 },\n    uDim: { value: 0 },\n    uVel: { value: 0 },\n    uVignette: { value: 0.65 },\n    uGrain: { value: 0.045 },\n    uAberr: { value: 0.018 },\n    uContrast: { value: 1.06 },\n    uSat: { value: 1.05 },\n    uShadows: { value: new THREE.Vector3(0, 0.02, 0.06) },\n    uHighlights: { value: new THREE.Vector3(0.06, 0.03, 0) },\n  },\n  vertexShader: /* glsl */ `varying vec2 vUv; void main(){ vUv = uv; gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(position, 1.); }`,\n  fragmentShader: /* glsl */ `\n    varying vec2 vUv; uniform sampler2D tDiffuse;\n    uniform float uTime, uFade, uDim, uVel, uVignette, uGrain, uAberr, uContrast, uSat;\n    uniform vec3 uShadows, uHighlights;\n    float hash(vec2 p){ p = fract(p * vec2(123.34, 456.21)); p += dot(p, p + 45.32); return fract(p.x * p.y); }\n    void main(){\n      vec2 c = vUv - .5; float d = dot(c, c);\n      vec2 off = c * (d * uAberr + uVel * .0028);\n      vec3 col = vec3(texture2D(tDiffuse, vUv + off).r, texture2D(tDiffuse, vUv).g, texture2D(tDiffuse, vUv - off).b);\n      float l = dot(col, vec3(.2126, .7152, .0722));\n      col = mix(vec3(l), col, uSat);\n      col = (col - .5) * uContrast + .5;\n      col += uShadows * (1. - smoothstep(0., .5, l)) + uHighlights * smoothstep(.5, 1., l);\n      col *= 1. - smoothstep(.10, .55, d) * uVignette;\n      col += (hash(vUv * vec2(1920., 1080.) + fract(uTime) * 91.) - .5) * uGrain;\n      gl_FragColor = vec4(max(col, 0.) * uFade * (1. - uDim * .5), 1.);\n    }`,\n};\n\ntype Ease = NonNullable<Shot[\"ease\"]>;\nconst EASES: Record<Ease, (t: number) => number> = { linear: ease.linear, inOut: ease.inOutCubic, out: ease.outCubic };\n\n/** uniform Catmull-Rom between b and c, with a/d as neighbours */\nconst cr = (a: number, b: number, c: number, d: number, t: number) =>\n  0.5 * (2 * b + (-a + c) * t + (2 * a - 5 * b + 4 * c - d) * t * t + (-a + 3 * b - 3 * c + d) * t * t * t);\n\nfunction sampleRail(shots: Shot[], p: number, pos: THREE.Vector3, look: THREE.Vector3, baseFov: number) {\n  const n = shots.length;\n  let i = 0;\n  while (i < n - 2 && p >= shots[i + 1].at) i++;\n  const s1 = shots[i], s2 = shots[Math.min(i + 1, n - 1)];\n  const s0 = shots[Math.max(i - 1, 0)], s3 = shots[Math.min(i + 2, n - 1)];\n  const span = Math.max(1e-5, s2.at - s1.at);\n  const t = EASES[s1.ease ?? \"linear\"](clamp((p - s1.at) / span));\n  pos.set(cr(s0.pos[0], s1.pos[0], s2.pos[0], s3.pos[0], t), cr(s0.pos[1], s1.pos[1], s2.pos[1], s3.pos[1], t), cr(s0.pos[2], s1.pos[2], s2.pos[2], s3.pos[2], t));\n  look.set(cr(s0.look[0], s1.look[0], s2.look[0], s3.look[0], t), cr(s0.look[1], s1.look[1], s2.look[1], s3.look[1], t), cr(s0.look[2], s1.look[2], s2.look[2], s3.look[2], t));\n  const f1 = s1.fov ?? baseFov, f2 = s2.fov ?? baseFov;\n  const r1 = s1.roll ?? 0, r2 = s2.roll ?? 0;\n  return { fov: f1 + (f2 - f1) * t, roll: r1 + (r2 - r1) * t };\n}\n\nexport async function mountAura(host: HTMLElement, build: Builder, opts: AuraOptions = {}): Promise<AuraHandle> {\n  const reduced = matchMedia(\"(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)\").matches;\n  let w = host.clientWidth || innerWidth, h = host.clientHeight || innerHeight;\n  const mobile = w < 760 || matchMedia(\"(pointer: coarse)\").matches;\n  const maxDpr = Math.min(devicePixelRatio || 1, opts.maxDpr ?? (mobile ? 1.25 : 1.75));\n\n  const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, powerPreference: \"high-performance\", stencil: false });\n  renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping;\n  renderer.info.autoReset = false; // composer renders several passes per frame — we reset once per frame so info() reports the whole frame\n  renderer.domElement.style.cssText = \"display:block;width:100%;height:100%\";\n  host.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n  let quality = mobile ? 0.75 : 1;\n  const dprFor = (q: number) => maxDpr * (0.6 + 0.4 * q);\n  let dpr = dprFor(quality);\n  renderer.setPixelRatio(dpr);\n  renderer.setSize(w, h);\n\n  const pmrem = new THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer);\n  const env = pmrem.fromScene(new RoomEnvironment(), 0.04).texture;\n\n  const built = await build({ renderer, env, mobile, reduced, quality });\n  renderer.toneMappingExposure = built.exposure ?? 1;\n  const baseFov = built.fov ?? 40;\n  const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(baseFov, w / h, 0.1, 2000);\n\n  const composer = new EffectComposer(renderer);\n  composer.setPixelRatio(dpr);\n  composer.setSize(w, h);\n  composer.addPass(new RenderPass(built.scene, camera));\n  const b = built.bloom ?? { strength: 0.6, radius: 0.7, threshold: 0.85 };\n  const bloom = new UnrealBloomPass(new THREE.Vector2(w, h), b.strength, b.radius, b.threshold);\n  composer.addPass(bloom);\n  composer.addPass(new OutputPass());\n  const fx = new ShaderPass(FX);\n  composer.addPass(fx);\n  const g = built.grade ?? {};\n  const U = fx.uniforms as typeof FX.uniforms;\n  if (g.vignette !== undefined) U.uVignette.value = g.vignette;\n  if (g.grain !== undefined) U.uGrain.value = g.grain;\n  if (g.aberration !== undefined) U.uAberr.value = g.aberration;\n  if (g.contrast !== undefined) U.uContrast.value = g.contrast;\n  if (g.saturation !== undefined) U.uSat.value = g.saturation;\n  if (g.shadows) U.uShadows.value.set(...g.shadows);\n  if (g.highlights) U.uHighlights.value.set(...g.highlights);\n  if (reduced) { U.uGrain.value *= 0.5; U.uAberr.value = 0; }\n\n  const S = { locked: false, target: 0, prog: 0, dim: 0, dimT: 0, fade: 0, speed: 0, bank: 0, ptrX: 0, ptrY: 0, tx: 0, ty: 0 };\n  const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => { S.tx = (e.clientX / innerWidth) * 2 - 1; S.ty = (e.clientY / innerHeight) * 2 - 1; };\n  addEventListener(\"pointermove\", onMove, { passive: true });\n\n  const applyQuality = () => {\n    dpr = dprFor(quality);\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(dpr);\n    composer.setPixelRatio(dpr);\n    renderer.setSize(w, h);\n    composer.setSize(w, h);\n    bloom.enabled = quality > 0.45;\n  };\n  const resize = () => {\n    w = host.clientWidth || innerWidth; h = host.clientHeight || innerHeight;\n    renderer.setSize(w, h); composer.setSize(w, h);\n    camera.aspect = w / h;\n    camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n  };\n  resize();\n  const ro = new ResizeObserver(resize);\n  ro.observe(host);\n\n  let visible = true;\n  const io = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => { visible = e.isIntersecting; });\n  io.observe(host);\n\n  const listeners = new Set<(f: Frame) => void>();\n  const pos = new THREE.Vector3(), look = new THREE.Vector3(), prev = new THREE.Vector3();\n  const right = new THREE.Vector3();\n  let last = performance.now(), elapsed = 0; // (THREE.Clock is deprecated in recent three)\n  let raf = 0, running = true, first = true, frames = 0, acc = 0, fps = 60, ema = 1 / 60;\n  const lastShot = built.shots[built.shots.length - 1].at;\n  if (built.shots[0].at !== 0 || lastShot !== 1) console.warn(`[aura] shots should start at 0 and end at 1 (got ${built.shots[0].at} → ${lastShot}). The camera freezes outside the rail.`);\n\n  const frame = () => {\n    if (!running) return;\n    raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);\n    const now = performance.now();\n    const raw = (now - last) / 1000;\n    last = now;\n    if (document.hidden || !visible) return;\n    const dt = Math.min(raw, 0.05);\n    elapsed += dt;\n    const t = elapsed;\n\n    // ── adaptive quality governor: after warm-up, if the average frame is slow, step the pixel ratio down (never back up → no flapping)\n    if (++frames > 90) {\n      acc += raw;\n      if ((frames - 90) % 45 === 0) {\n        if (acc / 45 > 1 / 38 && quality > 0.2) { quality = Math.max(0.2, quality - 0.2); applyQuality(); }\n        acc = 0;\n      }\n    }\n\n    ema += (raw - ema) * 0.05; fps = Math.round(1 / Math.max(ema, 1e-3));\n    if (!S.locked && opts.progress) S.target = clamp(opts.progress());\n    const before = S.prog;\n    S.prog = first || S.locked ? S.target : damp(S.prog, S.target, reduced ? 20 : 3.2, dt);\n    if (S.locked) { S.fade = 1; S.tx = S.ty = S.ptrX = S.ptrY = 0; }\n    S.dim = damp(S.dim, S.dimT, 2.4, dt);\n    S.fade = Math.min(1, S.fade + dt * 0.8);\n    S.ptrX = damp(S.ptrX, S.tx, 2.4, dt);\n    S.ptrY = damp(S.ptrY, S.ty, 2.4, dt);\n    const vel = dt > 0 && !S.locked ? Math.abs(S.prog - before) / dt : 0;\n    S.speed = damp(S.speed, vel, 4, dt);\n\n    const p = clamp(S.prog, 0, 0.9999);\n    prev.copy(pos);\n    const aspectFov = w / h < 0.8 ? 18 : 0; // portrait phones need a wider lens\n    const { fov, roll } = sampleRail(built.shots, p, pos, look, baseFov);\n    const par = (built.parallax ?? 0.8) * (1 - S.dim * 0.6) * (reduced ? 0 : 1);\n    pos.x += S.ptrX * par; pos.y -= S.ptrY * par * 0.55;\n    look.x += S.ptrX * par * 1.4; look.y -= S.ptrY * par * 0.8;\n    camera.position.copy(pos);\n    camera.lookAt(look);\n\n    // bank into lateral motion + roll + FOV kick on speed\n    right.set(1, 0, 0).applyQuaternion(camera.quaternion);\n    const lateral = first || dt === 0 ? 0 : (pos.clone().sub(prev).dot(right) / dt) * 0.0009;\n    S.bank = damp(S.bank, clamp(lateral, -0.06, 0.06), 3, dt);\n    camera.rotateZ(-S.bank * (reduced ? 0 : 1) + (roll * Math.PI) / 180);\n    const kick = reduced ? 0 : Math.min(S.speed * 10, 5);\n    if (Math.abs(camera.fov - (fov + aspectFov + kick)) > 0.001) {\n      camera.fov = fov + aspectFov + kick;\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n    }\n\n    const fr: Frame = { time: t, dt, p: S.prog, velocity: S.speed, pointer: { x: S.ptrX, y: S.ptrY }, camera, quality, reduced };\n    built.update(fr);\n\n    U.uTime.value = t;\n    U.uFade.value = S.fade;\n    U.uDim.value = S.dim;\n    U.uVel.value = reduced ? 0 : Math.min(S.speed * 4, 0.5);\n    renderer.info.reset();\n    composer.render(dt);\n    listeners.forEach((cb) => cb(fr));\n    first = false;\n  };\n  frame();\n\n  const seek = (p: number) => { S.locked = true; S.target = S.prog = clamp(p); S.speed = 0; };\n  const release = () => { S.locked = false; };\n  const info = (): AuraInfo => ({\n    p: S.prog, locked: S.locked, fps, quality, size: [w, h], shots: built.shots,\n    camera: { pos: camera.position.toArray() as V3, look: look.toArray() as V3, fov: camera.fov },\n    drawCalls: renderer.info.render.calls, triangles: renderer.info.render.triangles,\n  });\n  // agent / QA hook:  window.__aura.seek(0.37)  then screenshot.   ?p=0.37 in the URL does the same on load.\n  const urlP = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get(\"p\");\n  if (urlP !== null && !Number.isNaN(parseFloat(urlP))) seek(parseFloat(urlP));\n  const hook = { seek, release, info };\n  (window as unknown as { __aura?: unknown }).__aura = hook;\n\n  return {\n    seek, release, info,\n    setProgress: (p) => { S.target = clamp(p); },\n    progress: () => S.prog,\n    setDim: (d) => { S.dimT = clamp(d); },\n    onFrame: (cb) => { listeners.add(cb); return () => void listeners.delete(cb); },\n    quality: () => quality,\n    dispose() {\n      running = false;\n      cancelAnimationFrame(raf);\n      removeEventListener(\"pointermove\", onMove);\n      if ((window as unknown as { __aura?: unknown }).__aura === hook) delete (window as unknown as { __aura?: unknown }).__aura; // StrictMode double-mount safe\n      ro.disconnect(); io.disconnect();\n      built.dispose?.();\n      built.scene.traverse((o) => {\n        const m = o as THREE.Mesh;\n        m.geometry?.dispose?.();\n        const mat = m.material as THREE.Material | THREE.Material[] | undefined;\n        (Array.isArray(mat) ? mat : mat ? [mat] : []).forEach((x) => {\n          for (const v of Object.values(x)) if (v instanceof THREE.Texture) v.dispose();\n          x.dispose();\n        });\n      });\n      env.dispose(); pmrem.dispose(); composer.dispose(); renderer.dispose();\n      renderer.forceContextLoss();\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    },\n  };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/floor.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport { Reflector } from \"three/examples/jsm/objects/Reflector.js\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · floor.ts — the reflective floor (wet obsidian, still water, polished showroom).\n   Reflection is the single cheapest \"this is expensive\" signal. It also doubles the draw cost,\n   so: gate on quality, keep resolution at 0.5 and fade it into the distance with the overlay.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nexport function createMirrorFloor(o: { size?: number; y?: number; color?: THREE.ColorRepresentation; resolution?: number; fadeColor?: THREE.ColorRepresentation } = {}) {\n  const size = o.size ?? 240;\n  const res = o.resolution ?? 0.5;\n  const g = new THREE.Group();\n  const mirror = new Reflector(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(size, size), {\n    color: new THREE.Color(o.color ?? 0x2a2f3a), // < 1 darkens the reflection so it reads as a surface, not a second world\n    textureWidth: Math.round(innerWidth * res),\n    textureHeight: Math.round(innerHeight * res),\n    clipBias: 0.003,\n  });\n  mirror.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;\n  g.add(mirror);\n\n  // radial fade: the floor dissolves into the fog instead of ending at a hard edge\n  const c = document.createElement(\"canvas\");\n  c.width = c.height = 256;\n  const ctx = c.getContext(\"2d\")!;\n  const grd = ctx.createRadialGradient(128, 128, 8, 128, 128, 128);\n  const fc = new THREE.Color(o.fadeColor ?? 0x03050b);\n  const rgb = `${Math.round(fc.r * 255)},${Math.round(fc.g * 255)},${Math.round(fc.b * 255)}`;\n  grd.addColorStop(0, `rgba(${rgb},0)`);\n  grd.addColorStop(0.35, `rgba(${rgb},0.25)`);\n  grd.addColorStop(1, `rgba(${rgb},1)`);\n  ctx.fillStyle = grd; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, 256, 256);\n  const tex = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c);\n  tex.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n  const fade = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(size, size), new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map: tex, transparent: true, depthWrite: false }));\n  fade.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;\n  fade.position.y = 0.01;\n  g.add(fade);\n\n  g.position.y = o.y ?? 0;\n  return { group: g, mirror, setEnabled: (on: boolean) => { mirror.visible = on; } };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/index.ts":"export * from \"./engine\";\nexport * from \"./choreo\";\nexport * from \"./materials\";\nexport { createParticles, type ParticleOptions } from \"./particles\";\nexport { createMirrorFloor } from \"./floor\";\nexport { createDepthPlane, createLayerStack, loadTexture, type LayerSpec, type DepthPlaneOptions } from \"./layers\";\nexport { loadGLB, fitModel, prepModel } from \"./assets\";\nexport { AuraExperience, type AuraExperienceProps } from \"./AuraExperience\";\nexport { createSky, createLightShafts, createGlow, createHaze, type SkyOptions, type ShaftOptions } from \"./atmosphere\";\nexport { createDunes, type DunesOptions } from \"./terrain\";\nexport { createStudioEnv, productRig, createContactShadow, type StripSpec } from \"./studio\";\nexport { createRockGround, createBoulders, fbm2, type RockGroundOptions } from \"./rock\";\nexport { folio, PROJECTS as FOLIO_PROJECTS, folioAt } from \"./scene.folio\";\nexport { earbuds, dial, bottle, type ExplodePart } from \"./objects\";\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/layers.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · layers.ts — \"2.5D\": real parallax from IMAGES.\n   This is how a solo agent gets a photoreal, AAA-looking world with no modelling:\n     1. generate / source a hero image (see references/05-asset-pipeline.md)\n     2. EITHER cut it into 4–7 transparent depth layers (createLayerStack)\n        OR estimate a depth map from it and displace a dense plane (createDepthPlane)\n     3. fly the camera rail through them — parallax + fog + particles in between = a 3D world.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nconst loader = new THREE.TextureLoader();\n\nexport function loadTexture(url: string, srgb = true): Promise<THREE.Texture> {\n  return new Promise((res, rej) =>\n    loader.load(\n      url,\n      (t) => {\n        t.colorSpace = srgb ? THREE.SRGBColorSpace : THREE.NoColorSpace;\n        t.anisotropy = 8;\n        t.generateMipmaps = true;\n        t.minFilter = THREE.LinearMipmapLinearFilter;\n        res(t);\n      },\n      undefined,\n      rej,\n    ),\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface DepthPlaneOptions {\n  colorUrl: string;\n  /** greyscale depth map, white = near. (Depth Anything / MiDaS / Marigold output works as-is) */\n  depthUrl: string;\n  width: number;\n  height: number;\n  /** how far (world units) the nearest pixel pops toward the camera. 0.6–2.5 */\n  depthScale?: number;\n  segments?: number;\n  /** multiplicative colour grade, e.g. [0.9,1,1.15] for a cold push */\n  tint?: [number, number, number];\n}\n\n/** A dense plane whose vertices are pushed by a depth map. Orbit the camera ±15° and a flat picture becomes a diorama. */\nexport async function createDepthPlane(o: DepthPlaneOptions) {\n  const [color, depth] = await Promise.all([loadTexture(o.colorUrl), loadTexture(o.depthUrl, false)]);\n  const seg = o.segments ?? 200;\n  const mat = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n    uniforms: {\n      uColor: { value: color },\n      uDepth: { value: depth },\n      uScale: { value: o.depthScale ?? 1.4 },\n      uTint: { value: new THREE.Vector3(...(o.tint ?? [1, 1, 1])) },\n      uBreath: { value: 0 },\n    },\n    vertexShader: /* glsl */ `\n      uniform sampler2D uDepth; uniform float uScale, uBreath; varying vec2 vUv;\n      void main(){\n        vUv = uv;\n        float d = texture2D(uDepth, uv).r;\n        vec3 p = position; p.z += (d - .5) * uScale * (1. + uBreath * .15);\n        gl_Position = projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(p, 1.);\n      }`,\n    fragmentShader: /* glsl */ `\n      uniform sampler2D uColor; uniform vec3 uTint; varying vec2 vUv;\n      void main(){ gl_FragColor = vec4(texture2D(uColor, vUv).rgb * uTint, 1.); }`,\n  });\n  const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(o.width, o.height, seg, Math.round(seg * (o.height / o.width))), mat);\n  return { mesh, material: mat };\n}\n\nexport interface LayerSpec {\n  url: string;\n  /** world-space z (camera looks down -z, so more negative = further away) */\n  z: number;\n  width: number;\n  height: number;\n  y?: number;\n  x?: number;\n  opacity?: number;\n  /** let scene fog tint this layer (distant layers should be hazier) */\n  fog?: boolean;\n}\n\n/** Stack of transparent PNG layers at different depths — mountains, mid-ground, subject, foreground fronds… */\nexport async function createLayerStack(specs: LayerSpec[]) {\n  const group = new THREE.Group();\n  const meshes = await Promise.all(\n    specs.map(async (s) => {\n      const map = await loadTexture(s.url);\n      const m = new THREE.Mesh(\n        new THREE.PlaneGeometry(s.width, s.height),\n        new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map, transparent: true, opacity: s.opacity ?? 1, depthWrite: false, fog: s.fog ?? true }),\n      );\n      m.position.set(s.x ?? 0, s.y ?? 0, s.z);\n      m.renderOrder = Math.round(-s.z); // far → near\n      group.add(m);\n      return m;\n    }),\n  );\n  return { group, meshes };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/materials.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · materials.ts — physically-based recipes that read as EXPENSIVE.\n   Rule: nothing in a hero shot is a default MeshStandardMaterial.\n   Every surface needs roughness VARIATION (a map, noise or a gradient) — uniform roughness is what makes CG look like CG.\n   All of these rely on scene.environment (ctx.env) for reflections: no env = no realism.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\ntype Col = THREE.ColorRepresentation;\n\n/** Mirror-polished metal. Dark base + env reflections = chrome. */\nexport const chrome = (color: Col = 0xdfe6ee, roughness = 0.08) =>\n  new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color, metalness: 1, roughness, envMapIntensity: 1.4, clearcoat: 0.3, clearcoatRoughness: 0.1 });\n\n/** Piano-black lacquer with a razor clearcoat — the \"luxury product\" black. */\nexport const obsidian = (color: Col = 0x05060a) =>\n  new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color, metalness: 0.2, roughness: 0.28, clearcoat: 1, clearcoatRoughness: 0.04, envMapIntensity: 1.2, sheen: 0.4, sheenColor: new THREE.Color(0x6a7cff), sheenRoughness: 0.4 });\n\n/** Thin-film iridescence (soap bubble / anodised titanium / oil slick). Great for hero objects. */\nexport const iridescent = (color: Col = 0x10131a) =>\n  new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color, metalness: 0.6, roughness: 0.18, iridescence: 1, iridescenceIOR: 1.6, clearcoat: 1, clearcoatRoughness: 0.06, envMapIntensity: 1.5 });\n\n/** Real refraction glass. transmission re-renders the scene behind it → EXPENSIVE: use on 1–3 hero meshes, gate on quality. */\nexport const glass = (tint: Col = 0xffffff, thickness = 1.2) =>\n  new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color: tint, metalness: 0, roughness: 0.04, transmission: 1, thickness, ior: 1.45, attenuationColor: new THREE.Color(tint), attenuationDistance: 3, envMapIntensity: 1.2, specularIntensity: 1, clearcoat: 1 });\n\n/** A glowing surface. Intensity > 1 pushes it past the bloom threshold so it blooms. */\nexport const emissive = (color: Col, intensity = 2.5) => {\n  const m = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x000000, emissive: new THREE.Color(color), emissiveIntensity: intensity, roughness: 1 });\n  m.toneMapped = true;\n  return m;\n};\n\n/** Vertical (or horizontal) colour ramp as a texture — for skies, rim gradients, gradient maps. stops: [offset 0..1, css colour][] */\nexport function gradientTexture(stops: [number, string][], size = 256, vertical = true) {\n  const c = document.createElement(\"canvas\");\n  c.width = vertical ? 2 : size; c.height = vertical ? size : 2;\n  const ctx = c.getContext(\"2d\")!;\n  const g = vertical ? ctx.createLinearGradient(0, 0, 0, size) : ctx.createLinearGradient(0, 0, size, 0);\n  stops.forEach(([o, col]) => g.addColorStop(o, col));\n  ctx.fillStyle = g; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, c.width, c.height);\n  const t = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c);\n  t.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n  return t;\n}\n\n/** Tileable-ish value noise as a greyscale texture (roughness / bump / displacement for rock, skin, brushed metal). */\nexport function noiseTexture(size = 512, octaves = 5, seed = 1) {\n  const data = new Uint8Array(size * size * 4);\n  const rnd = (x: number, y: number) => { const s = Math.sin(x * 127.1 + y * 311.7 + seed * 17.3) * 43758.5453; return s - Math.floor(s); };\n  const vnoise = (x: number, y: number, f: number) => {\n    const xf = x * f, yf = y * f, xi = Math.floor(xf), yi = Math.floor(yf);\n    const tx = xf - xi, ty = yf - yi, sx = tx * tx * (3 - 2 * tx), sy = ty * ty * (3 - 2 * ty);\n    const m = (a: number, b: number) => rnd(((a % f) + f) % f, ((b % f) + f) % f);\n    const a = m(xi, yi), b = m(xi + 1, yi), c = m(xi, yi + 1), d = m(xi + 1, yi + 1);\n    return a + (b - a) * sx + (c - a) * sy + (a - b - c + d) * sx * sy;\n  };\n  for (let y = 0; y < size; y++) for (let x = 0; x < size; x++) {\n    let v = 0, amp = 0.5, f = 4;\n    for (let o = 0; o < octaves; o++) { v += vnoise(x / size, y / size, f) * amp; amp *= 0.5; f *= 2; }\n    const i = (y * size + x) * 4;\n    data[i] = data[i + 1] = data[i + 2] = Math.min(255, v * 255 * 1.15); data[i + 3] = 255;\n  }\n  const t = new THREE.DataTexture(data, size, size, THREE.RGBAFormat);\n  t.wrapS = t.wrapT = THREE.RepeatWrapping;\n  t.needsUpdate = true;\n  return t;\n}\n\n/** The classic 3-point-plus-practical light rig, tuned for dark cinematic scenes. Returns the group so you can animate it. */\nexport function lightRig(o: { key?: Col; rim?: Col; fill?: Col; practical?: Col } = {}) {\n  const g = new THREE.Group();\n  const key = new THREE.DirectionalLight(o.key ?? 0xfff1e0, 2.4); key.position.set(4, 6, 5);\n  const rim = new THREE.DirectionalLight(o.rim ?? 0x6fa8ff, 4); rim.position.set(-6, 3, -5); // the rim light is what makes shapes pop out of the dark\n  const fill = new THREE.HemisphereLight(o.fill ?? 0x1a2440, 0x05060a, 0.8);\n  const practical = new THREE.PointLight(o.practical ?? 0xff9a5c, 12, 14, 2); practical.position.set(1.5, 0.6, 2.5); // a warm \"in-world\" light source\n  g.add(key, rim, fill, practical);\n  return { group: g, key, rim, fill, practical };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/objects.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport { RoundedBoxGeometry } from \"three/examples/jsm/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js\";\nimport { chrome, emissive, noiseTexture, obsidian } from \"./materials\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · objects.ts — procedural HERO OBJECTS. Hard-surface products built from primitives, with bevels (highlights live\n   on bevels), varied roughness and an emissive detail. Restyle the colours / proportions and you have a different product.\n     earbuds()  — charging case + two buds + internals, with an explode/ascent part list\n     dial()     — machined instrument dial: knurled bezel, engraved face, glass dome, lit ticks, spring needle\n     bottle()   — glass serum / fragrance bottle with a liquid column and a metal cap\n   Everything faces +Z and sits on y = 0 so you can drop it on a plinth.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\ntype Col = THREE.ColorRepresentation;\n\nexport interface ExplodePart { name: string; obj: THREE.Object3D; rest: THREE.Vector3; /** how far it rises in the explode chapter */ lift: number; /** extra sideways travel */ spreadX?: number }\n\nexport function earbuds(o: { body?: Col; accent?: Col } = {}) {\n  const accent = new THREE.Color(o.accent ?? 0x7dffd9);\n  const lacquer = obsidian(o.body ?? 0x0d1015);\n  Object.assign(lacquer, { roughness: 0.2, metalness: 0.35, envMapIntensity: 1.1, sheen: 0 }); // sheen off: obsidian()'s blue sheen would tint graphite blue\n  const rough = noiseTexture(256, 4, 2); rough.repeat.set(3, 3);\n  const satin = new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color: 0x1a1e25, metalness: 0.85, roughness: 0.38, roughnessMap: rough, envMapIntensity: 1.3, clearcoat: 0.3 });\n  const rubber = new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color: 0x14171c, roughness: 0.72, sheen: 0.7, sheenColor: new THREE.Color(0x8ea0b8), sheenRoughness: 0.5 });\n  const ledMat = emissive(accent, 2.5);\n\n  const group = new THREE.Group();\n  const parts: ExplodePart[] = [];\n  const add = (name: string, obj: THREE.Object3D, lift: number, spreadX = 0) => { group.add(obj); parts.push({ name, obj, rest: obj.position.clone(), lift, spreadX }); return obj; };\n\n  // base shell\n  const base = new THREE.Mesh(new RoundedBoxGeometry(2.9, 0.82, 1.7, 8, 0.3), lacquer); base.position.y = 0.41;\n  const wells = new THREE.Group();\n  [-0.72, 0.72].forEach((x) => { const w = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.5, 0.46, 0.04, 48), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x020305, roughness: 0.9 })); w.position.set(x, 0.835, 0); wells.add(w); });\n  base.add(wells); wells.position.y = -0.41;\n  const led = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CapsuleGeometry(0.035, 0.34, 6, 12), ledMat); led.rotation.z = Math.PI / 2; led.position.set(0, 0.42, 0.855);\n  base.add(led); led.position.y = 0.01;\n  add(\"base\", base, 0);\n\n  // internals (revealed in the explode chapter)\n  const battery = new THREE.Mesh(new RoundedBoxGeometry(2.2, 0.2, 1.05, 4, 0.07), satin); battery.position.y = 0.45;\n  const stripe = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(2.0, 0.005, 0.12), emissive(accent, 1.6)); stripe.position.y = 0.105; battery.add(stripe);\n  add(\"battery\", battery, 1.15);\n  const pcb = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(2.4, 0.05, 1.3), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x07110f, roughness: 0.5, metalness: 0.4 })); pcb.position.y = 0.62;\n  for (let i = 0; i < 9; i++) { const c = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.14 + (i % 3) * 0.08, 0.04, 0.1 + (i % 2) * 0.08), chrome(0xd7b46a, 0.3)); c.position.set(-0.9 + i * 0.22, 0.045, ((i * 37) % 7) / 7 * 0.7 - 0.35); pcb.add(c); }\n  const chip = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.3, 0.05, 0.3), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x050607, roughness: 0.3 })); chip.position.set(0.3, 0.05, 0); pcb.add(chip);\n  add(\"pcb\", pcb, 2.1);\n\n  // the buds\n  const bud = (side: number) => {\n    const g = new THREE.Group();\n    const head = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.34, 48, 32), lacquer); head.scale.set(1, 0.9, 1.1);\n    const stem = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CapsuleGeometry(0.1, 0.62, 10, 20), lacquer); stem.position.set(0, -0.36, 0.1); stem.rotation.x = -0.18;\n    const tip = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.19, 32, 24), rubber); tip.position.set(side * 0.2, 0.14, -0.3); tip.scale.set(1, 0.9, 1.1);\n    const ring = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.TorusGeometry(0.2, 0.012, 12, 48), chrome(0xc9d3dd, 0.18)); ring.position.set(0, 0.05, 0.28); ring.rotation.x = Math.PI / 2 - 0.4;\n    const mic = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CircleGeometry(0.025, 16), ledMat); mic.position.set(0, -0.62, 0.22); mic.rotation.y = Math.PI;\n    g.add(head, stem, tip, ring, mic);\n    g.position.set(side * 0.72, 1.02, 0); g.rotation.z = -side * 0.08;\n    return g;\n  };\n  add(\"budL\", bud(-1), 3.0, -0.5);\n  add(\"budR\", bud(1), 3.0, 0.5);\n\n  // lid on a hinge at the back edge\n  const hinge = new THREE.Group(); hinge.position.set(0, 0.8, -0.85);\n  const lid = new THREE.Mesh(new RoundedBoxGeometry(2.9, 0.56, 1.7, 8, 0.27), lacquer); lid.position.set(0, 0.26, 0.85);\n  const logo = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.TorusGeometry(0.13, 0.012, 8, 40), chrome(0xe6edf3, 0.15)); logo.position.set(0, 0.545, 0.85); logo.rotation.x = Math.PI / 2; lid.add(logo);\n  hinge.add(lid);\n  add(\"lid\", hinge, 4.4);\n\n  const setOpen = (t: number) => { hinge.rotation.x = -t * 1.95; };\n  const setLed = (i: number) => { ledMat.emissiveIntensity = i; };\n  return { group, parts, led, ledMat, setOpen, setLed, lid: hinge, bounds: { w: 2.9, h: 1.36, d: 1.7 } };\n}\n\nexport function dial(o: { bezel?: Col; needle?: Col; ticks?: number; faceColor?: string } = {}) {\n  const N = o.ticks ?? 120;\n  const amber = new THREE.Color(o.needle ?? 0xffa94d);\n  const group = new THREE.Group();\n  const face = new THREE.Group(); // everything that spins with the ring lives here\n  const flip = new THREE.Group(); flip.rotation.x = Math.PI / 2; // lathe axis (Y) → +Z\n\n  const profile = [[0.0, 0], [1.02, 0], [1.1, 0.025], [1.14, 0.1], [1.12, 0.17], [1.04, 0.2], [0.95, 0.19], [0.93, 0.1], [0.0, 0.1]].map(([x, y]) => new THREE.Vector2(x, y));\n  const bezel = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.LatheGeometry(profile, 128), chrome(o.bezel ?? 0x8a949f, 0.24));\n  flip.add(bezel);\n  const knurl = new THREE.InstancedMesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.028, 0.12, 0.05), chrome(0x9aa4af, 0.35), 160);\n  const d = new THREE.Object3D();\n  for (let i = 0; i < 160; i++) { const a = (i / 160) * Math.PI * 2; d.position.set(Math.cos(a) * 1.135, 0.1, Math.sin(a) * 1.135); d.rotation.set(0, -a, 0); d.updateMatrix(); knurl.setMatrixAt(i, d.matrix); }\n  flip.add(knurl);\n  group.add(flip);\n\n  // engraved face texture\n  const c = document.createElement(\"canvas\"); c.width = c.height = 1024;\n  const g = c.getContext(\"2d\")!;\n  g.fillStyle = o.faceColor ?? \"#071a22\"; g.fillRect(0, 0, 1024, 1024);\n  const gr = g.createRadialGradient(512, 512, 40, 512, 512, 520); gr.addColorStop(0, \"rgba(255,255,255,0.07)\"); gr.addColorStop(1, \"rgba(0,0,0,0.5)\"); g.fillStyle = gr; g.fillRect(0, 0, 1024, 1024);\n  g.translate(512, 512); g.strokeStyle = \"rgba(232,238,242,0.8)\"; g.fillStyle = \"rgba(232,238,242,0.85)\"; g.textAlign = \"center\"; g.textBaseline = \"middle\"; g.font = \"500 34px 'JetBrains Mono', monospace\";\n  for (let i = 0; i < 72; i++) { const a = (i / 72) * Math.PI * 2, major = i % 6 === 0; g.lineWidth = major ? 4 : 2; g.beginPath(); g.moveTo(Math.sin(a) * (major ? 360 : 380), -Math.cos(a) * (major ? 360 : 380)); g.lineTo(Math.sin(a) * 405, -Math.cos(a) * 405); g.stroke(); }\n  for (let i = 0; i < 12; i++) { const a = (i / 12) * Math.PI * 2, lab = i === 0 ? \"N\" : i === 3 ? \"E\" : i === 6 ? \"S\" : i === 9 ? \"W\" : String(i * 30); g.fillText(lab, Math.sin(a) * 320, -Math.cos(a) * 320); }\n  const tex = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c); tex.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace; tex.anisotropy = 8;\n  const plate = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CircleGeometry(0.93, 96), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ map: tex, roughness: 0.55, metalness: 0.2 }));\n  plate.position.z = 0.1;\n  group.add(plate);\n\n  // lit tick ring (instanced; per-instance colour = lit/unlit)\n  const ticks = new THREE.InstancedMesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.012, 0.075, 0.012), new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0xffffff }), N);\n  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) { const a = (i / N) * Math.PI * 2; d.position.set(Math.sin(a) * 0.86, Math.cos(a) * 0.86, 0.112); d.rotation.set(0, 0, -a); d.updateMatrix(); ticks.setMatrixAt(i, d.matrix); ticks.setColorAt(i, new THREE.Color(0x12303a)); }\n  face.add(ticks); group.add(face);\n\n  // needle (rotates with heading)\n  const needle = new THREE.Group();\n  const bladeMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x000000, emissive: amber, emissiveIntensity: 1.8, roughness: 0.4 });\n  const blade = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.ConeGeometry(0.055, 0.7, 4), bladeMat); blade.position.y = 0.35; blade.scale.z = 0.25;\n  const tail = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.ConeGeometry(0.04, 0.42, 4), chrome(0xcfd6dd, 0.2)); tail.position.y = -0.21; tail.rotation.z = Math.PI; tail.scale.z = 0.25;\n  needle.add(blade, tail); needle.position.z = 0.15; group.add(needle);\n  const hub = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.075, 0.09, 0.07, 32), chrome(0xe2e8ee, 0.14)); hub.rotation.x = Math.PI / 2; hub.position.z = 0.17; group.add(hub);\n\n  // glass dome (cheap: thin clearcoat, no transmission)\n  const dome = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.95, 64, 24, 0, Math.PI * 2, 0, Math.PI / 2), new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color: 0xffffff, transparent: true, opacity: 0.1, roughness: 0.02, metalness: 0, clearcoat: 1, clearcoatRoughness: 0, envMapIntensity: 2.2, depthWrite: false }));\n  dome.rotation.x = Math.PI / 2; dome.scale.set(1, 0.2, 1); dome.position.z = 0.19; group.add(dome);\n\n  const lit = new THREE.Color(), off = new THREE.Color(0x12303a), hot = amber.clone().multiplyScalar(3);\n  /** 0..1 → how much of the tick ring is lit */\n  const setLit = (t: number) => { for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) { lit.copy(i / N < t ? hot : off); ticks.setColorAt(i, lit); } if (ticks.instanceColor) ticks.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true; };\n  const setHeading = (deg: number) => { needle.rotation.z = -THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(deg); };\n  return { group, needle, setLit, setHeading };\n}\n\nexport function bottle(o: { glass?: Col; liquid?: Col; cap?: Col } = {}) {\n  const group = new THREE.Group();\n  const pts = [[0, 0], [0.5, 0], [0.62, 0.05], [0.66, 0.2], [0.66, 1.6], [0.6, 1.85], [0.34, 2.05], [0.3, 2.2], [0.3, 2.4]].map(([x, y]) => new THREE.Vector2(x, y));\n  const glassM = new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color: o.glass ?? 0xe8f0ee, roughness: 0.35, metalness: 0, transmission: 0.85, thickness: 1.4, ior: 1.5, envMapIntensity: 1.2, attenuationColor: new THREE.Color(o.glass ?? 0xdfeae4), attenuationDistance: 2.5 });\n  const body = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.LatheGeometry(pts, 96), glassM);\n  const inner = [[0, 0.12], [0.5, 0.12], [0.55, 0.2], [0.55, 1.15], [0.5, 1.2], [0, 1.2]].map(([x, y]) => new THREE.Vector2(x, y));\n  const liquid = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.LatheGeometry(inner, 64), new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color: o.liquid ?? 0xc58f73, roughness: 0.15, transmission: 0.4, thickness: 1, ior: 1.38, emissive: new THREE.Color(o.liquid ?? 0xc58f73), emissiveIntensity: 0.08 }));\n  const cap = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.34, 0.34, 0.55, 64), chrome(o.cap ?? 0xcfd2d6, 0.2)); cap.position.y = 2.6;\n  const collar = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.37, 0.37, 0.08, 64), chrome(0xe8e8ea, 0.1)); collar.position.y = 2.33;\n  group.add(body, liquid, cap, collar);\n  return { group, liquid };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/particles.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport type { V3 } from \"./engine\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · particles.ts — GPU particle field (dust, embers, snow, stardust, fireflies, spores).\n   All motion is in the vertex shader: 1 draw call, 10k+ points, free on the CPU.\n   Particles are the cheapest way to make air visible, and visible air is what sells DEPTH.\n   Use 3 sizes/depth bands (foreground bokeh · mid dust · far stars) — never one uniform cloud.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nexport interface ParticleOptions {\n  count?: number;\n  /** half-extents of the box the particles live in */\n  extent?: V3;\n  /** base point size in px at distance 10 */\n  size?: number;\n  color?: THREE.ColorRepresentation;\n  color2?: THREE.ColorRepresentation;\n  /** drift speed multiplier */\n  speed?: number;\n  /** 0..1 how much the field streaks/expands with scroll velocity */\n  reactivity?: number;\n  opacity?: number;\n}\n\nexport function createParticles(o: ParticleOptions = {}) {\n  const count = o.count ?? 2400;\n  const ext = o.extent ?? [14, 8, 30];\n  const pos = new Float32Array(count * 3);\n  const seed = new Float32Array(count * 4);\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n    pos[i * 3] = (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * ext[0];\n    pos[i * 3 + 1] = (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * ext[1];\n    pos[i * 3 + 2] = (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * ext[2];\n    seed.set([Math.random(), Math.random(), Math.random(), Math.random()], i * 4);\n  }\n  const geo = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n  geo.setAttribute(\"position\", new THREE.BufferAttribute(pos, 3));\n  geo.setAttribute(\"aSeed\", new THREE.BufferAttribute(seed, 4));\n\n  const mat = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n    transparent: true,\n    depthWrite: false,\n    blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending,\n    uniforms: {\n      uTime: { value: 0 },\n      uVel: { value: 0 },\n      uSize: { value: o.size ?? 2.2 },\n      uSpeed: { value: o.speed ?? 1 },\n      uReact: { value: o.reactivity ?? 0.6 },\n      uOpacity: { value: o.opacity ?? 0.8 },\n      uExt: { value: new THREE.Vector3(...ext) },\n      uC1: { value: new THREE.Color(o.color ?? 0xbfe8ff) },\n      uC2: { value: new THREE.Color(o.color2 ?? 0xffc98a) },\n      uPx: { value: Math.min(devicePixelRatio || 1, 2) },\n    },\n    vertexShader: /* glsl */ `\n      attribute vec4 aSeed; uniform float uTime, uVel, uSize, uSpeed, uReact, uPx; uniform vec3 uExt;\n      varying float vA; varying float vMix;\n      void main(){\n        vec3 p = position;\n        float t = uTime * uSpeed;\n        // lazy turbulence, each particle on its own phase\n        p.x += sin(t * .21 + aSeed.x * 40.) * .6 + sin(t * .07 + aSeed.w * 20.) * 1.2;\n        p.y += cos(t * .17 + aSeed.y * 40.) * .5 + t * .06 * (aSeed.z - .3);\n        p.z += sin(t * .13 + aSeed.z * 40.) * .6;\n        // wrap vertically so rising dust never runs out\n        p.y = mod(p.y + uExt.y, uExt.y * 2.) - uExt.y;\n        vec4 mv = modelViewMatrix * vec4(p, 1.);\n        float dist = -mv.z;\n        float size = uSize * (.4 + aSeed.z * 1.6) * (1. + uVel * uReact * 2.);\n        gl_PointSize = clamp(size * uPx * 10. / max(dist, .1) * 8., 1., 64.);\n        gl_Position = projectionMatrix * mv;\n        // fade very near (avoid pops) and very far\n        vA = smoothstep(.6, 3., dist) * (1. - smoothstep(26., 52., dist)) * (.35 + aSeed.x * .65);\n        // twinkle\n        vA *= .65 + .35 * sin(t * (.8 + aSeed.y * 2.) + aSeed.w * 30.);\n        vMix = aSeed.y;\n      }`,\n    fragmentShader: /* glsl */ `\n      uniform vec3 uC1, uC2; uniform float uOpacity; varying float vA; varying float vMix;\n      void main(){\n        float d = length(gl_PointCoord - .5);\n        float a = smoothstep(.5, 0., d); a *= a;\n        if (a < .003) discard;\n        gl_FragColor = vec4(mix(uC1, uC2, vMix), a * vA * uOpacity);\n      }`,\n  });\n\n  const points = new THREE.Points(geo, mat);\n  points.frustumCulled = false;\n  return {\n    points,\n    uniforms: mat.uniforms,\n    /** call from AuraScene.update(f) */\n    update(time: number, velocity: number) {\n      mat.uniforms.uTime.value = time;\n      mat.uniforms.uVel.value = Math.min(velocity, 1.5);\n    },\n  };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/rock.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport { noiseTexture } from \"./materials\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · rock.ts — asteroid / basalt / lunar ground for product altars.\n   A displaced plane with craters, a flat polished SHELF in the middle (so the product sits on something),\n   a noise roughness map (uniform roughness = CG tell), plus instanced boulders. No assets.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nconst h2 = (x: number, y: number, s: number) => { const v = Math.sin(x * 127.1 + y * 311.7 + s * 74.7) * 43758.5453; return v - Math.floor(v); };\nfunction vnoise(x: number, y: number, s: number) {\n  const xi = Math.floor(x), yi = Math.floor(y), tx = x - xi, ty = y - yi;\n  const sx = tx * tx * (3 - 2 * tx), sy = ty * ty * (3 - 2 * ty);\n  const a = h2(xi, yi, s), b = h2(xi + 1, yi, s), c = h2(xi, yi + 1, s), d = h2(xi + 1, yi + 1, s);\n  return a + (b - a) * sx + (c - a) * sy + (a - b - c + d) * sx * sy;\n}\nexport const fbm2 = (x: number, y: number, s = 1, oct = 5) => { let v = 0, a = 0.5, f = 1; for (let i = 0; i < oct; i++) { v += vnoise(x * f, y * f, s + i) * a; a *= 0.5; f *= 2.03; } return v; };\n\nexport interface RockGroundOptions { size?: number; segments?: number; height?: number; shelfRadius?: number; color?: THREE.ColorRepresentation; seed?: number; craters?: number }\n\nexport function createRockGround(o: RockGroundOptions = {}) {\n  const size = o.size ?? 120, seg = o.segments ?? 220, H = o.height ?? 5, SR = o.shelfRadius ?? 4.2, seed = o.seed ?? 3;\n  const craters = Array.from({ length: o.craters ?? 14 }, (_, i) => {\n    const a = h2(i, 1, seed) * Math.PI * 2, r = 9 + h2(i, 2, seed) * size * 0.4;\n    return { x: Math.cos(a) * r, z: Math.sin(a) * r, r: 2.5 + h2(i, 3, seed) * 9, d: 0.5 + h2(i, 4, seed) * 1.6 };\n  });\n  const g = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(size, size, seg, seg);\n  g.rotateX(-Math.PI / 2);\n  const pos = g.attributes.position as THREE.BufferAttribute;\n  const heightAt = (x: number, z: number) => {\n    let h = (fbm2(x * 0.045, z * 0.045, seed) - 0.5) * H * 2 + (fbm2(x * 0.22, z * 0.22, seed + 9, 4) - 0.5) * H * 0.35;\n    for (const c of craters) { // bowl + raised rim\n      const d = Math.hypot(x - c.x, z - c.z) / c.r;\n      if (d < 1.35) h += d < 1 ? -c.d * (1 - d * d) : c.d * 0.35 * Math.exp(-(((d - 1) * 6) ** 2));\n    }\n    const dist = Math.hypot(x, z);\n    const shelf = 1 - Math.min(1, Math.max(0, (dist - SR) / (SR * 1.6))); // 1 on the shelf → 0 outside\n    const k = shelf * shelf * (3 - 2 * shelf);\n    return h * (1 - k) - 0.14 * k; // the shelf sits just BELOW y=0 so the altar's top face (y=0) never z-fights the rock\n  };\n  for (let i = 0; i < pos.count; i++) pos.setY(i, heightAt(pos.getX(i), pos.getZ(i)));\n  g.computeVertexNormals();\n  const rough = noiseTexture(512, 6, seed);\n  rough.repeat.set(24, 24);\n  const bump = noiseTexture(512, 6, seed + 4);\n  bump.repeat.set(40, 40);\n  const mat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: o.color ?? 0x3b424b, roughness: 1, roughnessMap: rough, bumpMap: bump, bumpScale: 2.2, metalness: 0.15, envMapIntensity: 0.5 });\n  const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(g, mat);\n  // polished obsidian disc on the shelf: the product's \"altar\" — glossy enough to catch the strip lights\n  const altar = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(SR * 0.62, SR * 0.66, 0.18, 96), new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({ color: 0x0b0e13, roughness: 0.22, metalness: 0.5, clearcoat: 1, clearcoatRoughness: 0.08, envMapIntensity: 1.4 }));\n  altar.position.y = -0.09;\n  const group = new THREE.Group();\n  group.add(mesh, altar);\n  return { group, mesh, altar, heightAt };\n}\n\n/** Instanced, noise-displaced boulders scattered outside the shelf. One draw call. */\nexport function createBoulders(o: { count?: number; ground: { heightAt: (x: number, z: number) => number }; inner?: number; outer?: number; color?: THREE.ColorRepresentation; seed?: number } ) {\n  const n = o.count ?? 90, seed = o.seed ?? 5, inner = o.inner ?? 9, outer = o.outer ?? 55;\n  const geo = new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1, 3);\n  const p = geo.attributes.position as THREE.BufferAttribute, v = new THREE.Vector3();\n  for (let i = 0; i < p.count; i++) { v.fromBufferAttribute(p, i); const d = 0.72 + fbm2(v.x * 1.7 + 5, v.y * 1.7 + v.z * 1.3, seed, 3) * 0.7; p.setXYZ(i, v.x * d, v.y * d * 0.8, v.z * d); }\n  geo.computeVertexNormals();\n  const mesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(geo, new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: o.color ?? 0x2d333b, roughness: 0.92, metalness: 0.1, envMapIntensity: 0.4 }), n);\n  const d = new THREE.Object3D();\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    const a = h2(i, 7, seed) * Math.PI * 2, r = inner + Math.pow(h2(i, 8, seed), 0.7) * (outer - inner);\n    const x = Math.cos(a) * r, z = Math.sin(a) * r, s = 0.4 + Math.pow(h2(i, 9, seed), 2.4) * 5.5;\n    d.position.set(x, o.ground.heightAt(x, z) + s * 0.25, z);\n    d.rotation.set(h2(i, 10, seed) * 6, h2(i, 11, seed) * 6, 0);\n    d.scale.set(s * (0.8 + h2(i, 12, seed) * 0.6), s * 0.8, s);\n    d.updateMatrix();\n    mesh.setMatrixAt(i, d.matrix);\n  }\n  return mesh;\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/scene.example.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport type { AuraScene, BuildContext, Frame, Shot } from \"./engine\";\nimport { iridescent, obsidian, emissive, lightRig } from \"./materials\";\nimport { createParticles } from \"./particles\";\nimport { createMirrorFloor } from \"./floor\";\nimport { ease, seg } from \"./choreo\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · scene.example.ts — \"Obsidian Gate\". A complete, zero-asset reference scene.\n   Read it as a pattern, not a product. Study how it:\n     · builds 5+ depth layers (floor reflection · hero · rings · shards · dust · fog)\n     · lights with a key / rim / fill / practical rig and a coloured hero glow\n     · drives EVERYTHING from the single damped playhead f.p\n     · sequences a shot list with deliberate pacing (establish → push → macro → crane → fly-through)\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nexport const OBSIDIAN_SHOTS: Shot[] = [\n  { at: 0.0, pos: [0, 1.4, 15], look: [0, 1.6, 0], fov: 38 }, //  1 establish — wide, quiet, a lot of air\n  { at: 0.2, pos: [3.4, 1.8, 7.5], look: [0, 1.5, 0], fov: 34 }, //  2 push-in — the object grows, parallax wakes\n  { at: 0.4, pos: [-2.6, 2.2, 3.4], look: [0, 1.6, 0], fov: 24, ease: \"inOut\" }, //  3 macro orbit — telephoto compression, detail\n  { at: 0.58, pos: [-2.6, 2.2, 3.4], look: [0, 1.6, 0], fov: 24 }, //     …HOLD (same shot twice) so the copy can be read\n  { at: 0.78, pos: [0.6, 6.5, -1.5], look: [0, 0.5, -7], fov: 52 }, //  4 crane up and over — reveal the whole stage\n  { at: 1.0, pos: [0, 2.7, 11.5], look: [0, 1.7, 0], fov: 30 }, //  5 FINAL IMAGE — swing back to a composed, symmetrical hero frame (the last frame is a poster, never empty space)\n];\n\nexport function obsidianGate({ env, mobile, quality }: BuildContext): AuraScene {\n  const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n  const BG = 0x03050b;\n  scene.background = new THREE.Color(BG);\n  scene.fog = new THREE.FogExp2(BG, mobile ? 0.04 : 0.032);\n  scene.environment = env;\n  scene.environmentIntensity = 0.32;\n\n  // ── light: designed, not default\n  const rig = lightRig({ key: 0xfff0dd, rim: 0x5ea2ff, practical: 0xff8a4c });\n  rig.rim.intensity = 2.6; // a rim light should trace the edge, not flood the face — tune by eye\n  scene.add(rig.group);\n\n  // ── hero object: an iridescent crystal with an inner emissive core\n  const hero = new THREE.Group();\n  const shell = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1.35, 1), iridescent(0x0a0d14));\n  Object.assign(shell.material, { flatShading: true, roughness: 0.34, clearcoatRoughness: 0.32, envMapIntensity: 1 });\n  // // facets catch the rim light — a smooth orb reads as a toy\n  const core = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(0.55, 2), emissive(0x7fd6ff, 3.2));\n  hero.add(shell, core);\n  hero.position.y = 1.6;\n  scene.add(hero);\n\n  // ── rings: thin emissive tori on different axes → scale cue + orbital motion\n  const rings: THREE.Mesh[] = [];\n  [2.4, 3.3, 4.6].forEach((r, i) => {\n    const m = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.TorusGeometry(r, 0.012 + i * 0.006, 8, 220), emissive(i === 1 ? 0xffb37a : 0x6fb4ff, 1.6));\n    m.position.y = 1.6;\n    m.rotation.set(Math.PI / 2 + i * 0.35, i * 0.6, 0);\n    rings.push(m);\n    scene.add(m);\n  });\n\n  // ── shards: 1 instanced draw call, 110 floating obsidian fragments\n  const N = mobile ? 60 : 110;\n  const shards = new THREE.InstancedMesh(new THREE.TetrahedronGeometry(0.22), obsidian(0x080a10), N);\n  const seeds = Array.from({ length: N }, () => ({ a: Math.random() * 6.28, r: 3.4 + Math.random() * 6, y: Math.random() * 5, s: 0.4 + Math.random() * 1.6, sp: 0.04 + Math.random() * 0.12, z: (Math.random() - 0.5) * 18 }));\n  scene.add(shards);\n  const dummy = new THREE.Object3D();\n\n  // ── floor: reflective, fades into fog\n  const floor = createMirrorFloor({ color: 0x3a4152, fadeColor: BG, resolution: mobile ? 0.35 : 0.5 });\n  floor.setEnabled(quality > 0.5);\n  scene.add(floor.group);\n\n  // ── atmosphere: two particle bands (near bokeh + far dust)\n  const dustFar = createParticles({ count: mobile ? 900 : 2600, extent: [16, 9, 34], size: 1.6, color: 0xbfe0ff, color2: 0xffd2a0, opacity: 0.7 });\n  const dustNear = createParticles({ count: mobile ? 60 : 160, extent: [9, 5, 14], size: 7, color: 0x9fd0ff, color2: 0xffa56a, opacity: 0.35, speed: 1.6 });\n  scene.add(dustFar.points, dustNear.points);\n\n  const hue = new THREE.Color();\n  return {\n    scene,\n    shots: OBSIDIAN_SHOTS,\n    fov: 38,\n    exposure: 0.9,\n    bloom: { strength: mobile ? 0.45 : 0.65, radius: 0.7, threshold: 0.88 },\n    grade: { vignette: 0.7, grain: 0.05, contrast: 1.08, saturation: 1.08, shadows: [0, 0.015, 0.06], highlights: [0.07, 0.035, 0] },\n    update(f: Frame) {\n      const { p, time: t } = f;\n      // the hero turns with scroll (not time) → scrub, never autoplay\n      hero.rotation.y = p * Math.PI * 3.2 + t * 0.05;\n      hero.rotation.x = Math.sin(p * Math.PI * 2) * 0.35;\n      hero.position.y = 1.6 + Math.sin(t * 0.6) * 0.06;\n      // the core \"ignites\" during the macro shot\n      const ign = ease.inOutCubic(seg(p, 0.25, 0.45)) * (1 - seg(p, 0.7, 0.85));\n      (core.material as THREE.MeshStandardMaterial).emissiveIntensity = 1.2 + ign * 3;\n      hue.setHSL(0.55 + p * 0.22, 0.85, 0.62);\n      (core.material as THREE.MeshStandardMaterial).emissive.copy(hue);\n      rig.practical.intensity = 9 + ign * 22 + Math.sin(t * 7) * 0.6; // a living, slightly unstable light\n      rings.forEach((r, i) => { r.rotation.z += f.dt * (0.15 + i * 0.1) * (1 + f.velocity * 6); r.scale.setScalar(1 + ign * 0.08 * (i + 1)); });\n      for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {\n        const s = seeds[i], a = s.a + t * s.sp;\n        dummy.position.set(Math.cos(a) * s.r, s.y + Math.sin(t * 0.3 + i) * 0.2, Math.sin(a) * s.r * 0.6 + s.z * 0.4);\n        dummy.rotation.set(t * s.sp * 3 + i, t * s.sp * 2, i);\n        dummy.scale.setScalar(s.s);\n        dummy.updateMatrix();\n        shards.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);\n      }\n      shards.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true;\n      dustFar.update(t, f.velocity);\n      dustNear.update(t, f.velocity);\n      floor.setEnabled(f.quality > 0.5);\n    },\n  };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/scene.folio.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport type { AuraScene, BuildContext, Frame, Shot, V3 } from \"./engine\";\nimport { createParticles } from \"./particles\";\nimport { ease, seg } from \"./choreo\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · scene.folio.ts — \"Mira\". World #4 — a FOLIO (portfolio / studio / personal brand).\n   Archetype: EDITORIAL GALLERY IN DEPTH. Where the other worlds are *places*, a folio is a *sequence of proofs*:\n   each project gets a plate, a hold, a name — and the camera climbs a helix from one to the next.\n   Concept:   six projects as luminous plates on a spiral around a spine of light; the active plate ignites, the rest sleep.\n   Palette:   ink (dominant) · one electric accent per project, picked from the work · bone type\n   Lighting:  the plates ARE the light (emissive faces + one travelling accent point light) over an ink floor that mirrors them.\n   Rhythm:    wide → arrive → HOLD (read the work) → climb → … → pull back to see the whole spiral → final frame + CTA.\n   Replace PROJECTS (title / hue / kind) — and, for real work, swap plateTexture() for loadTexture(\"/work/x.jpg\"). Never ship the fake UI.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nexport const PROJECTS = [\n  { title: \"Halcyon\", kind: \"Brand system\", hue: 168 },\n  { title: \"Ferro\", kind: \"E-commerce\", hue: 28 },\n  { title: \"Lumen\", kind: \"Mobile app\", hue: 212 },\n  { title: \"Oriel\", kind: \"Editorial\", hue: 330 },\n  { title: \"Vanta\", kind: \"Campaign\", hue: 268 },\n  { title: \"Nine\", kind: \"Data product\", hue: 48 },\n] as const;\n\nconst N = PROJECTS.length, R = 7.2, RISE = 3.1, TURN = 1.02;\nconst ang = (i: number) => i * TURN;\nconst plateAt = (i: number): V3 => [Math.cos(ang(i)) * R, 1.6 + i * RISE, Math.sin(ang(i)) * R];\n/** playhead position where project i is centred (and held) */\nexport const folioAt = (i: number) => 0.1 + (i / (N - 1)) * 0.68;\n\n/** A believable fake UI screen, drawn to a canvas. (Stand-in only — your real work goes here.) */\nfunction plateTexture(i: number): THREE.CanvasTexture {\n  const W = 1280, H = 800, c = document.createElement(\"canvas\");\n  c.width = W; c.height = H;\n  const g = c.getContext(\"2d\")!, hue = PROJECTS[i].hue;\n  const bg = g.createLinearGradient(0, 0, W, H);\n  bg.addColorStop(0, `hsl(${hue} 55% 9%)`); bg.addColorStop(1, `hsl(${hue + 30} 60% 17%)`);\n  g.fillStyle = bg; g.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);\n  const glow = g.createRadialGradient(W * 0.78, H * 0.3, 0, W * 0.78, H * 0.3, W * 0.5);\n  glow.addColorStop(0, `hsla(${hue} 100% 62% / .55)`); glow.addColorStop(1, \"transparent\");\n  g.fillStyle = glow; g.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);\n  g.fillStyle = \"rgba(255,255,255,.08)\"; g.fillRect(0, 0, W, 64);\n  [0, 1, 2].forEach((k) => { g.fillStyle = `rgba(255,255,255,${0.35 - k * 0.1})`; g.beginPath(); g.arc(34 + k * 26, 32, 7, 0, 7); g.fill(); });\n  g.fillStyle = \"#f4f1ea\"; g.font = \"700 150px 'Space Grotesk Variable','Space Grotesk',system-ui,sans-serif\";\n  g.fillText(PROJECTS[i].title, 70, 330);\n  g.fillStyle = `hsl(${hue} 100% 70%)`; g.font = \"500 30px 'JetBrains Mono',monospace\";\n  g.fillText(`${String(i + 1).padStart(2, \"0\")} — ${PROJECTS[i].kind.toUpperCase()}`, 74, 400);\n  for (let k = 0; k < 3; k++) {\n    const x = 70 + k * 392; g.fillStyle = \"rgba(255,255,255,.07)\"; g.beginPath(); g.roundRect(x, 470, 360, 250, 22); g.fill();\n    g.fillStyle = `hsla(${hue + k * 18} 100% 66% / .85)`; for (let b = 0; b < 7; b++) g.fillRect(x + 30 + b * 44, 690 - ((b * 37 + k * 53 + i * 19) % 150) - 30, 26, ((b * 37 + k * 53 + i * 19) % 150) + 30);\n  }\n  const t = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c);\n  t.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace; t.anisotropy = 8;\n  return t;\n}\n\nexport function folio({ env, mobile }: BuildContext): AuraScene {\n  const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n  scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x05060a);\n  scene.fog = new THREE.FogExp2(0x05060a, mobile ? 0.026 : 0.02);\n  scene.environment = env;\n  scene.environmentIntensity = 0.3;\n\n  // ── floor: ink that mirrors the plates (cheap: a glossy env-lit disc, no second render)\n  const floor = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CircleGeometry(90, 64), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x07080d, roughness: 0.22, metalness: 0.85 }));\n  floor.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2; floor.position.y = -1.2; scene.add(floor);\n\n  // ── spine of light + rings at every plate height\n  const spine = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.05, 0.05, N * RISE + 8, 12), new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0xbfe9ff }));\n  spine.position.y = (N * RISE) / 2; scene.add(spine);\n  const rings: THREE.Mesh[] = [];\n  const plates: { grp: THREE.Group; face: THREE.Mesh; mat: THREE.MeshBasicMaterial; edge: THREE.LineSegments }[] = [];\n  PROJECTS.forEach((pr, i) => {\n    const [x, y, z] = plateAt(i);\n    const col = new THREE.Color().setHSL(pr.hue / 360, 1, 0.62);\n    const ring = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.TorusGeometry(R, 0.012, 6, 120), new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: col, transparent: true, opacity: 0.35 }));\n    ring.rotation.x = Math.PI / 2; ring.position.y = y; scene.add(ring); rings.push(ring);\n\n    const grp = new THREE.Group();\n    const mat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map: plateTexture(i), toneMapped: false });\n    const face = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(6.4, 4), mat);\n    const body = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(6.6, 4.2, 0.16), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x0a0c12, roughness: 0.3, metalness: 0.9 }));\n    body.position.z = -0.1;\n    const edge = new THREE.LineSegments(new THREE.EdgesGeometry(new THREE.BoxGeometry(6.6, 4.2, 0.16)), new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({ color: col, transparent: true, opacity: 0.5 }));\n    edge.position.z = -0.1;\n    grp.add(body, face, edge);\n    grp.position.set(x, y, z); grp.rotation.y = Math.PI / 2 - ang(i);\n    scene.add(grp);\n    plates.push({ grp, face, mat, edge });\n    // a tether from the spine, so the plates read as hung in space, not floating props\n    const tether = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(0.012, 0.012, R, 6), new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: col, transparent: true, opacity: 0.4 }));\n    tether.position.set(x / 2, y, z / 2); tether.rotation.z = Math.PI / 2; tether.rotation.y = -ang(i); scene.add(tether);\n  });\n\n  // ── light: one accent point light travels with the active project\n  scene.add(new THREE.HemisphereLight(0x8aa0ff, 0x05060a, 0.25));\n  const accent = new THREE.PointLight(0xffffff, 0, 40, 1.6);\n  scene.add(accent);\n\n  // ── depth planes: dust close to the lens (foreground) + far motes (atmosphere)\n  const dust = createParticles({ count: mobile ? 500 : 1100, size: 0.05, color: 0xcfe6ff, extent: [34, 40, 34], opacity: 0.6 });\n  dust.points.position.y = 14; scene.add(dust.points);\n  const motes = createParticles({ count: mobile ? 60 : 140, size: 0.12, color: 0xffffff, extent: [10, 6, 10], opacity: 0.8 });\n  scene.add(motes.points);\n\n  // ── the shot list: wide → [arrive, HOLD] × 6 → pull back → final\n  const shots: Shot[] = [{ at: 0, pos: [R * 2.6, 3.5, R * 1.1], look: [0, 6, 0], fov: 42 }];\n  PROJECTS.forEach((_, i) => {\n    const [x, y, z] = plateAt(i), a = ang(i), ox = Math.cos(a), oz = Math.sin(a), tx = -Math.sin(a), tz = Math.cos(a);\n    const d = mobile ? 16 : 12.4, at = folioAt(i);   // distance tuned so the plate fills ~70% of frame height at fov 30\n    shots.push({ at: at - 0.035, pos: [x + ox * d + tx * 2.2, y + 0.5, z + oz * d + tz * 2.2], look: [x, y, z], fov: 30, ease: \"inOut\" });\n    shots.push({ at: at + 0.035, pos: [x + ox * d + tx * 2.08, y + 0.5, z + oz * d + tz * 2.08], look: [x, y, z], fov: 30, ease: \"inOut\" }); // HOLD — the camera all but stops while the work is read\n  });\n  // pull back to see the whole spiral, then HOLD the final image (the CTA lives on it)\n  const cy = (N * RISE) / 2 + 1.6, far: V3 = mobile ? [-22, cy + 2, 20] : [-16, cy + 1, 14.5];\n  shots.push({ at: 0.93, pos: far, look: [0, cy, 0], fov: mobile ? 56 : 50, ease: \"inOut\" });\n  shots.push({ at: 1, pos: [far[0] - 0.4, far[1], far[2] + 0.3], look: [0, cy, 0], fov: mobile ? 56 : 50 });\n\n  const tmp = new THREE.Color();\n  return {\n    scene, shots, exposure: 1.0, parallax: 0.6,\n    bloom: { strength: 0.42, radius: 0.7, threshold: 0.82 },\n    grade: { vignette: 0.7, grain: 0.05, contrast: 1.08, saturation: 1.08, shadows: [0.0, 0.01, 0.04] },\n    update(f: Frame) {\n      const { p, time: t } = f;\n      let nearest = 0, best = 9;\n      plates.forEach((pl, i) => {\n        const dp = Math.abs(p - folioAt(i)), focus = 1 - Math.min(1, dp / 0.11), e = ease.inOutCubic(focus);\n        if (dp < best) { best = dp; nearest = i; }\n        pl.mat.color.setScalar(0.28 + 0.72 * e);                 // sleeping plates dim, the active one ignites\n        (pl.edge.material as THREE.LineBasicMaterial).opacity = 0.2 + 0.7 * e;\n        (rings[i].material as THREE.MeshBasicMaterial).opacity = 0.12 + 0.5 * e;\n        pl.grp.scale.setScalar(0.94 + 0.06 * e + Math.sin(t * 0.6 + i) * 0.004);\n        pl.grp.position.y = plateAt(i)[1] + Math.sin(t * 0.5 + i * 1.7) * 0.06;\n      });\n      // the travelling accent light sits just in front of the active plate\n      const [x, y, z] = plateAt(nearest), a = ang(nearest);\n      tmp.setHSL(PROJECTS[nearest].hue / 360, 1, 0.6);\n      accent.color.lerp(tmp, 0.08);\n      accent.position.lerp(new THREE.Vector3(x + Math.cos(a) * 3.2, y + 0.4, z + Math.sin(a) * 3.2), 0.1);\n      accent.intensity = 28 + 10 * Math.sin(t * 1.3);\n      (spine.material as THREE.MeshBasicMaterial).color.lerp(tmp, 0.04);\n      spine.scale.x = spine.scale.z = 1 + 1.4 * ease.inOutSine(seg(p, 0.82, 0.95));\n      dust.update(t, f.velocity);\n      motes.points.position.lerp(f.camera.position, 0.08);       // lags the lens → foreground parallax plane\n      motes.update(t, f.velocity);\n    },\n  };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/scene.halo.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport type { AuraScene, BuildContext, Frame, Shot } from \"./engine\";\nimport { createStudioEnv, productRig, createContactShadow } from \"./studio\";\nimport { createRockGround, createBoulders } from \"./rock\";\nimport { earbuds } from \"./objects\";\nimport { createParticles } from \"./particles\";\nimport { createSky, createGlow } from \"./atmosphere\";\nimport { ease, seg, pulse } from \"./choreo\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · scene.halo.ts — \"NOCTA\". World #3, a PRODUCT ALTAR (direction 01 in references/11-art-directions.md).\n   Concept:   premium earbuds resting on a polished obsidian altar on a cratered asteroid, a black planet limb behind.\n              \"Silence, engineered.\" The environment IS the benefit.\n   Hero:      earbuds() — case, lid, two buds and three internal parts that rise in the Exploded Ascent.\n   Palette:   #05070b void · #7dffd9 LED accent · #9fb4c8 cold rock rim · #e9f1f6 type\n   Signature: THE EXPLODED ASCENT (p .38 → .62) — parts rise along ONE axis, staggered, with projected leader-line labels.\n   Light:     designed studio environment (strip softboxes) + cold raking key + hard rim + mint practical from the LED.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\n/** Live 3D anchors the DOM shell projects to screen each frame (leader-line labels). `k` = visibility 0..1. */\nexport const haloAnchors = { battery: new THREE.Vector3(), pcb: new THREE.Vector3(), driver: new THREE.Vector3(), k: 0 };\n\nexport function halo({ renderer, mobile }: BuildContext): AuraScene {\n  const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n  const VOID = 0x04060a;\n  scene.background = new THREE.Color(VOID);\n  scene.fog = new THREE.FogExp2(0x070b12, mobile ? 0.016 : 0.011);\n  scene.environment = createStudioEnv(renderer, { accent: { color: 0x7dffd9, intensity: 3, pos: [6, 0.5, 8], size: [0.5, 5] } });\n  scene.environmentIntensity = 0.7;\n\n  // ── ground: asteroid + polished altar + boulders\n  const ground = createRockGround({ size: 150, segments: mobile ? 120 : 220, height: 5.5, shelfRadius: 4.4, color: 0x2b323b });\n  scene.add(ground.group);\n  scene.add(createBoulders({ count: mobile ? 50 : 100, ground, inner: 10, outer: 62 }));\n  scene.add(createContactShadow(3.4, 0.8));\n\n  // ── sky + planet limb (cold rim light on a black sphere)\n  const sky = createSky({ top: 0x010207, horizon: 0x0b1522, ground: 0x04060a, sunDir: [-0.5, 0.28, -1], sunColor: 0xcfe4ff, sunSize: 0.009, stars: 1.0 });\n  scene.add(sky.mesh);\n  const planet = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(150, 96, 64), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x06080c, roughness: 1, fog: false }));\n  planet.position.set(-70, 62, -330);\n  const atmos = new THREE.Mesh(\n    new THREE.SphereGeometry(153, 96, 64),\n    new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      transparent: true, depthWrite: false, blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending, fog: false,\n      uniforms: { uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(0x6fb4ff) }, uK: { value: 1 } },\n      vertexShader: `varying vec3 vN; varying vec3 vV; void main(){ vec4 mv = modelViewMatrix*vec4(position,1.); vN = normalize(normalMatrix*normal); vV = normalize(-mv.xyz); gl_Position = projectionMatrix*mv; }`,\n      fragmentShader: `uniform vec3 uColor; uniform float uK; varying vec3 vN; varying vec3 vV; void main(){ float f = pow(1.-clamp(dot(vN,vV),0.,1.), 4.5); gl_FragColor = vec4(uColor*f*1.6*uK, f*uK); }`,\n    }),\n  );\n  atmos.position.copy(planet.position);\n  scene.add(planet, atmos);\n\n  // ── hero\n  const pod = earbuds({ accent: 0x7dffd9 });\n  scene.add(pod.group);\n  const rig = productRig({ accent: 0x7dffd9 });\n  scene.add(rig.group);\n  rig.accent.position.set(0, 0.7, 1.4);\n  const ledGlow = createGlow(0x7dffd9, 0.9, 0.0);\n  ledGlow.position.set(0, 0.42, 0.95);\n  scene.add(ledGlow);\n\n  // ── atmosphere: cold dust band around the altar + near motes that lag the lens (the foreground depth plane)\n  const dust = createParticles({ count: mobile ? 500 : 1400, extent: [16, 6, 22], size: 0.7, color: 0x9fc4ff, color2: 0xcfe0f5, opacity: 0.16, speed: 0.5 });\n  dust.points.position.set(0, 3, 0);\n  const motes = createParticles({ count: mobile ? 30 : 90, extent: [6, 3.5, 8], size: 2.8, color: 0x7dffd9, color2: 0xb8f5e4, opacity: 0.10, speed: 1.2 });\n  scene.add(dust.points, motes.points);\n\n  // ── THE RAIL — 8 shots. wide/close alternation, one hold (.74–.9), one composed last frame.\n  const shots: Shot[] = [\n    { at: 0.0, pos: [-4.6, 0.8, 6.0], look: [0.5, 0.95, 0], fov: 26 }, //                 1 reveal — low, raking the lid edge\n    { at: 0.12, pos: [2.2, 2.0, 3.4], look: [-0.1, 1.1, -0.4], fov: 20 }, //              2 macro — across the lacquer to the hinge\n    { at: 0.28, pos: [3.8, 2.6, 6.2], look: [0, 1.3, 0], fov: 28, ease: \"inOut\" }, //       3 crane — the case opens\n    { at: 0.42, pos: [5.6, 3.9, 9.8], look: [0, 3.1, 0], fov: 30 }, //                       4 anatomy — slightly high\n    { at: 0.6, pos: [4.2, 2.6, 11.5], look: [0, 2.2, 0], fov: 30 }, //                       5 anatomy settles\n    { at: 0.7, pos: [10.5, 3.4, 30], look: [-2, 6.5, -40], fov: 36, ease: \"inOut\" }, //     6 context — pull back, the planet limb enters\n    { at: 0.82, pos: [-4.8, 1.1, 9.2], look: [0, 1.2, 0], fov: 25, ease: \"inOut\" }, //       7 HERO — 3/4 low\n    { at: 1.0, pos: [-3.5, 1.2, 7.4], look: [0, 1.2, 0], fov: 23 }, //                       8 final — slow dolly in, centred\n  ];\n\n  const tmp = new THREE.Vector3();\n  const names = [\"battery\", \"pcb\"] as const;\n  return {\n    scene, shots, fov: 28, exposure: 0.9, parallax: 0.5,\n    bloom: { strength: mobile ? 0.4 : 0.55, radius: 0.6, threshold: 1.15 },\n    grade: { vignette: 0.75, grain: 0.05, contrast: 1.12, saturation: 0.95, shadows: [0.0, 0.012, 0.03], highlights: [0.0, 0.03, 0.03] },\n    update(f: Frame) {\n      const { p, time: t } = f;\n      // the case opens (.26–.40) and stays open\n      pod.setOpen(ease.inOutCubic(seg(p, 0.26, 0.4)));\n      // exploded ascent: each part rises on its own staggered window, then re-seats\n      pod.parts.forEach((part, i) => {\n        const rise = ease.inOutCubic(seg(p, 0.38 + i * 0.012, 0.5 + i * 0.012));\n        const fall = ease.inOutCubic(seg(p, 0.6 + (pod.parts.length - i) * 0.008, 0.7));\n        const e = rise * (1 - fall);\n        part.obj.position.set(part.rest.x + (part.spreadX ?? 0) * e, part.rest.y + part.lift * e, part.rest.z);\n        if (part.name.startsWith(\"bud\")) part.obj.rotation.y = e * (part.name === \"budL\" ? 0.6 : -0.6) + Math.sin(t * 0.6 + i) * 0.04 * e;\n      });\n      // labels: project from live part positions\n      const byName = (n: string) => pod.parts.find((q) => q.name === n)!.obj;\n      names.forEach((n) => byName(n).getWorldPosition(haloAnchors[n]));\n      byName(\"budR\").getWorldPosition(haloAnchors.driver);\n      haloAnchors.k = pulse(p, 0.46, 0.62, 0.05, 0.05);\n      // LED breathes, ramps as the case opens, steadies for the hero\n      const ign = ease.inOutCubic(seg(p, 0.26, 0.42));\n      const led = 0.9 + ign * 2.6 + Math.sin(t * 1.6) * 0.35 * (1 - seg(p, 0.8, 1));\n      pod.setLed(led);\n      rig.accent.intensity = led * 0.9;\n      (ledGlow.material as THREE.SpriteMaterial).opacity = 0.06 + ign * 0.18;\n      // the planet limb arrives with the pull-back\n      (atmos.material as THREE.ShaderMaterial).uniforms.uK.value = 0.35 + 0.65 * seg(p, 0.56, 0.74);\n      sky.update(t);\n      // rim light warms to the accent in the hero hold\n      rig.rim.intensity = 2.4 + 0.8 * seg(p, 0.76, 0.9);\n      motes.points.position.lerp(f.camera.position, 0.07);\n      dust.update(t, f.velocity);\n      motes.update(t, f.velocity);\n      void tmp;\n    },\n  };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/scene.umbra.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport type { AuraScene, BuildContext, Frame, Shot } from \"./engine\";\nimport { obsidian, emissive, lightRig } from \"./materials\";\nimport { createParticles } from \"./particles\";\nimport { createSky, createLightShafts, createGlow, createHaze } from \"./atmosphere\";\nimport { createDunes } from \"./terrain\";\nimport { ease, seg } from \"./choreo\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · scene.umbra.ts — \"Umbra\". World #2, built ONLY from the starter's helpers (no assets, ~190 lines).\n   Archetype: EVENT / CAMPAIGN — a journey with a destination.\n   Concept:   a desert valley at the moment of totality; a lone obsidian monolith; the camera walks toward the dark.\n   Palette:   ember orange (accent) · bruise navy (dominant) · bone white (type)\n   Lighting:  the ONLY warm light is the eclipse corona, BEHIND the subject → everything is rim-lit silhouette.\n              A cold, dim fill from the camera side keeps the dunes legible. Warm vs cold = depth.\n   The last frame is composed on purpose: monolith centred, corona ringing its top, CTA below.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nexport function umbra({ env, mobile, quality }: BuildContext): AuraScene {\n  const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n  const HORIZON = 0x34150a, ZENITH = 0x04050c;\n  scene.background = new THREE.Color(ZENITH);\n  scene.fog = new THREE.FogExp2(0x20100b, mobile ? 0.0105 : 0.0085); // fog ≈ the sky's horizon colour so dunes melt into the glow\n  scene.environment = env;\n  scene.environmentIntensity = 0.18;\n\n  // ── ground + geometry anchors\n  const dunes = createDunes({ size: 460, segments: mobile ? 150 : 230, height: 11, color: 0x1d130f, sheen: 0xff7a3a, valley: { width: 24, sway: 12 } });\n  scene.add(dunes.mesh);\n  const gy = (z: number) => dunes.heightAt(dunes.valleyX(z), z);\n  const vx = dunes.valleyX;\n  const MZ = -72, MX = vx(MZ), MH = 13, BASE = gy(MZ);\n\n  // ── sky: the eclipse. direction chosen so that from the FINAL camera the disc sits right behind the monolith's top\n  const sky = createSky({ top: ZENITH, horizon: HORIZON, ground: 0x1a0b08, sunDir: [0, 0.205, -1], sunColor: 0xff9455, sunSize: 0.072, eclipse: 0.5, stars: 0.9 });\n  scene.add(sky.mesh);\n  const sunWorld = sky.sunPosition(380);\n  const corona = createGlow(0xff8a4a, 110, 0.0);\n  corona.position.copy(sunWorld);\n  scene.add(corona);\n\n  // ── light: warm rim from behind the subject, cold dim fill from the camera side\n  const rig = lightRig({ key: 0x6f8fc8, rim: 0xff8a45, fill: 0x1a2038, practical: 0xff7a35 });\n  rig.key.intensity = 0.55; rig.key.position.set(MX + 30, 40, 60);\n  rig.rim.intensity = 5; rig.rim.position.set(MX, 18, -140);\n  rig.fill.intensity = 0.5;\n  rig.practical.position.set(MX, BASE + 7, MZ + 4); rig.practical.distance = 40; rig.practical.intensity = 0;\n  scene.add(rig.group);\n\n  // ── hero: the monolith (obsidian slab) with a hairline emissive seam that \"ignites\" at totality\n  const mono = new THREE.Group();\n  const slab = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(2.6, MH, 0.8, 1, 1, 1), obsidian(0x06070c));\n  Object.assign(slab.material, { roughness: 0.16, metalness: 0.5, envMapIntensity: 1.6 });\n  slab.position.y = MH / 2;\n  const seam = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.035, MH * 0.92, 0.02), emissive(0xffb070, 2));\n  seam.position.set(0, MH / 2, 0.41);\n  const plinth = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.CylinderGeometry(3.4, 3.8, 0.25, 64), obsidian(0x090a10));\n  plinth.position.y = 0.05;\n  mono.add(slab, seam, plinth);\n  mono.position.set(MX, BASE - 0.15, MZ);\n  scene.add(mono);\n\n  // ── an orbit of slate fragments circling the monolith (1 draw call) — turns with scroll, never with time\n  const N = mobile ? 28 : 56;\n  const frags = new THREE.InstancedMesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.7, 0.07, 0.38), obsidian(0x0a0b11), N);\n  const fs = Array.from({ length: N }, (_, i) => ({ a: (i / N) * Math.PI * 2 * 3.1, r: 4.2 + (i % 7) * 0.55, y: 1.5 + ((i * 37) % 100) / 100 * (MH + 2), s: 0.35 + ((i * 53) % 100) / 100 * 0.8 }));\n  scene.add(frags);\n  const d = new THREE.Object3D();\n\n  // ── atmosphere (planes 4+5): haze bands, crepuscular shafts leaking around the monolith, 3 dust fields\n  const haze = createHaze({ color: 0xff6a30, layers: 5, width: 300, height: 26, zStart: 10, zStep: -30, y: 6, opacity: 0.28 });\n  scene.add(haze.group);\n  const shafts = createLightShafts({ from: [MX, BASE + MH + 2, MZ - 40], to: [MX + 2, BASE + 3, MZ + 70], color: 0xff9a5a, spread: 11, count: 3, intensity: 0 });\n  scene.add(shafts.group);\n  const dustA = createParticles({ count: mobile ? 500 : 1500, extent: [34, 7, 60], size: 1.5, color: 0xffb27a, color2: 0xffe2c4, opacity: 0.4 });\n  dustA.points.position.set(vx(0), gy(0) + 6, 0);\n  const dustB = createParticles({ count: mobile ? 500 : 1500, extent: [34, 8, 50], size: 1.5, color: 0xffa36a, color2: 0xffd9b0, opacity: 0.4 });\n  dustB.points.position.set(MX, BASE + 7, MZ + 10);\n  const motes = createParticles({ count: mobile ? 40 : 110, extent: [9, 5, 12], size: 6, color: 0xff9a60, color2: 0xffd0a0, opacity: 0.3, speed: 1.4 });\n  scene.add(dustA.points, dustB.points, motes.points);\n\n  // ── THE RAIL. 7 shots, one hold, wide/close alternation, a composed last frame (centred monolith + corona crown)\n  const shots: Shot[] = [\n    { at: 0.0, pos: [vx(34) + 5, gy(34) + 1.5, 34], look: [MX, 9, MZ], fov: 40 }, //  1 establish — low, wide, the dark sun already half-risen\n    { at: 0.2, pos: [vx(-6) - 3.5, gy(-6) + 1.9, -6], look: [MX, 7, MZ], fov: 34 }, //  2 the walk — dunes slide past on both sides\n    { at: 0.4, pos: [MX + 8, BASE + 0.5, MZ + 21], look: [MX, BASE + 9.5, MZ], fov: 25, ease: \"inOut\" }, //  3 hero — telephoto, worm's-eye\n    { at: 0.56, pos: [MX + 7.4, BASE + 0.7, MZ + 20], look: [MX, BASE + 9.5, MZ], fov: 24.5 }, //     …HOLD (copy is read here; the seam ignites)\n    { at: 0.76, pos: [MX + 17, BASE + 2.6, MZ - 3], look: [MX - 2, BASE + 8, MZ - 14], fov: 38, roll: 3 }, //  4 the pass — the corona slides behind the slab's edge\n    { at: 0.9, pos: [MX + 6, BASE + 30, MZ + 46], look: [MX, BASE + 3, MZ - 20], fov: 50, ease: \"inOut\" }, //  5 crane — see the whole valley, the monolith small\n    { at: 1.0, pos: [MX, BASE + 2.8, MZ + 36], look: [MX, BASE + 7.6, MZ], fov: 31 }, //  6 FINAL — centred, symmetrical, corona crowns the monolith\n  ];\n\n  return {\n    scene,\n    shots,\n    fov: 40,\n    exposure: 0.85,\n    parallax: 0.6,\n    bloom: { strength: mobile ? 0.55 : 0.85, radius: 0.8, threshold: 0.9 },\n    grade: { vignette: 0.8, grain: 0.055, contrast: 1.1, saturation: 1.1, shadows: [0.0, 0.018, 0.05], highlights: [0.09, 0.035, 0.0] },\n    update(f: Frame) {\n      const { p, time: t } = f;\n      // totality builds through the walk, peaks at the hold, never fully relaxes\n      const total = 0.5 + 0.5 * ease.inOutCubic(seg(p, 0.0, 0.42));\n      sky.uniforms.uEclipse.value = Math.min(1, total);\n      sky.uniforms.uHaze.value = 1 - total * 0.5;\n      sky.update(t);\n      (corona.material as THREE.SpriteMaterial).opacity = 0.04 + total * 0.1;\n      // the seam ignites in the hold, flares as the corona passes behind, calms for the final frame\n      const ign = ease.inOutCubic(seg(p, 0.32, 0.5)) * (1 - 0.35 * seg(p, 0.85, 1));\n      (seam.material as THREE.MeshStandardMaterial).emissiveIntensity = 0.4 + ign * 4.5 + Math.sin(t * 5) * 0.12 * ign;\n      rig.practical.intensity = ign * 26;\n      // shafts: invisible in the establishing shot, strongest when the monolith occludes the corona\n      shafts.setIntensity(0.25 + 1.5 * (ease.inOutSine(seg(p, 0.6, 0.78)) * (1 - seg(p, 0.9, 1)) + 0.35 * seg(p, 0.9, 1)));\n      shafts.update(t);\n      haze.update(t);\n      // the fragments orbit with SCROLL (scrub), plus a whisper of time\n      const orb = p * Math.PI * 2.4 + t * 0.03;\n      for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {\n        const s = fs[i], a = s.a + orb * (0.6 + (i % 5) * 0.12);\n        d.position.set(MX + Math.cos(a) * s.r, BASE + s.y + Math.sin(t * 0.4 + i) * 0.15, MZ + Math.sin(a) * s.r * 0.7);\n        d.rotation.set(a * 0.7, a, i);\n        d.scale.setScalar(s.s);\n        d.updateMatrix();\n        frags.setMatrixAt(i, d.matrix);\n      }\n      frags.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true;\n      dustA.update(t, f.velocity);\n      dustB.update(t, f.velocity);\n      motes.points.position.lerp(f.camera.position, 0.08); // lag behind the lens → a real foreground parallax plane\n      motes.update(t, f.velocity);\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nexport const UMBRA_QUALITY_NOTE = \"Mirror floor intentionally absent: sand is rough. Reflection budget goes to the monolith's clearcoat instead.\";\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/studio.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · studio.ts — product-render lighting.\n   The #1 reason web 3D looks cheap is the environment map. This builds a *designed* one: a dark room with\n   long softbox strips whose reflections draw the luxury highlight lines down curved surfaces.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\ntype Col = THREE.ColorRepresentation;\nexport interface StripSpec { color: Col; intensity: number; pos: [number, number, number]; size: [number, number] }\n\nconst DEFAULT_STRIPS: StripSpec[] = [\n  { color: 0xdbe8ff, intensity: 3.2, pos: [-9, 5, 3], size: [1.2, 12] },   // tall cool strip, camera-left → vertical highlight line\n  { color: 0xfff1e0, intensity: 2.2, pos: [9, 6, -2], size: [1.6, 10] },   // warm strip, right\n  { color: 0xffffff, intensity: 1.8, pos: [0, 11, 0], size: [12, 2.2] },   // top bar → horizon highlight on flat tops\n  { color: 0xffffff, intensity: 1.0, pos: [0, 2, -11], size: [14, 3] },  // back wall glow for rim\n];\n\n/** A PMREM environment from a procedural softbox room. Assign to scene.environment (and tune scene.environmentIntensity). */\nexport function createStudioEnv(renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer, o: { base?: Col; baseIntensity?: number; strips?: StripSpec[]; accent?: StripSpec } = {}) {\n  const room = new THREE.Scene();\n  const base = new THREE.Color(o.base ?? 0x05070b).multiplyScalar(o.baseIntensity ?? 1);\n  room.add(new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(30, 24, 30), new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: base, side: THREE.BackSide })));\n  const mats: THREE.Material[] = [];\n  [...(o.strips ?? DEFAULT_STRIPS), ...(o.accent ? [o.accent] : [])].forEach((s) => {\n    const m = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: new THREE.Color(s.color).multiplyScalar(s.intensity), side: THREE.DoubleSide });\n    mats.push(m);\n    const p = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(s.size[0], s.size[1]), m);\n    p.position.set(...s.pos);\n    p.lookAt(0, 0, 0);\n    room.add(p);\n  });\n  const pm = new THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer);\n  const tex = pm.fromScene(room, 0.02).texture;\n  pm.dispose();\n  room.traverse((c) => { if (c instanceof THREE.Mesh) c.geometry.dispose(); });\n  mats.forEach((m) => m.dispose());\n  return tex;\n}\n\n/** Key / rim / kicker / fill for a hero product. Position is relative to the product at the origin. */\nexport function productRig(o: { key?: Col; rim?: Col; kicker?: Col; accent?: Col } = {}) {\n  const group = new THREE.Group();\n  const key = new THREE.SpotLight(o.key ?? 0xe4ecf8, 70, 40, 0.3, 1, 1.6);\n  key.position.set(-9, 3.5, 7);\n  const rim = new THREE.DirectionalLight(o.rim ?? 0xcfe6ff, 5.5);\n  rim.position.set(7, 6, -9);\n  const kicker = new THREE.DirectionalLight(o.kicker ?? 0xffffff, 1.4);\n  kicker.position.set(2, 1.2, 9);\n  const fill = new THREE.HemisphereLight(0x1a2438, 0x05070b, 0.35);\n  const accent = new THREE.PointLight(o.accent ?? 0x7dffd9, 0, 7, 2);\n  group.add(key, key.target, rim, kicker, fill, accent);\n  return { group, key, rim, kicker, fill, accent };\n}\n\n/** Soft blurred disc under an object — without it, things float. */\nexport function createContactShadow(radius = 2.4, opacity = 0.75) {\n  const c = document.createElement(\"canvas\");\n  c.width = c.height = 128;\n  const g = c.getContext(\"2d\")!;\n  const rg = g.createRadialGradient(64, 64, 0, 64, 64, 64);\n  rg.addColorStop(0, `rgba(0,0,0,${opacity})`);\n  rg.addColorStop(0.55, `rgba(0,0,0,${opacity * 0.45})`);\n  rg.addColorStop(1, \"rgba(0,0,0,0)\");\n  g.fillStyle = rg;\n  g.fillRect(0, 0, 128, 128);\n  const t = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c);\n  const m = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(radius * 2, radius * 2), new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map: t, transparent: true, depthWrite: false, fog: false }));\n  m.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;\n  m.position.y = 0.012;\n  return m;\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/aura/terrain.ts":"import * as THREE from \"three\";\nimport { noiseTexture } from \"./materials\";\n\n/* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   AURA · terrain.ts — procedural ground you can fly over. Zero assets.\n\n   createDunes()  wind-sculpted ridged dunes (grazing light makes them read as sand), with a micro-ripple normal map,\n                  a CPU heightAt(x, z) so the camera rail / props can sit ON the surface, and an optional winding \"road\"\n                  trench so the camera has somewhere to go.\n   Swap the height function (`shape`) for mountains, snow fields, lava crust, ocean swell, moon regolith.\n   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */\n\nconst hash = (x: number, y: number, s: number) => { const v = Math.sin(x * 127.1 + y * 311.7 + s * 74.7) * 43758.5453; return v - Math.floor(v); };\nconst vnoise = (x: number, y: number, s: number) => {\n  const xi = Math.floor(x), yi = Math.floor(y), tx = x - xi, ty = y - yi;\n  const sx = tx * tx * (3 - 2 * tx), sy = ty * ty * (3 - 2 * ty);\n  const a = hash(xi, yi, s), b = hash(xi + 1, yi, s), c = hash(xi, yi + 1, s), d = hash(xi + 1, yi + 1, s);\n  return a + (b - a) * sx + (c - a) * sy + (a - b - c + d) * sx * sy;\n};\nconst ridged = (x: number, y: number, s: number) => {\n  let v = 0, amp = 0.55, f = 1;\n  for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) { v += (1 - Math.abs(vnoise(x * f, y * f, s + i) * 2 - 1)) * amp; amp *= 0.46; f *= 2.05; }\n  return v;\n};\n\nexport interface DunesOptions {\n  size?: number;\n  segments?: number;\n  height?: number;\n  /** horizontal scale of the dune field (bigger = broader dunes) */ scale?: number;\n  seed?: number;\n  color?: THREE.ColorRepresentation;\n  /** carve a gentle winding valley along z so the camera path stays low and cinematic */ valley?: { width: number; sway: number } | false;\n  /** tint of the grazing-light sheen (the rim of each dune crest) */ sheen?: THREE.ColorRepresentation;\n}\n\nexport function createDunes(o: DunesOptions = {}) {\n  const size = o.size ?? 420, seg = o.segments ?? 220, H = o.height ?? 9, sc = o.scale ?? 0.011, seed = o.seed ?? 3;\n  const valley = o.valley === false ? null : o.valley ?? { width: 14, sway: 12 };\n  const valleyX = (z: number) => (valley ? Math.sin(z * 0.035) * valley.sway : 0);\n\n  const heightAt = (x: number, z: number) => {\n    // domain-warp so crests meander instead of running in straight lines\n    const wx = x + (vnoise(x * sc * 2, z * sc * 2, seed + 9) - 0.5) * 60;\n    const wz = z + (vnoise(z * sc * 2, x * sc * 2, seed + 5) - 0.5) * 60;\n    let h = ridged(wx * sc, wz * sc * 1.6, seed) * H;\n    if (valley) {\n      const d = Math.abs(x - valleyX(z)) / valley.width;\n      h *= 0.12 + 0.88 * Math.min(1, d * d); // flatten toward the valley floor\n    }\n    return h - H * 0.35;\n  };\n\n  const geo = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(size, size, seg, seg);\n  geo.rotateX(-Math.PI / 2);\n  const p = geo.attributes.position as THREE.BufferAttribute;\n  for (let i = 0; i < p.count; i++) p.setY(i, heightAt(p.getX(i), p.getZ(i)));\n  geo.computeVertexNormals();\n\n  const ripple = noiseTexture(256, 3, seed);\n  ripple.repeat.set(40, 40);\n  const mat = new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({\n    color: o.color ?? 0x2a1a14,\n    roughness: 0.92,\n    metalness: 0,\n    sheen: 0.6,\n    sheenColor: new THREE.Color(o.sheen ?? 0xff8a4a),\n    sheenRoughness: 0.35,\n    bumpMap: ripple,\n    bumpScale: 0.55,\n    \n    envMapIntensity: 0.4,\n  });\n  const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geo, mat);\n  return { mesh, material: mat, heightAt, valleyX };\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/src/main.tsx":"import { StrictMode } from \"react\";\nimport { createRoot } from \"react-dom/client\";\nimport \"@fontsource-variable/space-grotesk\";\nimport \"@fontsource/jetbrains-mono/500.css\";\nimport ExampleExperience from \"./aura/ExampleExperience\";\nimport UmbraExperience from \"./aura/UmbraExperience\";\nimport HaloExperience from \"./aura/HaloExperience\";\nimport FolioExperience from \"./aura/FolioExperience\";\n\n// ?world=halo   → NOCTA, a PRODUCT altar (exploded ascent, studio env, asteroid)\n// ?world=umbra  → an EVENT / terrain world (eclipse sky, light shafts)\n// ?world=folio  → Mira, a FOLIO (portfolio / studio: projects on a helix, plates ignite on hold)\n// default       → Obsidian Gate, an OBJECT world (crystal, rings, mirror floor)\nconst w = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get(\"world\");\nconst World = w === \"folio\" ? FolioExperience : w === \"halo\" ? HaloExperience : w === \"umbra\" ? UmbraExperience : ExampleExperience;\n\n// Replace with your own <AuraExperience build={yourScene}>…chapters…</AuraExperience>\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\"root\")!).render(\n  <StrictMode>\n    <World />\n  </StrictMode>,\n);\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/tsconfig.json":"{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"lib\": [\"ES2022\", \"DOM\", \"DOM.Iterable\"],\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"strict\": true,\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"noEmit\": true,\n    \"isolatedModules\": true\n  },\n  \"include\": [\"src\"]\n}\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/starter/vite.config.mjs":"import react from \"@vitejs/plugin-react\";\nimport { defineConfig } from \"vite\";\n\nexport default defineConfig({ plugins: [react()], build: { target: \"es2022\", chunkSizeWarningLimit: 900 } });\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/templates/AURA_BRIEF.template.md":"# AURA BRIEF — [Project]\n\n**One sentence:** A scroll-driven film that takes [audience] through [world] so they feel [emotion], then [conversion action].\n\n**World:** [place · time of day · weather · material palette]\n**Hero:** [the one object / image / building / word that owns the poster frame]\n**Emotional arc:** Act I [..] → Act II [..] → Act III [..]\n**Palette:** bg #______ · fog #______ (== bg) · key #______ · rim #______ · accent #______ · text #______\n**Type:** Display [font] (giant) · Mono [font] (HUD/labels)\n**Lens language:** [e.g. 24° tele on details, 60° on speed, one Dutch roll at the twist]\n**Light:** key [dir / colour] · rim [colour] · practical [what / where] · time-of-day arc [..]\n**Atmosphere:** [fog density · particle bands · floor · sky]\n**Sound (opt-in):** [mood · 3 stems]\n**Never:** [3–5 things this world must not contain]\n**Conversion:** [single CTA · where · what it does]\n\n## Authorship test\nPaste the one-sentence concept onto three competitors. Does it still work? → rewrite with a more specific noun, place, time and material.\n","/skill-src/aura-3d-web/templates/STORYBOARD.template.md":"# STORYBOARD — [Project]\n\nTotal scroll: [N]vh · Beats: [N] · Holds: [N] · Chapters: [5–9]\n\n| # | Scroll (0–1) | Shot (move · lens) | World change | Copy (≤12 words) | Type treatment | Transition out |\n|---|--------------|--------------------|--------------|------------------|----------------|----------------|\n| 0 | loader | — | black → title wipe | [project name] | counter 000→100 | wipe up |\n| 1 | 0.00–0.12 | Establish · wide 38° · slow drift | [world at rest] | \"[line]\" | GIANT [WORD] | dolly forward |\n| 2 | 0.12–0.30 | Push-in · 34° · parallax | [first reveal] | — | word slips behind subject | pass behind foreground |\n| 3 | 0.30–0.48 | Macro orbit · 24° | [detail / material / light ignites] | \"[line]\" | mono labels | — |\n| 4 | 0.48–0.58 | **HOLD** · 24° | [hero moment, no copy or 1 line] | \"[line]\" | — | — |\n| 5 | 0.58–0.78 | Crane up · 52° | [scale reveal] | \"[line]\" | headline | fog pass |\n| 6 | 0.78–0.92 | Fly-through · 60°+ · FOV kick | [crescendo: particles surge] | — | giant, tracking scrub | light flash |\n| 7 | 0.92–1.00 | Locked-off · 45° · slow settle | [final tableau, practical glow] | \"[CTA line]\" | CTA (magnetic) | — (end card) |\n\n## Shot list (copy into code)\n```ts\nconst shots: Shot[] = [\n  { at: 0.00, pos: [ , , ], look: [ , , ], fov: 38 },\n  // …\n];\n```\n\n## Checklist\n- [ ] every scroll range covered · [ ] ≥1 hold · [ ] ≥4 different lenses · [ ] ≥1 chapter without copy\n- [ ] wide/close alternate · [ ] last frame is a poster · [ ] backwards scroll also coherent\n"};
export default RAW;
